(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("dhammasangani",[{"sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK I. THE GENESIS OF THOUGHTS"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "PART I. GOOD STATES OF CONSCIOUSNESS"}, {"st": "1.1.1", "t": "Type I."}, {"st": "1.1.2", "t": "(1) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.3", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, which is accompanied by happiness and associated with knowledge, and has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.1.4", "t": "(i) contact (§ 2),<br>(ii) feeling (§ 3),<br>(iii) perception (§4),<br>(iv) thinking (§ 5),<br>(V) thought (§ 6),<br>(vi) conception (§ 7),<br>(vii) discursive thought (§8),<br>(viii) joy (§ 9),<br>(ix) ease (§ 10),<br>(x) self-collectedness (§11),<br>(xi) the faculty of faith (§ 12),<br>(xii) the faculty of energy (§ 13),<br>(xiii) the faculty of mindfulness (§ 14),<br>(xiv) the faculty of concentration (§ 15),<br>(xv) the faculty of wisdom (§ 16),<br>(xvi) the faculty of ideation (§ 17),<br>(xvii) the faculty of happiness (§ 18),<br>(xviii) the faculty of vitality (§ 19);<br>(xix) right views (§ 20),<br>(xx) right intention (§21),<br>(xxi) right endeavour (§ 22),<br>(xxii) right mindfulness (§ 23),<br>(xxiii) right concentration (§ 24);<br>(xxiv) the power of faith (§ 25),<br>(xxv) the power of energy (§ 26),<br>(xxvi) the power of mindfulness (§ 27),<br>(xxvii) the power of concentration (§ 28),<br>(xxviii) the power of wisdom (§ 29),<br>(xxix) the power of conscientiousness (§ 30),<br>(xxx) the power of the fear of blame (§ 31);<br>(xxxi) absence of lust (§ 32),<br>(xxxii) absence of hate (§ 33),<br>(xxxiii) absence of dulness (§ 34);<br>(xxxiv) absence of covetousness (§ 35),<br>(xxxv) absence of malice (§ 36),<br>(xxxvi) right views (§ 37);<br>(xxxvii) conscientiousness (§ 38),<br>(xxxviii) fear of blame (§ 39);<br>(xxxix, xl) serenity in sense and thought (§§ 40, 41),<br>(xli, xlii) lightness in sense and thought (§§ 42, 43),<br>(xliii, xliv) plasticity in sense and thought (§§ 44, 45),<br>(xlv, xlvi) facility in sense and thought (§§ 46, 47),<br>(xlvii, xlviii) fitness in sense and thought (§§ 48, 49),<br>(xlix, l) directness in sense and thought (§§ 50, 51);<br>(li) mindfulness (§ 52),<br>(lii) intelligence (§53)<br>(liii) quiet (§ 54)<br>(liv) insight (§ 55),<br>(lv) grasp (§ 56),<br>(lvi) balance (§ 57)."}, {"st": "1.1.5", "t": "Now these — or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.6", "t": "(2) What on that occasion is contact (phasso)?"}, {"st": "1.1.7", "t": "The contact which on that occasion is touching, the being brought into contact, the state of having been brought into touch with — this is the contact that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.8", "t": "(3) What on that occasion is feeling (vedana)?"}, {"st": "1.1.9", "t": "The mental pleasure, the mental ease, which, on that occasion, is born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection; the pleasurable, easeful sensation which is born of contact with thought; the pleasurable, easeful feeling which is born of contact with thought — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.10", "t": "(4) What on that occasion is perception (sanna)?"}, {"st": "1.1.11", "t": "The perception, the perceiving, the state of having perceived which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection — this is the perception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.12", "t": "(5) What on that occasion is thinking (cetana)?"}, {"st": "1.1.13", "t": "The thinking, the cogitating, the reflection, which is born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection — this is the thinking that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.14", "t": "(6) What on that occasion is thought (cittam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.15", "t": "The thought which on that occasion is ideation, mind, heart, that which is clear, ideation as the sphere of mind, the faculty of mind, intellection, the skandha of intellection, the appropriate element of representative intellection — this is the thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.16", "t": "(7) What on that occasion is conception (vitakko)?"}, {"st": "1.1.17", "t": "The ratiocination, the conception, which on that occasion is the disposition, the fixation, the focussing, the application of the mind, right intention — this is the conception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.18", "t": "(8) What on that occasion is discursive thought (vicaro)?"}, {"st": "1.1.19", "t": "The process, the sustained procedure (vicaro), the progress and access [of the mind] which on that occasion is the [continuous] adjusting and focussing of thought — this is the discursive thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.20", "t": "(9) What on that occasion is joy (piti)?"}, {"st": "1.1.21", "t": "The joy which on that occasion is gladness, rejoicing at, rejoicing over, mirth and merriment, felicity, exultation, transport of mind — this is the joy that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.22", "t": "(10) What on that occasion is ease (sukham)?"}, {"st": "1.1.23", "t": "The mental pleasure, the mental ease which on that occasion is the pleasant, easeful experience born of contact with thought, the pleasant, easeful feeling born of contact with thought — this is the ease that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.24", "t": "(11) What on that occasion is self-collectedness (cittass’ ekaggata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.25", "t": "The stability, solidity, absorbed steadfastness of thought which on that occasion is the absence of distraction, balance, imperturbed mental procedure, quiet, the faculty and the power of concentration, right concentration — this is the self-collectedness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.26", "t": "(12) What on that occasion is the faculty of faith (saddhindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.27", "t": "The faith which on that occasion is a trusting in, the professing confidence in, the sense of assurance, faith, faith as a faculty and as a power — this is the faith that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.28", "t": "(13) What on that occasion is the faculty of energy (viriyindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.29", "t": "The mental inception of energy which there is on that occasion, the striving and the onward effort, the exertion and endeavour, the zeal and ardour, the vigour and fortitude, the state of unfaltering effort, the state of sustained desire, the state of unflinching endurance, the solid grip of the burden, energy, energy as faculty and as power, right endeavour — this is the energy that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.30", "t": "(14) What on that occasion is the faculty of mindfulness (satindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.31", "t": "The mindfulness which on that occasion is recollecting, calling back to mind; the mindfulness which is remembering, bearing in mind, the opposite of superficiality and of obliviousness; mindfulness as faculty, mindfulness as power, right mindfulness — this is the faculty of mindfulness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.32", "t": "(15) What on that occasion is the faculty of concentration (samadhindriyam)?<br><i>Answer as for ‘self-collectedness,’ § 11.<\/i><br>(16) What on that occasion is the faculty of wisdom (pannindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.33", "t": "The wisdom which there is on that occasion is understanding, search, research, searching the Truth, discernment, discrimination, differentiation, erudition, proficiency, subtlety, criticism, reflection, analysis, breadth, sagacity, leading, insight, intelligence, incitement; wisdom as faculty, wisdom as power, wisdom as a sword, wisdom as a height, wisdom as light, wisdom as glory, wisdom as splendour, wisdom as a precious stone; the absence of dulness, searching the Truth, right views — this is the wisdom that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.34", "t": "(17) What on that occasion is the faculty of ideation (representative imagination, manindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.35", "t": "<i>Answer as for “thought” (cittam), § 6.<\/i><br>(18) What on that occasion is the faculty of pleasure (somanassindriyam)?<br><i>Answer as for “ease” (sukham), § 10.<\/i><br>(19) What on that occasion is the faculty of vitality (jivitindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.36", "t": "The persistence of these incorporeal states, their subsistence, going on, their being kept going on, their progress, continuance, preservation, life, life as faculty — this is the faculty of vitality that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.37", "t": "(20) What on that occasion are right views (sammadithi)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of wisdom”, § 16.<\/i><br>(21) What on that occasion is right intention (sammasankappo)?<br><i>Answer as for “conception”, § 7.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.38", "t": "(22) What on that occasion is right endeavour (sammavayamo)?<br><i>Ansiver as for the “faculty of energy”, § 13.<\/i><br>(23) What on that occasion is right - mindfulness (sammasati)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of mindfulness”, § 14.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.39", "t": "(24) What on that occasion is right concentration (sammasamadhi)?<br><i>Answer as for “self-collectedness”, § 11.<\/i><br>(25) What on that occasion is the power of faith (saddhabalam)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of faith”, § 12.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.40", "t": "(26) What on that occasion is the power of energy (viriyabalam)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of energy”, § 13.<\/i><br>(27) What on that occasion is the power of mindfulness (satibalam)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of mindfulness”, § 14.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.41", "t": "(28) What on that occasion is the power of concentration (samadhibalam)?<br><i>Answer as for “self-collectedness”, § 11.<\/i><br>(29) What on that occasion is the power of wisdom (pannabalam)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of wisdom”, § 16.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.42", "t": "(30) What on that occasion is the power of conscientiousness (hiribalam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.43", "t": "The feeling of conscientious scruple which there is on that occasion when scruples ought to be felt, conscientious scruple at attaining to bad and evil states — this is the power of conscientiousness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.44", "t": "(31) What on that occasion is the power of the fear of blame (ottappabalam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.45", "t": "The sense of guilt, which there is on that occasion, where a sense of guilt ought to be felt, a sense of guilt at attaining to bad and evil states — this is the fear of blame that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.46", "t": "(32) What on that occasion is the absence of lust (alobho)?"}, {"st": "1.1.47", "t": "The absence of lust, of lusting, of lustfulness, which there is on that occasion, the absence of infatuation, the feeling and being infatuated, the absence of covetousness, that absence of lust which is the root of good — this is the absence of lust that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.48", "t": "(33) What on that occasion is the absence of hate (adoso)?"}, {"st": "1.1.49", "t": "The absence of hate, of hating, of hatred, which there is on that occasion, the absence of malice, of spleen, the absence of hate which is the root of good — this is the absence of hate that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.50", "t": "(34) What on that occasion is the absence of dulness (amoho)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of wisdom”, § 16.<\/i><br>(35) What on that occasion is the absence of covetousness (anabhijjha?<br><i>Answer as for the “absence of lust”, § 32.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.51", "t": "(36) What on that occasion is the absence of malice (avyapado)?<br><i>Answer as for the “absence of hate”, § 33.<\/i><br>(37) What on that occasion are right views (sammaditthi)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of wisdom”, § 16.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.52", "t": "(38) What on that occasion is conscientiousness (hiri)?<br><i>Answer as for the “power of conscientiousness”, § 30.<\/i><br>(39) What on that occasion is the fear of blame (ottappam)?<br><i>Answer as for the “power of the fear of blame”, § 31.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.53", "t": "(40) What on that occasion is repose of sense (kayapassaddhi)?"}, {"st": "1.1.54", "t": "The serenity, the composure which there is on that occasion, the calming, the tranquillizing, the tranquillity of the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the serenity of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.55", "t": "(41) What on that occasion is serenity of thought (cittapassaddhi)?"}, {"st": "1.1.56", "t": "The serenity, the composure which there is on that occasion, the calming, the tranquillizing, the tranquillity of the skandha of intellect — this is the serenity of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.57", "t": "(42) What on that occasion is buoyancy of sense (kayalahuta)?"}, {"st": "1.1.58", "t": "The buoyancy which there is on that occasion, the alertness in varying, the absence of sluggishness and inertia, in the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the buoyancy of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.59", "t": "(43) What on that occasion is buoyancy of thought (cittalahuta)?"}, {"st": "1.1.60", "t": "The buoyancy, etc. (as in § 42), in the skandha of intellect — this is the buoyancy of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.61", "t": "(44) What on that occasion is plasticity of sense (kayamuduta)?"}, {"st": "1.1.62", "t": "The plasticity which there is on that occasion, the suavity, smoothness, absence of rigidity, in the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the plasticity of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.63", "t": "(45) What on that occasion is plasticity of thought (cittamuduta)?"}, {"st": "1.1.64", "t": "The plasticity which, etc. (as in § 44), in the skandha of intellect — this is the plasticity of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.65", "t": "(46) What on that occasion is wieldiness of sense (kayakammannata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.66", "t": "The wieldiness which there is on that occasion, the tractableness, the pliancy, of the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the wieldiness of sense that then is."}, {"st": "1.1.67", "t": "(47) What on that occasion is wieldiness of thought (cittakammannata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.68", "t": "The wieldiness, etc. (as in § 46), of the skandha of intellect — this is the wieldiness of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.69", "t": "(48) What on that occasion is fitness of sense (kayapagunnata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.70", "t": "The fitness which there is on that occasion, the competence, the efficient state of the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the fitness of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.71", "t": "(49) What on that occasion is fitness of thought (cittapagunnata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.72", "t": "The fitness, etc. (as in § 48), of the skandha of intellect — this is the fitness of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.73", "t": "(50) What on that occasion is rectitude of sense (kayujjukata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.74", "t": "The straightness which there is on that occasion, the rectitude, without deflection, twist or crookedness, of the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses — this is the directness of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.75", "t": "(51) What on that occasion is rectitude of thought (cittujjukata)?"}, {"st": "1.1.76", "t": "The straightness, etc. (as in § 50), of the skandha of intellect — this is the rectitude of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.77", "t": "(52) What on that occasion is mindfulness (sati)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of mindfulness”, § 14.<\/i><br>(53) What on that occasion is intelligence (sampajannam)?<br><i>Answer as for “wisdom”, § 16.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.78", "t": "(54) What on that occasion is quiet (samatho)?<br><i>Answer as for “self-collectedness”, § 11.<\/i><br>(55) What on that occasion is insight (vipassana)?<br>Ansiver as for “wisdom”, § 16."}, {"st": "1.1.79", "t": "(56) What on that occasion is grasp (paggaho)?<br><i>Answer as for the “faculty of energy”, § 13.<\/i><br>(57) What on that occasion is balance (avikkhepo)?<br><i>Answer as for “self-collectedness”, § 11.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.80", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.81", "t": "[Here ends the delimitation of terms (Pada-bhajaniyam).]"}, {"st": "1.1.82", "t": "[End of the First Portion for Eecitation.]"}, {"st": "1.1.83", "t": "[Summary of the constituents of the First Type of Thought (sangahavaram or kotthasavaram).]"}, {"st": "1.1.84", "t": "(58)"}, {"st": "1.1.85", "t": "Now, on that occasion<br>the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres (ayatanani) are two,<br>the elements (dhatuyo) are two,<br>the nutriments (ahara) are three,<br>the faculties (indriyani) are eight,<br>the Jhana is fivefold,<br>the Path is fivefold,<br>the powers (balani) are seven,<br>the causes (hetu) are three;"}, {"st": "1.1.86", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,"}, {"st": "1.1.87", "t": "<i>are each single [factors];<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.88", "t": "the skandhas of:"}, {"st": "1.1.89", "t": "feeling,<br>perception,<br>syntheses,<br>intellect,"}, {"st": "1.1.90", "t": "<i>are each single [factors];<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.91", "t": "the sphere of ideation (manayatanam),<br>the faculty of ideation,<br>the element of representative intellection (manovinnanadhatu),<br>the sphere of a (representative) state,<br>the element of a (representative) state,"}, {"st": "1.1.92", "t": "<i>are each single [factors].<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.93", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.94", "t": "(59) What on that occasion are the four skandhas?<br>The skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses and intellection.<br>(60) (i.) What on that occasion is the skandha of feeling?"}, {"st": "1.1.95", "t": "The mental pleasure, the mental ease, which there is on that occasion, the pleasurable, easeful sensation which is born of contact with thought, the pleasant, easeful feeling born of contact with thought — this is the skandha of feeling that there then is (§§ 3, 10, 18)."}, {"st": "1.1.96", "t": "(61) (ii.) What on that occasion is the skandha of perception?"}, {"st": "1.1.97", "t": "The perception, the perceiving, the state of having perceived, which there is on that occasion — this is the skandha of perception that there then is (§ 4)."}, {"st": "1.1.98", "t": "(62) (iii.) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br>i. Contact,<br>ii. thinking,<br>iii. conception,<br>iv. discursive thought,<br>v. joy,<br>vi. self-collectedness,<br>vii. the faculty of faith,<br>viii. the faculty of energy,<br>ix. the faculty of mindfulness,<br>x. the faculty of concentration,<br>xi. the faculty of wisdom,"}, {"st": "1.1.99", "t": "xii. the faculty of vitality,<br>xiii. right views,<br>xiv. right intention,<br>xv. right endeavour,<br>xvi. right mindfulness,<br>xvii. right concentration,<br>xviii. the power of faith,<br>xix. the power of energy,<br>xx. the power of mindfulness,<br>xxi. the power of concentration,<br>xxii. the power of wisdom,<br>xxiii. the power of conscientiousness,"}, {"st": "1.1.100", "t": "xxiv. the power of the fear of blame,<br>xxv. absence of lust,<br>xxvi. absence of hate,<br>xxvii. absence of dulness."}, {"st": "1.1.101", "t": "xxviii. absence of covetousness,<br>xxix. absence of malice,<br>xxx. right views,<br>xxxi. conscientiousness,<br>xxxii. the fear of blame,<br>xxxiii. serenity of sense,<br>xxxiv. serenity of thought,<br>xxxv. buoyancy of sense,<br>xxxvi. buoyancy of thought,<br>xxxvii. plasticity of sense,<br>xxxviii. plasticity of thought,<br>xxxix. wieldiness of sense,"}, {"st": "1.1.102", "t": "xl. wieldiness of thought,<br>xli. fitness of sense,<br>xlii. fitness of thought,<br>xliii. rectitude of sense."}, {"st": "1.1.103", "t": "xliv. rectitude of thought,<br>xlv. mindfulness,<br>xlvi. intelligence,<br>xlvii. quiet,<br>xlviii. insight,<br>xlix. grasp,<br>l. balance."}, {"st": "1.1.104", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellection — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.1.105", "t": "(63) (iv.) What on that occasion is the skandha of intellect?"}, {"st": "1.1.106", "t": "The thought which on that occasion is ideation, mind, the heart, that which is clear, ideation as the sphere of mind, as the faculty of mind, the skandha of intellect, the appropriate element of representative intellection — this is the skandha of intellect that there then is (§ 6). These on that occasion are the four skandhas."}, {"st": "1.1.107", "t": "(64) What on that occasion are the two spheres?<br>The sphere of ideation, the sphere of (mental) states.<br>(65) What on that occasion is the »sphere of ideation (manayatanam)?<br><i>Answer as for ‘thought,’ § 6, and for the ‘skandha of intellection,’ § 63.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.108", "t": "(66) What on that occasion is the sphere of (mental) states (dhammayatanam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.109", "t": "The skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses — this is on that occasion the sphere of (mental) states."}, {"st": "1.1.110", "t": "These are on that occasion the two spheres.<br>(67) What on that occasion are the two elements?<br>The element of representative intellection, the element of (mental) states."}, {"st": "1.1.111", "t": "(68) What on that occasion is the element of representative intellection (manovinnanadhatu)?"}, {"st": "1.1.112", "t": "<i>Answer as for “thought”, § 6; cf. §§ 63, 65.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.113", "t": "(69) What on that occasion is the element of (mental) states (dhammadhatu)?"}, {"st": "1.1.114", "t": "The skandhas of feeling, of perception, of syntheses — these are on that occasion the element of (mental) states."}, {"st": "1.1.115", "t": "These are on that occasion the two elements."}, {"st": "1.1.116", "t": "(70) What on that occasion are the three nutriments?"}, {"st": "1.1.117", "t": "The nutriment of contact, the nutriment of representative cogitation, the nutriment of intellection."}, {"st": "1.1.118", "t": "(71) What on that occasion is the nutriment of contact (phassaharo)?"}, {"st": "1.1.119", "t": "<i>Answer as for “contact”, § 2.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.120", "t": "(72) What on that occasion is the nutriment of representative cogitation (manosancetanaharo)?"}, {"st": "1.1.121", "t": "The thinking, the cogitating, the reflection which there is on that occasion — this is the representative cogitation that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.122", "t": "(73) What on that occasion is the nutriment of intellection (vinnanaharo)?<br><i>Answer as for the “skandha of intellection”, § 63.<\/i><br>These on that occasion are the three nutriments.<br>(74) What on that occasion are the eight faculties?"}, {"st": "1.1.123", "t": "The faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom, ideation, happiness, vitality."}, {"st": "1.1.124", "t": "[75-82] What on that occasion is the faculty of faith... vitality?<br><i>Answers as in §§ 12-19 respectively.<\/i><br>These on that occasion are the eight faculties.<br>(83) What on that occasion is the fivefold Jhana (pancangikam jhanam)?"}, {"st": "1.1.125", "t": "Conception, discursive thought, joy, ease, self-collectedness.<br>[84-88] What on that occasion is conception... selfcollectedness?<br><i>Answers as in §§ 7-11 respectively.<\/i><br>This on that occasion is the fivefold Jhana."}, {"st": "1.1.126", "t": "(89) What on that occasion is the fivefold Path (pancangiko maggo)?"}, {"st": "1.1.127", "t": "Eight views, right intention, right endeavour, right mindfulness, right concentration."}, {"st": "1.1.128", "t": "[90-94] What on that occasion are right views... is... right concentration?<br><i>Answers<\/i> as in §§ 20-24 respectively.<br>This on that occasion is the fivefold Path.<br>(95) What on that occasion are the seven powers?"}, {"st": "1.1.129", "t": "The power of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom, conscientiousness, the fear of blame."}, {"st": "1.1.130", "t": "[96-102] What on that occasion is the power of faith... the fear of blame?<br><i>Answers as in §§ 25-31 respectively.<\/i><br>These on that occasion are the seven powers.<br>(103) What on that occasion are the three causes (tayohetu)?"}, {"st": "1.1.131", "t": "The absence of lust, of hate, and of dulness.<br>[104-106] What on that occasion is the absence of lust... dulness?<br><i>Answers as in §§ 32-34 respectively.<\/i><br>These are on that occasion the three causes."}, {"st": "1.1.132", "t": "(107) What on that occasion is contact...<br>(108) feeling...<br>(109) perception...<br>(110) thinking...<br>(111) thought...<br>(112) the skandha of feeling...<br>(113) the skandha of perception...<br>(114) the skandha of syntheses...<br>(115) the skandha of intellection...<br>(116) the sphere of ideation...<br>(117) the faculty of ideation...<br>(118) the element of ideational intellection...<br>(119) the sphere of (mental) states...<br>(120) the element of (mental) states, regarded as a single factor?"}, {"st": "1.1.133", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 2-6, 60-63, 65, 65, 65, 66, 66, respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.134", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.135", "t": "[Here ends] the Summary [of the constituents of the First Main Type of Good Thoughts]."}, {"st": "1.1.136", "t": "[The “Emptiness” Section (sunnatavaro)]."}, {"st": "1.1.137", "t": "(121)"}, {"st": "1.1.138", "t": "Now, at that time there are states (distinguishable constituents of the “thought”),"}, {"st": "1.1.139", "t": "skandhas, powers,<br>spheres, causes,<br>elements, contact,<br>nutriments, feeling,<br>faculties, perception,<br>Jhana, thinking,<br>the Path, thought,"}, {"st": "1.1.140", "t": "the skandha of feeling,<br>the skandha of perception,<br>the skandha of syntheses,<br>the skandha of intellect,<br>the sphere of ideation,<br>the faculty of ideation,<br>the element of representative intellection,<br>the sphere of [mental] states,<br>the element of [mental] states."}, {"st": "1.1.141", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.142", "t": "(122) What on that occasion are states?<br>The skandhas of feeling, of perception, of syntheses, of intellection.<br>(123) What on that occasion are skandhas?<br>Ansiver as in § 59."}, {"st": "1.1.143", "t": "[124-145] <i>Similar questions are then put respecting “spheres”, “elements”, and so on through the list of constituent species. The Answers are identical with those given to similar questions in the previous ‘ Summary,’ viz. in §§ 64, 67, 70, 74, 83, 89, 95, 103, and 107-120.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.144", "t": "[Here ends] the “Emptiness” Section.<br>[Here ends] the First Main Type of Good Thoughts.<br>Type II.<br>(146) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.145", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen by the prompting of a conscious motive, a thought which is accompanied by pleasure, associated with knowledge, and having, as its object, a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, feeling, etc. [here follows the list of ‘ states ‘ dealt with in §§ 1-145 and constituting the First Thought] — these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good...."}, {"st": "1.1.146", "t": "[Here ends] the Second Thought.<br>Type III.<br>(147) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.147", "t": "“When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen accompanied by pleasure, disconnected with knowledge, and having as its object, a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.1.148", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.1.149", "t": "<i>[Summary, cf. § 58 et seq.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.150", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.1.151", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are seven,<br>the Jhana is fivefold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>the powers are six,<br>the causes are two.<br>contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.1.152", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.] <\/i><br>* * * *<br>(148) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.1.153", "t": "The content of the sanskara-skandha is the same as in the First Type of Thought, § 62, with the following omissions:"}, {"st": "1.1.154", "t": "“The faculty of wisdom”,<br>“right views”,<br>“the power of wisdom”,<br>“the absence of dulness”,<br>“intelligence”,<br>“insight”."}, {"st": "1.1.155", "t": "These are omitted as incompatible with the quality “disconnected with knowledge”."}, {"st": "1.1.156", "t": "* * * *<br>These, or whatever other incorporeal, etc.<br>* * * *<br>[Here ends] the Third Type of Thought."}, {"st": "1.1.157", "t": "Type IV.<br>(149) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.158", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen by the prompting of a conscious motive, a thought which is accompanied by happiness, disconnected with knowledge, and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, etc. [continue as in § 147] — these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good...."}, {"st": "1.1.159", "t": "* * * *<br>[Here ends] the Fourth Thought.<br>Type V.<br>(150) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.160", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with knowledge, and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, etc. [continue as in § 1, but for “joy” and “happiness” substitute “equanimity” (upekkha), and for “the faculty of happiness” substitute”the faculty of disinterestedness”]."}, {"st": "1.1.161", "t": "(151) What on that occasion is contact?<br><i>Answer as m § 2.<\/i><br>(152) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.1.162", "t": "The mental [condition] neither pleasant nor unpleasant, which, on that occasion, is born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection; the sensation, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful; the feeling, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.1.163", "t": "* * * *<br><i>[Continue as in §§ 4-8.] <\/i><br>(153) What on that occasion is disinterestedness?"}, {"st": "1.1.164", "t": "<i>Answer as in preceding reply, omitting the phrase “born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.165", "t": "* * * *<br><i>[Continue as in §§ 11-17.]<\/i><br>(154) What on that occasion is the faculty of disinterestedness?<br><i>Answer as in preceding reply. Continue as in §§ 19-57.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.166", "t": "<i>[Summary.] <\/i><br>Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.1.167", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are eight,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is fivefold,<br>the powers are seven,<br>the causes are three,<br>contact,<br>etc. etc. [cf. § 58],"}, {"st": "1.1.168", "t": "the sphere of mental states is a single factor,<br>the element of mental states is a single factor."}, {"st": "1.1.169", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good...."}, {"st": "1.1.170", "t": "<i>[Continue as in §§ 59-61.]<\/i><br>(155) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br><i>Answer as in § 62, omitting “joy”.<\/i><br>* * * *"}, {"st": "1.1.171", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the Summay-y and “Emptiness” Section of the First Type of Thought.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.172", "t": "[Here ends] the Fifth Type of Thought.]<br>Type VI.<br>(156) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.173", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with knowledge, prompted by a conscious motive, and having, as its object, a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.1.174", "t": "* * * *<br><i>[Continue as in the Fifth Type of Thought.]<\/i><br>* * * *<br>[Here ends] the Sixth Type of Thought."}, {"st": "1.1.175", "t": "Type VII.<br>(157) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.176", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, disconnected with knowledge, and having, as its object, a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, etc...."}, {"st": "1.1.177", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the Third Type of Thought, substituting “disinterestedness” for “joy” and “ease”, the “faculty of disinterestedness” for that of “happiness”, and “fourfold” for “fivefold Jhana”.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.178", "t": "<i>[Summary.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.179", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.1.180", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.1.181", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the Third Type of Thought, substituting “fourfold” for “fivefold Jhana”.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.182", "t": "(158) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.1.183", "t": "The content of this skandha is the same as in the Third Type of Thought (see § 148), with the further omission of “joy”."}, {"st": "1.1.184", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the First Type of Thought.]<\/i><br>[Here ends] the Seventh Type of Thought.<br>Type VIII.<br>(159) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.1.185", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, disconnected with knowledge, prompted by a conscious motive, and having, as its object, a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.1.186", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the Seventh Type of Thought.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.1.187", "t": "[Here ends] the Eighth Type of Thought."}, {"st": "1.1.188", "t": "[End of Chapter I. on] the Eight Main Types of Thought concerning the Sensuous Universe."}, {"st": "1.1.189", "t": "(Here ends the Second Portion for Recitation.)"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER II. GOOD IN RELATION TO THE UNIVERSE OF FORM"}, {"st": "1.2.1", "t": "Methods for inducing Jhana,<br>Method I: The Eight Artifices (atthakasinam)."}, {"st": "1.2.2", "t": "The Earth Artifice (pathavikasinam).<br>(a) The Fourfold System of Jhdna (catukkanayo).<br>(160) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.3", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the First Jhana (the first rapt meditation), wherein conception works and thought discursive, which is born of solitude, and full of joy and ease — then the contact, the feeling... the grasp, the balance, which arise in him, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states that there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.4", "t": "<i>Continue as in the First Type of Thought relating to the sensuous universe, including the Summary and “Emptiness” divisions.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.5", "t": "(161) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.6", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, suppressing the working of conception and of thought discursive, and so, by earthgazing, enters into and abides in the Second Jhana (the second rapt meditation), which is self-evolved, born of concentration, full of joy and ease, in that, set free from the working of conception and of thought discursive, the mind grows calm and sure dwelling on high — then the contact, the feeling, the perception, the thinking, the thought, the joy, the ease, the self-collectedness, the faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom, ideation, happiness, and vitality, the right views, right endeavour... the grasp, the balance that arises — these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states that there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.7", "t": "<i>[Summary.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.8", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.2.9", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are eight,<br>the Jhana is threefold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>the powers are seven,<br>the causes are three,<br>contact counts as a single factor,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.2.10", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58 et seq.]<\/i><br>(162) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.2.11", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 62 et seq.]<\/i><br>(163) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.12", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and further, through the waning of all passion for joy, holds himself unbiassed, the while, mindful and self-possessed, he experiences in his sense-consciousness that ease whereof the Noble Ones declare:"}, {"st": "1.2.13", "t": "“He that is unbiassed and watchful dwelleth at ease”"}, {"st": "1.2.14", "t": "— and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the Third Jhana — then the contact, the feeling, the perception, the thinking, the thought, the ease, the self-collectedness, the faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom, ideation, happiness and vitality, the right views, right endeavour, etc.... the grasp, the balance that arises — these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states that there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.15", "t": "<i>[Summary.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.16", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.2.17", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are eight,<br>the Jhana is twofold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>the powers are seven,<br>the causes are three,<br>contact counts as a single factor,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.2.18", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i><br>(164) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.2.19", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 62.]<\/i><br>(165) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.20", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, by the putting away of ease and by the putting away of ill, by the passing away of the happiness and of the misery he was wont to feel, he thus, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the Fourth Jhana (the fourth rapt meditation) of that utter purity of mindfulness which comes of disinterestedness, where no ease is felt nor any ill — then the contact, the feeling, the perception, the thinking, the thought, the disinterestedness, the self-collectedness, the faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom, ideation, disinterestedness and vitality, the right views, the right endeavour, etc...."}, {"st": "1.2.21", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 163.] <\/i><br><i>[Summary.]<\/i><br>Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.2.22", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are eight,<br>the Jhana is twofold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>the powers are seven,<br>the causes are three,<br>contact counts as a single factor,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.2.23", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58, etc.]<\/i><br>(166) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br>Answer as in § 164.<br>[Here ends] the Fourfold System of Jhana."}, {"st": "1.2.24", "t": "(b) The Fivefold System of Jhana (pancakanayo).<br>(167) The First Jhana.<br>Question and answer as in the fourfold course, § 160.<br>(168) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.25", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the Second Jhana (the second rapt meditation) wherein is no working of conception, but only of thought discursive — which is born of concentration, and is full of joy and ease — then the contact, the feeling, the perception, the thinking, the thought, the discursive inquiry, the joy, the ease, the self-collectedness, etc...."}, {"st": "1.2.26", "t": "<i>[Continue as for the Second Jhana in § 161.] <\/i><br><i>[Summary.]<\/i><br>Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.2.27", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are eight,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.2.28", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i><br>(169) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br>Contact, thinking, discursive thought, joy, etc....<br><i>[Continue as in § 162.]<\/i><br>[170-175] The Third, Fourth and Fifth Jhanas."}, {"st": "1.2.29", "t": "<i>[These are identical in formulation with the Second, Third and Fourth Jhanas of the Fourfold System, Questions and answers as in §§ 161-166.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.30", "t": "<i>[Here ends] the Fivefold System of Jhana.<\/i><br>(c) The Four Modes of Progress (catasso patipada).<br>(176) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.31", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress being painful and intuition sluggish — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.32", "t": "(177)... [or] when... he... so enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress being painful, but intuition quick..."}, {"st": "1.2.33", "t": "(178)"}, {"st": "1.2.34", "t": "... [or] when... he... so enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress being easy, but intuition sluggish..."}, {"st": "1.2.35", "t": "(179)... [or] when... he... so enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress being easy and intuition quick — then the contact, etc.... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.36", "t": "(180) These four combinations are repeated in the case of the 2nd to the 4th Jhanas on the Fourfold System, and of the 2nd to the 5th on the Fivefold System."}, {"st": "1.2.37", "t": "<i>[Here end] the Four Modes of Progress.<\/i><br>(d) The Four Objects of Thought (cattari aramman ani).<br>(181) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.38", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the First Jhana (the first rapt meditation), wherein conception works and thought discursive, which is born of solitude, and is full of joy and ease, but which is limited, and has a limited object of thought — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.39", "t": "(182)... [or] when... the First Jhana... is limited, but has an object of thought capable of infinite extension..."}, {"st": "1.2.40", "t": "(183)... [or] when... the First Jhana... is capable of infinite extension, but has a limited object of thought..."}, {"st": "1.2.41", "t": "(184)... [or] when... the First Jhana... is capable of infinite extension, and has an object of thought capable of infinite extension — then the contact, etc.... the balance that arises, these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.42", "t": "(185) These four combinations are repeated in the case of the 2nd to the 4th Jhdnas on the Fourfold System, and of the 1st to the 5th Jhanas on the Fivefold System."}, {"st": "1.2.43", "t": "[Here end] the Four Objects of Thought.<br>(e) ( = c and d) The Sixteenfold Combination (solasakkhattukam).<br>(186) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.44", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the first Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.2.45", "t": "(202)"}, {"st": "1.2.46", "t": "<i>[These sixteen combinations are repeated in the case of the 2nd to the 4:th Jhanas on the Fourfold System, and of the 1st to the 5th Jhanas on the Fivefold System,]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.47", "t": "[Here ends] the Sixteenfold Combination."}, {"st": "1.2.48", "t": "The Remaining Seven Artifices which may also be developed in sixteenfold combination (atthakasinam solasakkhattukam)."}, {"st": "1.2.49", "t": "(203) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.50", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, and so, by the artifice of"}, {"st": "1.2.51", "t": "water...<br>fire...<br>air...<br>blue-black...<br>yellow...<br>red...<br>white..."}, {"st": "1.2.52", "t": "enters into and abides in the First Jhana... then the contact, etc. that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.53", "t": "[Here ends] the Sixteenfold Combination in the case of the seven remaining artifices for induction."}, {"st": "1.2.54", "t": "Method II: The Stations of Mastery (abhibhayatanani)."}, {"st": "1.2.55", "t": "“Forms as Limited” (rupani parittani).<br>(a and b) Fourfold and Fivefold Jhana.<br>(204) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.56", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, unconscious of any part of his corporeal self, but seeing external objects to be limited, gets the mastery over them with the thought “I know, I see!” and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, etc.... then the contact, etc. that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.57", "t": "(205) <i>[Repeat in the case of the 2nd to the 4ih Jhana on the Fourfold System, and of the 2nd to the 5th Jhana on the Fivefold System.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.58", "t": "(c) The Four Modes of Progress."}, {"st": "1.2.59", "t": "[206-210] <i>Repeat the four combinations of progress as painful or easy, and of intuition as sluggish or quick set out in §§ 176-180, substituting for “earth - gazing” the Mastery formula just stated.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.60", "t": "(d) The Two Objects of Thought."}, {"st": "1.2.61", "t": "[211-213] <i>Repeat, substituting for “earth - gazing” the Mastery -formula f § 181, where the Jhana “is limited, and has a limited object of thought”, and § 183, where the Jhana “is capable of infinite extension, but has a limited object of thought”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.62", "t": "(e = c and d) The Eightfold Combination (atthakkhattukam)."}, {"st": "1.2.63", "t": "[214-221] <i>Repeat, with the same substitution, §§ 186, 188, 190, 192, 194, 196, 198, and 200 of the Sixteenfold Combination.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.64", "t": "(222) <i>Repeat these eight combinations in the case of each of the remaining Jhanas.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.65", "t": "“Forms as limited and as beautiful or ugly” (rupani parittani suvann a-d ubbannani)."}, {"st": "1.2.66", "t": "[(a) and (b)]"}, {"st": "1.2.67", "t": "(223) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.68", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, unconscious of any part of his corporeal self, but seeing external objects to be limited, and to be beautiful or ugly, gets the mastery over them with the thought, “I know, I see!” and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, etc.... then the contact, etc. that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.69", "t": "(224) <i>Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhanas,<\/i><br>Develop in eightfold combination."}, {"st": "1.2.70", "t": "“Forms as infinite” (rupani appamanani).<br>(a) and (b)<br>(225) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.71", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, unconscious of any part of his corporeal self, but seeing external objects to be infinite, gets the mastery over them with the thoughts “I know, I see!” and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.72", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 204.]<\/i><br>(226) <i>Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhanas.<\/i><br>(c) The Four Modes of Progress.<br><i>[227-231] Repeat §§ 206-210, substituting “infinite” for “limited”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.73", "t": "(d) The Two Objects of Thought.<br>[232-234] <i>Repeat, with the same substitution as in (c), §§ 211-213.<\/i><br>(e = c and d) The Eightfold Combination."}, {"st": "1.2.74", "t": "[235-242] <i>Develop, with the same substitution as in (c) and (d), after the manner of §§ 187, 189, and so on to § 201.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.75", "t": "(243)<i> Repeat these eight combinations in the case of each of the remaining Jhdnas.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.76", "t": "“Forms as infinite and as beautiful or ugly” (rupani appamanani suvanna dubbannani)."}, {"st": "1.2.77", "t": "(a) and (b)"}, {"st": "1.2.78", "t": "(244) <i>Repeat § 223, substituting ‘infinite’ for ‘limited’. (245) Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhanas.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.79", "t": "Develop in eightfold combination."}, {"st": "1.2.80", "t": "“Forms as blue-black”, etc. (rupani nilani).<br>(a)<br>(246) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.81", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, unconscious of any part of his corporeal self, but seeing external objects which are blue-black, blue-black in colour, blue-black in visible expanse, blue-black in luminousness, gets the mastery over them with the thought, “I know, I see!” and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.82", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 204.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.83", "t": "“Forms as yellow”, etc. (rupani pitani)."}, {"st": "1.2.84", "t": "(247) <i>Repeat § 246, substituting for “blue-black, blue-black in colour”, etc. “yellow,” “red”, and “white” successively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.85", "t": "Develop these Stations of Mastery in the Sixteenfold Combination.<br>Method III: The Three First Deliverances (tini vimokkhani)."}, {"st": "1.2.86", "t": "(248) When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, conscious of his bodily form, sees bodily forms, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, etc.... then the contact, etc. which arises, these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.87", "t": "(249) When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, unconscious of his corporeal self, sees external bodily forms, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.88", "t": "<i>[Continue as in preceding section.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.89", "t": "(250) When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, with the thought, “How fair it is!” aloof from sensuous appetites, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.90", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the first Deliverance.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.91", "t": "These three Deliverances may also be developed in Sixteenfold Combination."}, {"st": "1.2.92", "t": "Method IV: The Four Jhanas of the Sublime Abodes (cattari brahmaviharajhanani)."}, {"st": "1.2.93", "t": "Love (metta).<br>(a) Fourfold Jhana.<br>(251) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.94", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana (the first rapt meditation), wherein conception works and thought discursive, which is born of solitude, is full of joy and ease, and is accompanied by Love — then the contact, etc.... <i>[? continue as m § 1]<\/i>... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.95", "t": "(252) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.96", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, suppressing the working of conception and of thought discursive, and so, by earthgazing, enters into and abides in the Second Jhana (the second rapt meditation), which is self -evolved, born of concentration, is full of joy and ease, in that, set free... the mind grows calm and sure, dwelling on high — and which is accompanied by Love — then the contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.97", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the foregoing,]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.98", "t": "(253) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.99", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and further, through the waning of all passion for joy, holds himself unbiassed, the while, mindful and self-possessed, he experiences in his sense-consciousness that ease whereof the Noble Ones declare:"}, {"st": "1.2.100", "t": "“He that is unbiassed and watchful dwelleth at ease”"}, {"st": "1.2.101", "t": "— and so, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the Third Jhana, which is accompanied by Love — then, etc,"}, {"st": "1.2.102", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the foregoing.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.103", "t": "(b) Fivefold Jhana."}, {"st": "1.2.104", "t": "[254-257] <i>Repeat question and answers in §§ 167, 168, 170, 172, adding in each answer, as in the foregoing section, “and which is accompanied by Love”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.105", "t": "Pity (karuna)."}, {"st": "1.2.106", "t": "[258, 259]<i> Repeat question and answers in the preceding sections (a) and (b), hut substituting in each case “and which is accompanied by Pity” for the clause on Love.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.107", "t": "Sympathy (mudita)."}, {"st": "1.2.108", "t": "[260, 261]<i> Repeat question and answers in the preceding two sections, but substituting in each case “and which is accompanied by Sympathy” for the clause on Pity.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.109", "t": "Disinterestedness (upekkha)."}, {"st": "1.2.110", "t": "(262) When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, by the putting away of ease and by the putting away of ill, by the passing away of the happiness and of the misery he was wont to feel, he thus, by earth -gazing, enters into and abides in the Fourth Jhana (the fourth rapt meditation) of that utter purity of mindfulness which comes of disinterestedness, where no ease is felt nor any ill, and which is accompanied by Disinterestedness — then the contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.2.111", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 165.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.2.112", "t": "The Four Jhanas of the Sublime Abodes may be developed in Sixteen Combinations."}, {"st": "1.2.113", "t": "Method V: The Jhana of Foul Things (asubha-jhanam)."}, {"st": "1.2.114", "t": "(263) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.2.115", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, wherein, etc...."}, {"st": "1.2.116", "t": "and which is accompanied by the idea of a bloated corpse...<br>[or] (264) of a discoloured corpse...<br>[or] of a festering corpse...<br>[or] of a corpse with cracked skin...<br>[or] of a corpse gnawn and mangled...<br>[or] of a corpse cut to pieces...<br>[or] of a corpse mutilated and cut in pieces...<br>[or] of a bloody corpse...<br>[or] of a corpse infested with worms...<br>[or] of a skeleton..."}, {"st": "1.2.117", "t": "then the contact... the balance which arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.2.118", "t": "The Jhana of Foul Things may be developed in Sixteen Combinations."}, {"st": "1.2.119", "t": "[Here ends the Chapter on] Good in relation to the Universe of Form."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER III. GOOD IN RELATION TO THE UNIVERSE OF THE FORMLESS"}, {"st": "1.3.1", "t": "The Four Jhanas connected with Formless Existence (cattari arupajjhanani)."}, {"st": "1.3.2", "t": "The Sphere of Unbounded Space (akasanancayatanam).<br>(265) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.3.3", "t": "When, that he may attain to the Formless heavens, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, by passing wholly beyond all consciousness of form, by the dying out of the consciousness of sensory reaction, by turning the attention from any consciousness of the manifold, he enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of unbounded space — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, and all sense of ill must have been put away, and there must have been a dying out of the happiness and misery he was wont to feel — (the rapt meditation) which is imbued with disinterestedness, and where no ease is felt nor any ill, but only the perfect purity that comes of mindfulness and disinterestedness — then the contact, etc.... [cf. § 165] the balance that arises, these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.3.4", "t": "The Sphere of Inlinite Intellection (vinnanancayatanam)."}, {"st": "1.3.5", "t": "(266) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.3.6", "t": "When, that he may attain to the Formless heavens, he cultivates the way thereto, and, having passed wholly beyond the sphere of boundless space, enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of infinite intellection — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc."}, {"st": "1.3.7", "t": "<i>[Continue as in previous section.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.3.8", "t": "The Sphere of Nothingness (akincannayatanam).<br>(267) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.3.9", "t": "When, that he may attain to the Formless heavens, he cultivates the way thereto, and, having passed wholly beyond the sphere of infinite intellection, enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of nothingness — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc."}, {"st": "1.3.10", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 265.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.3.11", "t": "The Sphere where there is neither Perception nor Non-perception (neva-sanna-nasannayatanam)."}, {"st": "1.3.12", "t": "(268) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.3.13", "t": "When, that he may attain to the Formless heavens, he cultivates the way thereto, and, having passed wholly beyond the sphere of nothingness, enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere where there is neither perception nor non-perception — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc."}, {"st": "1.3.14", "t": "<i>[Contifiue as in § 265.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.3.15", "t": "The Four Jhanas connected with Formless Existence may be developed in sixteen combinations."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER IV. DEGREES OF EFFICACY IN GOOD RELATING TO THE THREE KEALMS"}, {"st": "1.4.1", "t": "Good in relation to the Universe of Sense (kamavacarakusalam)."}, {"st": "1.4.2", "t": "(269) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.4.3", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen, which is (I.) accompanied by happiness and associated with knowledge — a thought which is"}, {"st": "1.4.4", "t": "of inferior,<br>or of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy,"}, {"st": "1.4.5", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.6", "t": "desire,<br>or energy,<br>or [another] thought,<br>or investigation;"}, {"st": "1.4.7", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.8", "t": "desire of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.9", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.10", "t": "energy of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.11", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.12", "t": "[another] thought of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.13", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.14", "t": "investigation of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy,"}, {"st": "1.4.15", "t": "then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.4.16", "t": "(270) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.4.17", "t": "When a good thought concerning the sensuous universe has arisen which is (II.) accompanied by happiness, associated with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.4.18", "t": "or (III.) accompanied by happiness, and disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.4.19", "t": "or (IV.) accompanied by happiness, disconnected with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.4.20", "t": "or (V.) accompanied by disinterestedness, and associated with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.4.21", "t": "or (VI.) accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.4.22", "t": "or (VII.) accompanied by disinterestedness, and disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.4.23", "t": "or (VIII.) accompanied by disinterestedness, disconnected with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive —"}, {"st": "1.4.24", "t": "a thought which is of inferior..."}, {"st": "1.4.25", "t": "or of medium...<br>or of superlative efficacy..."}, {"st": "1.4.26", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.27", "t": "desire,<br>or energy,<br>or another thought;"}, {"st": "1.4.28", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.29", "t": "desire of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.30", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.31", "t": "energy of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.32", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.33", "t": "[another] thought of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.34", "t": "then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.4.35", "t": "Good in relation to the Universe of Form."}, {"st": "1.4.36", "t": "(271) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.4.37", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, by earth-gazing enters into and abides in the First Jhana (the first rapt meditation)... which is"}, {"st": "1.4.38", "t": "of inferior,<br>or of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy;"}, {"st": "1.4.39", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.40", "t": "desire,<br>or energy,<br>or a thought,<br>or investigation;"}, {"st": "1.4.41", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.42", "t": "desire... energy... a thought... investigation<br>of inferior,<br>of medium,<br>or of superlative efficacy —"}, {"st": "1.4.43", "t": "then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.4.44", "t": "(272) <i>Repeat in the case of the other Jhanas, both of (a) and (b).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.4.45", "t": "Good in relation to the Formless Universe.<br>(273) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.4.46", "t": "When, that he may attain to the Formless heavens, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, by passing wholly beyond all consciousness of form, by the dying out of the consciousness of sensory reaction, by turning the attention from any consciousness of the manifold, he enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of unbounded space — even into the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc. — (the rapt meditation) where there is neither ill nor ease, but only the perfect purity that comes of mindfulness and disinterestedness, and which is of"}, {"st": "1.4.47", "t": "inferior...<br>medium...<br>or superlative efficacy..."}, {"st": "1.4.48", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.49", "t": "desire...<br>or energy...<br>or a thought...<br>or investigation..."}, {"st": "1.4.50", "t": "or the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.4.51", "t": "desire... energy... a thought... investigation<br>of inferior...<br>medium...<br>superlative efficacy —"}, {"st": "1.4.52", "t": "then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.4.53", "t": "[274-276] <i>Here follows the three remaining “Jhanas connected with Formless Existence”, each modified by the characteristics enumerated in the foregoing ansiver. Cf. §§ 266-268.<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER V. THOUGHT ENGAGED UPON THE HIGHER IDEAL"}, {"st": "1.5.1", "t": "I. The First Path (pathamo maggo.) The Twenty Great Methods (visati mahanaya)."}, {"st": "1.5.2", "t": "Rapt Meditation (jhanam).<br>(i.) The Four Modes of Progress in Purification (suddhikapatipada).<br>(277) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.3", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth"}, {"st": "1.5.4", "t": "— and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, wherein conception works and thought discursive, which is born of solitude, is full of joy and ease, progress thereto being difficult and intuition sluggish —"}, {"st": "1.5.5", "t": "then there is"}, {"st": "1.5.6", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>joy,<br>ease,<br>self-collectedness,<br>the faculties of faith,<br>energy,<br>mindfulness,<br>concentration,<br>wisdom,<br>ideation,<br>happiness,<br>vitality,<br>and the faculty of believing, “I shall come to know the unknown”,<br>right views,<br>right intention,<br>right speech,<br>right action,<br>right livelihood,<br>right endeavour,<br>right mindfulness,<br>right concentration;"}, {"st": "1.5.7", "t": "the powers of faith,<br>energy,<br>mindfulness,<br>concentration,<br>wisdom,<br>conscientiousness,<br>the fear of blame;"}, {"st": "1.5.8", "t": "the absence of lust,<br>hate,<br>dulness,<br>covetousness and malice,<br>right views,<br>conscientiousness,<br>the fear of blame,<br>serenity,<br>lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>facility,<br>fitness and directness in both sense and thought,<br>mindfulness,<br>intelligence,<br>quiet,<br>insight,<br>grasp and balance."}, {"st": "1.5.9", "t": "Now these — or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.10", "t": "[278-282]<i> “Contact”, “feeling”, “perception”, “thinking”, and “thought” are described as in §§ 2-6.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.11", "t": "(283) What on that occasion is conception?"}, {"st": "1.5.12", "t": "The ratiocination, the conception, which on that occasion is the disposition, the fixation, the focussing, the application of the mind, right intention, “Path-component”, “contained in the Path” — this is the conception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.13", "t": "(284)<i> “Discursive thought” is described as in § 8.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.14", "t": "(285) What on that occasion is joy?"}, {"st": "1.5.15", "t": "The joy which on that occasion is gladness, rejoicing at, rejoicing over, mirth, merriment, felicity, exultation, transport of heart, the joy which is a factor in the Great Awakening — this is the joy that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.16", "t": "(286) <i>“Ease” is described as in § 10.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.17", "t": "(287) What on that occasion is self-collectedness?"}, {"st": "1.5.18", "t": "The stability, solidity, absorbed steadfastness of thought which on that occasion is the absence of distraction, balance, imperturbed mental procedure, quiet, the faculty and the power of concentration, right concentration, the concentration which is a factor in the Great Awakening, a “Path-component”, “contained in the Path” — this is the conception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.19", "t": "(288) <i>“Faith” is described as in § 12.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.20", "t": "(289) What on that occasion is the faculty of energy?"}, {"st": "1.5.21", "t": "The mental inception of energy which there is on that occasion, the striving and the onward effort, the exertion and endeavour, the zeal and ardour, the vigour and fortitude, the state of unfaltering effort, the state of sustained desire, the state of unflinching endurance, the solid grip of the burden, energy, energy as faculty and as power, right energy, the energy which is a factor in the Great Awakening, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the energy that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.22", "t": "(290) What on that occasion is the faculty of mindfulness?"}, {"st": "1.5.23", "t": "The mindfulness which on that occasion is recollecting, calling back to mind the mindfulness which is remembering, bearing in mind, the opposite of superficiality and of obliviousness; mindfulness, mindfulness as faculty and as power, right mindfulness, the mindfulness which is a factor in the Great Awakening, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the mindfulness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.24", "t": "(291)<i> “Concentration” is described in the same terms as “self-collectedness”, § 287.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.25", "t": "(292) What on that occasion is the faculty of wisdom?"}, {"st": "1.5.26", "t": "The wisdom which there is on that occasion is understanding, search, research, searching the Truth, discernment, discrimination, differentiation, erudition, proficiency, subtlety, criticism, reflection, analysis, breadth, sagacity, leading, insight, intelligence, incitement, wisdom as faculty and as power, wisdom as a sword, as a height, as light, as glory, as splendour, as a precious stone; the absence of dulness, searching the Truth, right views, that searching the Truth which is a factor in the Great Awakening, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the wisdom that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.27", "t": "[293-295]<i> The faculties of “ideation”, “happiness”, and “vitality” are described as in §§ 17-19.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.28", "t": "(296) What on that occasion is the faculty of believing, “I shall come to know the unknown” (anannatannassamitindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.5.29", "t": "The wisdom that makes for the realization of those Truths that are unrealized, uncomprehended, unattained to, undiscerned, unknown — the wisdom that is understanding, search, research, searching the Truth, etc."}, {"st": "1.5.30", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 292.]<\/i><br>(297) What on that occasion are right views? <i>Answer as for “wisdom”, § 292.<\/i><br>(298) <i>“Eight intention” is described in the same terms as “conception”, § 283.<\/i><br>(299) What on that occasion is right speech (samma vaca)?"}, {"st": "1.5.31", "t": "To renounce on that occasion, abstain and refrain from, and feel averse to, the four errors of speech, to leave them uncommitted and undone, to incur no guilt, nor to trespass nor transgress with respect to them, to destroy the causeway leading to them — right speech, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the right speech that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.32", "t": "(300) What on that occasion is right action (samma kammanto)?"}, {"st": "1.5.33", "t": "To renounce on that occasion, abstain and refrain from, and feel averse to, the three errors of conduct, to leave them uncommitted and undone, to incur no guilt, nor to trespass nor transgress with respect to them, to destroy the causeway leading to them — right conduct, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the right conduct that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.34", "t": "(301) What on that occasion is right livelihood (samma ajivo)?"}, {"st": "1.5.35", "t": "To renounce on that occasion, abstain and refrain from, and feel averse to, wrong modes of livelihood, to leave them unpractised and undone, to incur no guilt, nor to trespass nor transgress with respect to them, to destroy the causeway leading to them — right livelihood, a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is the right livelihood that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.36", "t": "[302-304] <i>“Eight endeavour”, “right mindfulness”, “right concentration”, are described as in §§ 289-291.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.37", "t": "[305-311] <i>The “powers” of “faith”, “energy”, “mindfulness”, “concentration” and “wisdom” are described as in §§ 288-292; those of “conscientiousness” and “the fear of blame” as in §§ 30, 31.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.38", "t": "[312-319] <i>“The absence of lust” and “the absence of hate” are described as in §§ 32, 33; “the absence of dulness” as in § 309 (“wisdom”); “the absence of covetousness” and “the absence of malice” are described as in §§ 35, 36; “conscientiousness” and “the fear of blame” as in §§ 38, 39; “right views” as in § 292 or 309 (“wisdom”).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.39", "t": "(320) What on that occasion is serenity of sense?"}, {"st": "1.5.40", "t": "The serenity, the composure which there is on that occasion, the calming, the tranquillizing, the tranquillity of the skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses, the serenity which is a factor in the Great Awakening — this is the serenity of sense that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.41", "t": "(321) What on that occasion is serenity of thought?"}, {"st": "1.5.42", "t": "The serenity, the composure which there is on that occasion, the calming, the tranquillizing, the tranquillity of the skandha of intellect, the serenity which is a factor in the Great Awakening — this is the serenity of thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.5.43", "t": "[322-331] <i>The remaining five attributes characterizing both sense and thought “on that occasion”:— “buoyancy”, “plasticity”, etc. — are described as in §§ 42-51.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.44", "t": "[332-337] <i>“Mindfulness”, “intelligence”, “quiet”, “insight”, “grasp” and “balance” are described as in §§ 290, 292 (“wisdom”), 291, 292, 289 (“energy”) and 291 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.45", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.46", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.5.47", "t": "Now at that time"}, {"st": "1.5.48", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are nine,<br>the Jhana is fivefold,<br>the Path is eightfold,<br>the powers are seven,<br>the causes are three,"}, {"st": "1.5.49", "t": "the skandhas of"}, {"st": "1.5.50", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.51", "t": "<i>[Here the questions and answers concerning the first two, of the four skandhas enumerated are to he understood to follow as in §§ 59-61.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.52", "t": "(338) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.5.53", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>joy,<br>self-collectedness,"}, {"st": "1.5.54", "t": "the faculties of"}, {"st": "1.5.55", "t": "faith,<br>concentration,<br>energy,<br>wisdom,<br>mindfulness,<br>vitality,<br>believing “I shall come to know the unknown”;"}, {"st": "1.5.56", "t": "right views,<br>right livelihood,<br>right intention,<br>right endeavour,<br>right speech,<br>right mindfulness,<br>right action,<br>right concentration;"}, {"st": "1.5.57", "t": "the seven powers;"}, {"st": "1.5.58", "t": "the absence of lust,<br>hate and dulness;"}, {"st": "1.5.59", "t": "the absence of covetousness and malice,<br>the fear of blame;"}, {"st": "1.5.60", "t": "right views;"}, {"st": "1.5.61", "t": "conscientiousness,<br>serenity,<br>wieldiness,<br>buoyancy,<br>fitness,<br>plasticity,<br>directness of sense and thought;"}, {"st": "1.5.62", "t": "mindfulness and intelligence;<br>quiet and insight;<br>grasp and balance."}, {"st": "1.5.63", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.5.64", "t": "<i>[Questions on the remaining items in the “Summary” are understood to follow.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.65", "t": "(340) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.66", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth — and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress thereto being difficult, but intuition quick..."}, {"st": "1.5.67", "t": "[or] (341)... progress thereto being easy, but intuition sluggish..."}, {"st": "1.5.68", "t": "[or] (342)... progress thereto being easy and intuition quick — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.69", "t": "(343) <i>Repeat the Four Modes in the case of the 2nd to the 4th Jhana on the Fourfold System, and of the 1st to the 5th Jhana on the Fivefold System.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.70", "t": "[Here end] the Modes of Progress in Purification.<br>(ii.) The Section on Emptiness (sunnatam).<br><i>(a and b)<\/i><br>(344) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.71", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth — and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana, wherein conception works and thought discursive, which is born of solitude, is full of joy and ease, and which is Empty — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.72", "t": "(345) <i>Repeat the 2nd to the 4th Jhanas on the Fourfold System, and the 1st to the 5th on the Fivefold System, with the addition in each case of the phrase “and which is Empty”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.73", "t": "[Here ends] the “Emptiness” Section."}, {"st": "1.5.74", "t": "(c) The Modes of Progress, with “Emptiness” as the Basis (sunnata-mulaka-patipada)."}, {"st": "1.5.75", "t": "(346) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.76", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal... and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he... enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress thereto being difficult and intuition sluggish, the method being the concept of Emptiness — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.77", "t": "[347-349]<i> Repeat the same formula, substituting in succession the three remaining Modes of Progress (§§ 176-179), with the addition in each case of the plirase “the method being the concept of Emptiness”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.78", "t": "(350) <i>Repeat the same formula, substituting in succession the remaining Jhdnas on the Fourfold System and those on the Fivefold System, and applying in each case the Four Modes of Progress, with the additional phrase on “Emptiness”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.79", "t": "(ii.) The Aimless (appanihitam).<br>(a and b)<br>(351) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.80", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal... and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he... enters into and abides in the First Jhana... which is born of solitude, is full of joy and ease, and which is Aimless — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.81", "t": "(352) <i>Repeat the same formida, substituting the remaining three, and the five Jhdnas in succession, with the addition in each case of the phrase “and which is Aimless”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.82", "t": "(c) The Modes of Progress, with Aimlessness as the Basis (appanihita-mulaka-patipada)."}, {"st": "1.5.83", "t": "(353) When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal... and when, that he may attain to the First Stage of it, he... enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, the method being the concept of Aimlessness — then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.84", "t": "[354-356] <i>Repeat the same formula, substituting in succession the three remaining Modes of Progress, with the addition in each case of the phrase “the method being the concept of Aimlessness”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.85", "t": "(357) <i>Repeat the same formula, substituting in succession the remaining three, and the five Jhanas, and applying in each case the Four Modes of Progress, with the additional phrase on “Aimlessness”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.86", "t": "[2-20. The Eemaining Nineteen Great Methods.]"}, {"st": "1.5.87", "t": "(358) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.88", "t": "Here follow nineteen concepts, each of which can be substituted for “the Jhana of the Higher Ideal” in the preceding 81 answers [§§ 277-357], as a vehicle in training the mind for Arahatship. They are as follows:"}, {"st": "1.5.89", "t": "The Path of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.90", "t": "The Advance in Mindfulness toward the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.91", "t": "The System of Eight Efforts toward the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.92", "t": "The Series of Mystic Potencies applied to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.93", "t": "The Faculty relating to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.94", "t": "The Power relating to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.95", "t": "The Great Awakening to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.96", "t": "The Truth of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.97", "t": "The Peace of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.98", "t": "The Doctrine of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.99", "t": "The Skandha related to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.100", "t": "The Sphere of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.101", "t": "The Element of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.102", "t": "The Nutriment of the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.103", "t": "Contact with the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.104", "t": "Feeling relating to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.105", "t": "Perception relating to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.106", "t": "Thinking relating to the Higher Ideal."}, {"st": "1.5.107", "t": "Thought relating to the Higher Ideal.<br>[The Dominant Influences in the Modes of Progress (adhipati).]<br>(359) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.108", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal... and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he... enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is painful and intuition sluggish, and the dominant influence in which is desire, energy, a thought, or investigation, then the contact... the balance that arises — these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.109", "t": "(360)<i> Repeat this formula in the case of the remaining three and five Jhanas.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.110", "t": "(361) <i>Repeat the foregoing [§§ 359, 360] in the case of each of the nineteen remaining “Great Methods”.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.111", "t": "[Here ends] the First Path.<br>II. The Second Path.<br>(362) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.112", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth — and when, that he may attain to the Second Stage, he has diminished the strength of sensual passions and of malice, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish — then the contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.113", "t": "[Here ends] the Second Path.<br>III. The Third Path.<br>(363) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.114", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth — and when, that he may attain to the Third Stage, he has put away the entire residuum of sensual passions and of malice, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish — then the contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... the balance that arises — these... states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.115", "t": "[Here ends] the Third Path.<br>IV. The Fourth Path.<br>(364) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "1.5.116", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain the Fourth Stage, he has put away absolutely and entirely all passion for Form, all passion for the Formless, all conceit, excitement and ignorance, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish — then the contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... the balance that arises — these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.117", "t": "What on that occasion is the faculty of knowledge made perfect (annindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.5.118", "t": "The wisdom that makes for the realization of those truths that have been realized, comprehended, attained to, discerned and known — the wisdom that is understanding, search, research, searching the Truth, etc."}, {"st": "1.5.119", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 292.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.5.120", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, these are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.5.121", "t": "[Here ends] the Fourth Path."}, {"st": "1.5.122", "t": "[Here ends] Thought engaged upon the Higher Ideal."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "PART II. BAD STATES OF CONSCIOUSNESS"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VI. THE TWELVE BAD THOUGHTS"}, {"st": "1.7.1", "t": "I."}, {"st": "1.7.2", "t": "(365) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.3", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen, which is accompanied by happiness, and associated with views and opinions, and has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.7.4", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>joy,<br>ease,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.5", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>ideation,<br>happiness,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.6", "t": "wrong views,<br>wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.7", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.8", "t": "lust, covetousness,<br>dulness, wrong views,"}, {"st": "1.7.9", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,"}, {"st": "1.7.10", "t": "quiet,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.11", "t": "Now, these — or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states that there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.12", "t": "[366-370] What on that occasion is contact... feeling... perception... thinking... thought?"}, {"st": "1.7.13", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 2-6 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.14", "t": "(371) What on that occasion is conception?"}, {"st": "1.7.15", "t": "<i>Answer as in § 7, substituting ‘wrong intention’ (micchasankappo) for ‘right intention’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.16", "t": "[372-374] What on that occasion is discursive thought... joy... ease?<br><i>Answers as in §§ 8-10 respectively.<\/i><br>(375) What on that occasion is self-collectedness?"}, {"st": "1.7.17", "t": "<i>Answer as in § 11, substituting ‘ wrong concentration ‘for’ right concentration’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.18", "t": "(376) What on that occasion is the faculty of energy?<br><i>Answer as in § 13, substituting ‘wrong endeavour’ for ‘right endeavour’.<\/i><br>(377) What on that occasion is the faculty of concentration?<br><i>Answer as in § 375.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.19", "t": "[378-380] What on that occasion is the faculty of ideation... happiness... vitality?"}, {"st": "1.7.20", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 17-19 respectively.<\/i><br>(381) What on that occasion are wrong views (micchaditthi)?<br>The views which on that occasion are"}, {"st": "1.7.21", "t": "a walking in opinion,<br>the jungle of opinion,<br>the wilderness of opinion,<br>the puppet-show of opinion,<br>the scuffling of opinion,<br>the Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination totvards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>a by-path,<br>a wrong road,<br>wrongness,<br>the ‘fording place’,<br>shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "1.7.22", "t": "— these are the wrong views that there then are."}, {"st": "1.7.23", "t": "[382-384] What on that occasion is wrong intention.. wrong endeavour... wrong concentration?"}, {"st": "1.7.24", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 371, 376, 375 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.25", "t": "[885, 386] What on that occasion is the power of energy... the power of concentration?"}, {"st": "1.7.26", "t": "<i>Ansicers as in §§ 383, 384 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.27", "t": "(387) What on that occasion is the power of unconscientiousness (ahirikabalam)?"}, {"st": "1.7.28", "t": "The absence which there is on that occasion of anyfeeling of conscientious scruple when scruples ought to be felt, the absence of conscientious scruple at attaining to bad and evil states — this is the power of unconscientiousness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.29", "t": "(388) What on that occasion is the power of disregard of blame (anottappabalam)?"}, {"st": "1.7.30", "t": "The absence which there is on that occasion of any sense of guilt where a sense of guilt ought to be felt, the absence of a sense of guilt at attaining to bad and evil states — this is the power of disregard of blame that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.31", "t": "(389) What on that occasion is lust?"}, {"st": "1.7.32", "t": "The lust, lusting, lustfulness which there is on that occasion, the infatuation, the feeling and being infatuated, the covetousness, the lust that is the root of badness — this is the lust that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.33", "t": "(390) What on that occasion is dulness?"}, {"st": "1.7.34", "t": "The lack of knowledge, of vision, which there is on that occasion;<br>the lack of co-ordination, of judgment, of wakefulness, of penetration;<br>the inability to comprehend, to grasp thoroughly;<br>the inability to compare, to consider, to demonstrate;<br>the folly, the childishness, the lack of intelligence;<br>the dulness that is vagueness, obfuscation, ignorance, the Flood of ignorance, the Bond of ignorance, the bias of ignorance, the obsession of ignorance, the barrier of ignorance;<br>the dulness that is the root of badness"}, {"st": "1.7.35", "t": "— this the dulness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.36", "t": "[391-397] What on that occasion is covetousness... are wrong views... is unconscientiousness... disregard of blame... quiet... grasp... balance?"}, {"st": "1.7.37", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 389, 381, 387, 388, 375, 376, and, again, 375 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.38", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.39", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.7.40", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.7.41", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fivefold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>the powers are four,<br>the causes are two,<br>contact, &gt; are each single [factors];<br>etc. &gt; etc."}, {"st": "1.7.42", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.43", "t": "(398) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.7.44", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>joy,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.45", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.46", "t": "wrong views,<br>wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.47", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.48", "t": "lust, covetousness,<br>dulness; wrong views;"}, {"st": "1.7.49", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.50", "t": "quiet,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.51", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.7.52", "t": "[Continue as in § 58.]<br>II.<br>(399) Which are the states fchat are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.53", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by pleasure, associated with views and opinions, and prompted by a conscious motive, and which has as its object a sight... or what not, then there is contact... balance..."}, {"st": "1.7.54", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the First Thought, § 365.]<\/i><br>III.<br>(400) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.55", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by happiness and disconnected with views and opinions, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.56", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the first Bad Thought, but omitting the single, twice enumerated item ‘wrong views’.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.57", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.7.58", "t": "Now, at that time"}, {"st": "1.7.59", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fivefold,<br>the Path is threefold,<br>etc. etc."}, {"st": "1.7.60", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i><br>(401) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br><i>Answer as in § 398, omitting ‘wrong views’.<\/i><br>IV."}, {"st": "1.7.61", "t": "(402) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.62", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by pleasure, disconnected with views and opinions, and prompted by a conscious motive, and which has as its object a sight... or what not, then there is contact... balance..."}, {"st": "1.7.63", "t": "<i>[Continue as in the Third Thought, § 400.]<\/i><br>V.<br>(403) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.64", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by disinterestedness, and associated with views and opinions, and has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a [mental] state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.7.65", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.66", "t": "the faculties of<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>ideation;"}, {"st": "1.7.67", "t": "disinterestedness,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.68", "t": "wrong views,<br>wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.69", "t": "the powers of<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.70", "t": "lust, covetousness,<br>dulness; wrong views;"}, {"st": "1.7.71", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>composure,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.72", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.73", "t": "[404-407] <i>Questions and answers on ‘contact’, ‘feeling’, ‘disinterestedness’, and the faculty of disinterestedness’ identical with those in §§ 151-154.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.74", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.7.75", "t": "Now, at that time"}, {"st": "1.7.76", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc.<br>the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is fourfold,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.7.77", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i><br>(408) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br>Contact, thinking, conception, discursive thought, self-collectedness, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.78", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 398, ‘joy’ having been omitted as incompatible with ‘disinterestedness’.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.79", "t": "VI."}, {"st": "1.7.80", "t": "(409) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.81", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with views and opinions, and prompted by a conscious motive, and which has as its object a sight... or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.82", "t": "<i>[Continue as in Thought V.]<\/i><br>VII.<br>(410) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.83", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by disinterestedness, and disconnected with views and opinions, and which has as its object a sight... or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.84", "t": "<i>[Continue as in Thought V. omitting ‘wrong views’.]<\/i><br>[Summary.]<br>Now at that time"}, {"st": "1.7.85", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc.<br>the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is threefold,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.7.86", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 397a.]<\/i><br>(411) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br><i>Ansiver as in § 398, omitting both ‘joy’ and ‘wrong views’.<\/i><br>VIII."}, {"st": "1.7.87", "t": "(412) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.88", "t": "<i>Ansiver as in Thought VII. with the additional Jactor, inserted as in Thoughts II,, IV. VI. of ‘prompted by a conscious motive’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.89", "t": "IX."}, {"st": "1.7.90", "t": "(413) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.91", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by melancholy and associated with repugnance, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a mental state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.7.92", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>distress,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.93", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>ideation,<br>melancholy,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.94", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.95", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.96", "t": "hate,<br>dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.97", "t": "malice;"}, {"st": "1.7.98", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>quiet,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.99", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.100", "t": "(414)<i> The question and answer on ‘contact’, § 2.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.101", "t": "(415) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.7.102", "t": "The mental pain, the mental distress (dukkham), which, on that occasion, is born of contact with the appropriate element of representative intellection; the painful, distressful sensation which is born of contact with thought; the painful, distressful feeling which is born of contact with thought — this is the distress that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.103", "t": "[416, 417] What on that occasion is distress (dukkham)... the faculty of melancholy (domanassindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.7.104", "t": "<i>Answers as for ‘feeling’ in § 415, omitting ‘with the appropriate element of representative intellection’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.105", "t": "(418) What on that occasion is hate?"}, {"st": "1.7.106", "t": "The hate, hating, hatred which on that occasion is a disordered temper, the getting upset, opposition, hostility, churlishness, abruptness, disgust of heart — this is the hate that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.107", "t": "(419) What on that occasion is malice?"}, {"st": "1.7.108", "t": "<i>Answer as for ‘hate’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.109", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.110", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.7.111", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.7.112", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc.<br>the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is threefold,<br>the powers are four,<br>the causes are two,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.7.113", "t": "<i>[Continue as in §§ 58-61.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.114", "t": "(420) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.7.115", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.116", "t": "the faculties of<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.117", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.118", "t": "the powers of<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.119", "t": "hate,<br>dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.120", "t": "malice;"}, {"st": "1.7.121", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>quiet,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.122", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.7.123", "t": "X."}, {"st": "1.7.124", "t": "(421) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.125", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by melancholy, associated with repugnance, and prompted by a conscious motive, and which has as its object a sight... or what not, then there is contact, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.126", "t": "<i>[Continue as in Thought IX.]<\/i><br>XI.<br>(422) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.127", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by disinterestedness and associated with perplexity, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a mental state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.7.128", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.129", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy, disinterestedness,<br>ideation, vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.130", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour;"}, {"st": "1.7.131", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.132", "t": "perplexity;"}, {"st": "1.7.133", "t": "dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.134", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>grasp."}, {"st": "1.7.135", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.136", "t": "(423) What on that occasion is contact?<br><i>The usual formula.<\/i><br>(424) What on that occasion is self-collectedness?"}, {"st": "1.7.137", "t": "The sustaining of thought which there is on that occasion — this is the self-collectedness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.138", "t": "(425) What on that occasion is perplexity (vicikiccha)?"}, {"st": "1.7.139", "t": "The doubt, the hesitating, the dubiety, which on that occasion is puzzlement perplexity; distraction, standing at cross-roads; collapse, uncertainty of grasp; evasion, hesitation; incapacity of grasping thoroughly, stiffness of mind, mental scarifying — this is the perplexity that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.140", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.7.141", "t": "Now, at that time"}, {"st": "1.7.142", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc.<br>the faculties are four,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is twofold,<br>the powers are three,<br>the cause is one,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.7.143", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.144", "t": "(426) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.7.145", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.146", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.147", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour;"}, {"st": "1.7.148", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.149", "t": "perplexity,<br>dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.150", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>grasp."}, {"st": "1.7.151", "t": "Or whatever other, etc.<br><i>[Continue as in § 420.]<\/i><br>XII.<br>(427) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "1.7.152", "t": "When a bad thought has arisen which is accompanied by disinterestedness and associated with excitement, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, a touch, a mental state, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.7.153", "t": "contact,<br>feeling,<br>perception,<br>thinking,<br>thought,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness,<br>the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.154", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.155", "t": "the powers of energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.156", "t": "excitement;"}, {"st": "1.7.157", "t": "dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.158", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>quiet, grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.159", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are bad."}, {"st": "1.7.160", "t": "(428) <i>Usual question and answer on ‘contact’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.161", "t": "(429) What on that occasion is excitement (uddhaccam)?"}, {"st": "1.7.162", "t": "The excitement of mind which on that occasion is disquietude, agitation of heart, turmoil of mind — this is the excitement that there then is."}, {"st": "1.7.163", "t": "Now, at that time"}, {"st": "1.7.164", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc. the faculties are five,<br>the Jhana is fourfold,<br>the Path is threefold,<br>the powers are four,<br>the cause is one, etc."}, {"st": "1.7.165", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.7.166", "t": "(430) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.7.167", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.7.168", "t": "the faculties of<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "1.7.169", "t": "wrong intention,<br>wrong endeavour,<br>wrong concentration;"}, {"st": "1.7.170", "t": "the powers of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame;"}, {"st": "1.7.171", "t": "excitement;"}, {"st": "1.7.172", "t": "dulness;"}, {"st": "1.7.173", "t": "unconscientiousness,<br>disregard of blame,<br>quiet,<br>grasp,<br>balance."}, {"st": "1.7.174", "t": "Or whatever other, etc.<br><i>[Continue as in § 62.]<\/i><br>[Here end] the Twelve Bad Thoughts."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "PART III. INDETERMINATE STATES OF CONSCIOUSNESS"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER I. ON EFFECT, OR RESULT"}, {"st": "1.9.1", "t": "A. Good Karma."}, {"st": "1.9.2", "t": "In the sensuous universe."}, {"st": "1.9.3", "t": "(a) The Five Modes of Cognition considered as effects of good (kusalavipakani pane a-v innanani).]"}, {"st": "1.9.4", "t": "<i>(i.) <\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.5", "t": "(431) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.6", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe, visual cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object something seen, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.7", "t": "contact, thinking,<br>feeling, thought,<br>perception, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.8", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.9", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.10", "t": "(432) <i>Question and answer on ‘contact’ as above, passim.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.11", "t": "(433) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.9.12", "t": "The mental [condition], neither pleasant nor unpleasant, which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of visual cognition; the sensation, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful; the feeling, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.13", "t": "(434) What on that occasion is perception?"}, {"st": "1.9.14", "t": "The perception, the perceiving, the state of having perceived, which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of visual cognition — this is the perception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.15", "t": "(435) What on that occasion is thinking?"}, {"st": "1.9.16", "t": "The thinking, the cogitating, the reflection which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of visual cognition — this is the thinking that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.17", "t": "(436) What on that occasion is thought?"}, {"st": "1.9.18", "t": "The thought which on that occasion is ideation, mind, heart, that which is clear, ideation as the sphere of mind, the faculty of ideation, intellect, the skandha of intellect, the appropriate element of visual cognition — this is the thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.19", "t": "(437) What on that occasion is disinterestedness?"}, {"st": "1.9.20", "t": "Answer as for ‘feeling’, § 436, omitting the phrase ‘which is born of contact with the appropriate element of visual cognition’."}, {"st": "1.9.21", "t": "(438) What on that occasion is self-collectedness?"}, {"st": "1.9.22", "t": "The persistence of thought which there is on that occasion — this is the self-collectedness that there then is. (439) What on that occasion is the faculty of ideation?"}, {"st": "1.9.23", "t": "<i>Answer as for ‘thought’, § 436.<\/i><br>(440) What on that occasion is the faculty of disinterestedness?<br><i>Answer as in § 437.<\/i><br>(441) What on that occasion is the faculty of vitality?"}, {"st": "1.9.24", "t": "<i>Answer as in § 19.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.25", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are the states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.26", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.27", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.9.28", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are three,<br>contact counts as a single factor,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.29", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58],<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.30", "t": "the faculty of ideation counts as a single factor,<br>the element of visual cognition counts as a single factor,<br>the sphere of [mental] states counts as a single factor,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.31", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.32", "t": "(442) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.9.33", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>self-collectedness,<br>the faculty of vitality,"}, {"st": "1.9.34", "t": "or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.9.35", "t": "<i>(ii.-v.)<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.36", "t": "(443) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.37", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe,"}, {"st": "1.9.38", "t": "auditory cognition,<br>olfactory cognition, or<br>gustatory cognition"}, {"st": "1.9.39", "t": "has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object"}, {"st": "1.9.40", "t": "a sound,<br>a smell, or<br>a taste"}, {"st": "1.9.41", "t": "respectively... or"}, {"st": "1.9.42", "t": "cognition of body"}, {"st": "1.9.43", "t": "has arisen, accompanied by ease, and having as its object something tangible, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.44", "t": "contact, thinking,<br>feeling, thought,<br>perception, ease,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.45", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>ease,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.46", "t": "Now, these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.47", "t": "(444)<i> Question and answer on ‘contact’ as above, passim,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.48", "t": "(445) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.9.49", "t": "The bodily pleasure, the bodily ease, which on that occasion is born of the appropriate element of the cognition of body; the pleasurable, easeful sensation which is born of contact with the body; the pleasurable, easeful feeling which is born of contact with the body — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.50", "t": "(446) What on that occasion is perception?"}, {"st": "1.9.51", "t": "The perception, the perceiving, the state of having perceived, which on that occasion is born of contact with appropriate element of the cognition of body — this is the perception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.52", "t": "(447) What on that occasion is thinking?"}, {"st": "1.9.53", "t": "The thinking, the cogitating, the reflection, which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of the cognition of body — this is the thinking that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.54", "t": "(448) What on that occasion is thought?"}, {"st": "1.9.55", "t": "The thought which on that occasion is ideation, mind, heart, that which is clear; ideation as the sphere of mind, the faculty of ideation, intellect, the skandha of intellect, the appropriate element of the cognition of body — this is the thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.56", "t": "(449) What on that occasion is ease?"}, {"st": "1.9.57", "t": "The bodily pleasure, the bodily ease which on that occasion is the pleasant, easeful sensation born of contact with the body; the pleasant, easeful feeling born of contact with the body — this is the ease that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.58", "t": "[450-453] What on that occasion is self-collectedness... the faculty of ideation... of ease... of vitality?"}, {"st": "1.9.59", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 438, 448, 449 and 441 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.60", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.61", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.62", "t": "Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.9.63", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.64", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 441a, substituting ‘the element of the cognition of body’ for ‘the element of visual cognition’.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.65", "t": "(454) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?<br><i>Answer as in § 442.<\/i><br>(b) Good (karma) taking effect in ideation (kusalavipaka manodhatu).<br>(455) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.66", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe, an element of ideation has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.67", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.68", "t": "the faculties of<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.69", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.70", "t": "(456) <i>Question and ansiver on ‘contact’ as above, passim,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.71", "t": "(457) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.9.72", "t": "The mental [condition], neither pleasant nor unpleasant, which on that occasion is born of contact with the appropriate element of ideation; the sensation, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful; the feeling, born of contact with thought, which is neither easeful nor painful — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.73", "t": "[458-460] What on that occasion is perception... thinking... thought?"}, {"st": "1.9.74", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 446-448, suhstituting ‘element of ideation’ for ‘element of the cognition of body’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.75", "t": "(461) What on that occasion is conception?"}, {"st": "1.9.76", "t": "The ratiocination, the conceiving which on that occasion is the disposition, the fixation, the focussing, the application of the mind — this is the conception that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.77", "t": "(462) What on that occasion is discursive thought?"}, {"st": "1.9.78", "t": "The process, the sustained procedure, the progress and access [of the mind] which on that occasion is the continuous adjusting and directing of thought — this is the discursive thought that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.79", "t": "[463-467] What on that occasion is disinterestedness... self-collectedness... the faculty of ideation... of disinterestedness... of vitality?"}, {"st": "1.9.80", "t": "<i>Answers as m §§ 437, 438, 460, 440, 441 respectively.<\/i><br>[Summary.]<br>Now, on that occasion"}, {"st": "1.9.81", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are three,<br>contact counts as a single factor,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.82", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.83", "t": "the faculty of ideation counts as a single factor,<br>the element of ideation counts as a single factor,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.84", "t": "(468) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.9.85", "t": "Contact, discursive thought,<br>thinking, self-collectedness,<br>conception, the faculty of vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.86", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.9.87", "t": "(c) Good (karma) taking effect in representative intellection (kusala-vipaka manoyinnanadhatu)."}, {"st": "1.9.88", "t": "<i>(i.) When accompanied by happiness,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.89", "t": "(469) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.90", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe, an element of representative cognition has arisen, accompanied by happiness and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, the idea [of any of these], or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.91", "t": "contact, conception,<br>feeling, discursive thought,<br>perception, joy,<br>thinking, ease,<br>thought, self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.92", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>happiness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.93", "t": "These, or whatever other, etc."}, {"st": "1.9.94", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 455.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.95", "t": "[470-482] <i>These thirteen constituent states are described as in §§ 2-11 and 17-19, with the exception of ‘conception’ (vitakko) and ‘self-collectedness’ (cittass’ ekaggata), which are described with the restricted connotation used m §§ 461, 464.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.96", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.97", "t": "<i>Identical tvith § 467a, but ‘the element of representative cognition’ (manovinnanadhatu) must be substituted for ‘the element of ideation’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.98", "t": "(483) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.9.99", "t": "Contact, discursive thought,<br>thinking, joy,<br>conception, self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.100", "t": "the faculty of:<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.101", "t": "Or whatever incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.9.102", "t": "<i>(ii.) When accompanied by disinterestedness.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.103", "t": "(484) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.104", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe, an element of representative cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object a sight, etc. (cf. above, § 469]... then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.105", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.106", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.107", "t": "These, or whatever other, etc."}, {"st": "1.9.108", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 469.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.109", "t": "[485-496] <i>These Thirteen states are described as in the foregoing section (i.), except that the questions and answers on ‘feeling’ and ‘disinterestedness’, as given in §§ 152-154, must he substituted for those on ‘feeling’, ‘joy’, and the ‘faculty of happiness’ given in §§ 471, 477, and 481. ‘Ease’ is omitted.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.110", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.111", "t": "<i>Terms identical with those in § 482a.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.112", "t": "(497)<i> The skandha of syntheses is identical with the content stated in § 483, hut with the omission of ‘joy’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.113", "t": "(d) The Eight Main Types of Kesults (attha mahavipaka)."}, {"st": "1.9.114", "t": "(498) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.115", "t": "When, as the result of good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the sensuous universe, an element of representative cognition has arisen,"}, {"st": "1.9.116", "t": "i. accompanied by happiness and associated with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.9.117", "t": "ii. accompanied by happiness, associated with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.9.118", "t": "iii. accompanied by happiness and disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.9.119", "t": "iv. accompanied by happiness, disconnected with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.9.120", "t": "v. accompanied by disinterestedness and associated with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.9.121", "t": "vi. accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.9.122", "t": "vii. accompanied by disinterestedness and disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.9.123", "t": "viii. accompanied by disinterestedness, disconnectedwith knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive, and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not, then there is contact... balance."}, {"st": "1.9.124", "t": "These, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.125", "t": "That absence of lust which is the root of indeterminateness... that absence of hate which is the root of indeterminateness... that absence of dulness which is the root of indeterminateness... these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.126", "t": "In the universe of Form."}, {"st": "1.9.127", "t": "(499) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.128", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of Form, he cultivates the way thereto, and aloof from sensuous appetites, etc. enters into and abides in the First Jhana... then there is contact, etc. Now, these... are states that are good. But when, as the result of just this good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the universe of Form, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, etc. enters into and abides in the First Jhana... then there is contact, etc. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.129", "t": "<i>(500) Repeat, substituting the formulce of the remaining Jhanas on the Fourfold System, and of all the Jhanas on the Fivefold System.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.130", "t": "In the universe of the Formless."}, {"st": "1.9.131", "t": "(501) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.132", "t": "When, that he may attain to the heavens of the Formless, he cultivates the way thereto, and so, by passing wholly beyond all consciousness of form, by the dying out of the consciousness of sensory reaction, by turning the attention from any consciousness of the manifold, he enters into and abides in that frame of mind which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of unbounded space — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc. [continue as in § 265] — then the contact... the balance that arises, these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.133", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good karma having been wrought, having been stored up in connexion with the universe of the Formless, he, by passing wholly beyond all consciousness of form, by the dying out of the consciousness of sensory reaction, by turning the attention from any consciousness of the manifold, enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of unbounded space — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc. [continue as above]... then the contact... the balance that arises, these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.134", "t": "<i>[502-504] Here follow in succession the other three ‘Jhanas connected with Formless Existence’ (§§ 266-268), namely, ‘the Sphere of Infinite Intellection’, ‘the Sphere of Nothingness’, and ‘the Sphere where there is neither Perception nor Non-perception’, each having the Fourth Jhana as its ‘result’, as in the formula stated in § 501.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.135", "t": "In the pursuit of the Higher Ideal (lokuttaravipako).<br>I. The First Path. The Twenty Great Methods."}, {"st": "1.9.136", "t": "Rapt Meditation.<br><i>(i.) The Four Modes of Progress in Purification.<\/i><br>(505) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.137", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana... [continue as in § 277] progress thereto being difficult and intuition sluggish — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.138", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is painful, intuition wherein is sluggish, and which is Empty — then there is contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.139", "t": "(506) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.140", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation) whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana... [continue as in § 277] progress thereto being difficult and intuition sluggish — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.141", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is painful, intuition wherein is sluggish, and which is Signless..."}, {"st": "1.9.142", "t": "[or] (507) <i>(repeating all the foregoing)<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.143", "t": "which is Aimless — then there is contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.144", "t": "(508) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.145", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, suppressing theworking of conception and of thought discursive, enters into and abides in the Second Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.9.146", "t": "[or]... in the Third Jhana...<br>[or]... in the Fourth Jhana...<br>[or]... <i>[continue in the same ivay for fivefold Jhana]<\/i>... progress whereto is painful and intuition sluggish, then this constitutes good (karma)."}, {"st": "1.9.147", "t": "But when, as the result <i>[of this or that Jhana the corresponding Jhana is attained]<\/i> progress whereto is painful, intuition wherein is sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.148", "t": "and which is Empty,<br>Signless,<br>[or] Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.149", "t": "then the contact... the balance that arises — these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.150", "t": "<i>(509) Repeat the two foregoing sections, substituting in order the three remaining Modes of Progress: ‘progress whereto is painful, but intuition quick’, ‘progress whereto is easy, but intuition sluggish’, and ‘progress whereto is easy, and intuition quick’ (§§ 176-180).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.151", "t": "<i>(ii.) The Notion of Emptiness applied to Purification (suddhika-sunnatam).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.152", "t": "(510) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.153", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana... which is Empty — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.154", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... which is Empty..."}, {"st": "1.9.155", "t": "[or] (511) which is Signless...<br>[or] (512) which is Aimless — then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.156", "t": "(513)<i> Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhdnas on the Fourfold System, and of all the J lianas on the Fivefold System,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.157", "t": "<i>(iii.) The Four Modes of Progress taken in connexion with the Notion of Emptiness (sunnata-patipada).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.158", "t": "<i>[The First Mode.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.159", "t": "(514) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.160", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana.. progress whereto is painful and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.161", "t": "and which is Empty,"}, {"st": "1.9.162", "t": "then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.163", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is painful and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.164", "t": "and which is Empty...<br>... [or] (515) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (516) which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.165", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.166", "t": "<i>(517) Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhdnas on the Fourfold System, and of all the Jhdnas on the Fivefold System.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.167", "t": "<i>[The Second, Third and Fourth Modes.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.168", "t": "(518) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.169", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.9.170", "t": "— progress whereto is painful, but intuition quick,<br>and which is Empty...<br>— progress whereto is easy, but intuition sluggish,<br>and which is Empty...<br>— progress whereto is easy and intuition quick,<br>and which is Empty..."}, {"st": "1.9.171", "t": "<i>[Repeat, substituting each of the remaining Jhdnas in the case of each of the three Modes]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.172", "t": "... then these constitute good (karma)."}, {"st": "1.9.173", "t": "But when, as the result [of this or that Jhana in any the three Modes], he enters into and abides in any of the Jhanas taken in order, which is in any of the three Modes,"}, {"st": "1.9.174", "t": "and which is Empty...<br>... [or] which is Signless...<br>... [or] which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.175", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.176", "t": "<i>(iv.) The Notion of Aimlessness applied to Purification (suddhika-appanihitam).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.177", "t": "(519) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.178", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, he enters into and abides in the First Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.9.179", "t": "which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.180", "t": "then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.181", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.9.182", "t": "... which is Aimless...<br>... [or] (520) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (521) which is Empty,"}, {"st": "1.9.183", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.184", "t": "(522) <i>Repeat in the case of each q/ the remaining Jhanas on the Fourfold System, and of all the Jhanas on the Fivefold System,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.185", "t": "<i>(v.) The Four Modes of Progress taken in connexion with the Notion of Aimlessness (appanihita-patipada).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.186", "t": "(523) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.187", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.188", "t": "and which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.189", "t": "then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.190", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the first Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.191", "t": "and which is Aimless...<br>... [or] (524) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (525) which is Empty,"}, {"st": "1.9.192", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.193", "t": "(526) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.194", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation) whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, suppressing the working of conception and of thought discursive, he enters into and abides in the Second Jhana, or into <i>[any of the remaining Jhanas, both on the Fourfold and the Fivefold System]<\/i>... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.195", "t": "and which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.196", "t": "then this constitutes good (karma)."}, {"st": "1.9.197", "t": "But when, as the result<i> [of this or that Jhana the corresponding Jhana is attained]<\/i>^ progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.198", "t": "and which is Aimless,<br>[or] which is Signless,<br>[or] which is Empty,"}, {"st": "1.9.199", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.200", "t": "(527)<i> The Second, Third and Fourth Modes are now substituted in turn, as teas done in § 518.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.201", "t": "The Remaining Nineteen Great Methods."}, {"st": "1.9.202", "t": "(528) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.203", "t": "<i>Here follow nineteen concepts, each of which can he substituted for ‘the Jhana of the Higher Ideal’ in the preceding exercises (§§ 505-528), the exercise to which they are actually applied in this paragraph being the ‘Modes of Progress in Purification’ given in §§ 505-509. These nineteen concepts are enumerated in § 358.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.204", "t": "<i>(vi.) The Modes of Progress in Purification taken in connexion with the Dominant Influence of Desire (chandadhipateyya-suddhika-patipada).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.205", "t": "(529) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.206", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.207", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish."}, {"st": "1.9.208", "t": "— which is Empty...<br>... [or] (530) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (531) which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.209", "t": "and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.210", "t": "(532)<i> Repeat in the case of each of the remaining Jhdnas on both systems.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.211", "t": "(533)<i> Repeat in the case of each of the three remaining ‘Modes of Progress’ applied to each Jhdna.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.212", "t": "<i>(vii.) The Notion of Emptiness applied to Purification, and taken in connexion with Desire as the Dominant Influence (chandadhipateyyam suddhika-sunnatam).<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.213", "t": "(534) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.214", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... which is Empty, and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.215", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana..."}, {"st": "1.9.216", "t": "which is Empty...<br>... [or] (535) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (536) which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.217", "t": "and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.218", "t": "(537) <i>Repeat in the case oj each of the remaining Jhdnas on both systems.<\/i><br><i>(viii.)<\/i><br>(538) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.219", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, which is Empty, and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.220", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish,"}, {"st": "1.9.221", "t": "— which is Empty...<br>... [or] (539) which is Signless...<br>... [or] (540) which is Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.222", "t": "and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.223", "t": "(541)<i> Repeat in the case of each remaining Jhana as passim,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.224", "t": "(542)<i> Repeat in the case of each of the three remaining ‘Modes of Progress’ applied to each Jhana,<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.225", "t": "<i>(ix.)<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.226", "t": "[543-546]<i> This group is identical tvith group vii. [§§ 534-537], except that the concept Aimless takes the place oj the concept Empty, and conversely.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.227", "t": "<i>(X.)<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.228", "t": "[547-551] <i>This group is identical with group viii. [§§ 538-542], except that the concept Aimless takes the place of the concept Empty, and conversely.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.229", "t": "The Remaining Nineteen Great Methods."}, {"st": "1.9.230", "t": "(552) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.231", "t": "When he cultivates the Path of the Higher Ideal, the Advance in Mindfulness toward the Higher Ideal, the System of Eight Efforts toward the Higher Ideal, etc. [continue as in § 358, doum to ‘Thought relating to the Higher Ideal’], whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the First Stage, he has put away views and opinions, and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, and the dominant influence in which is Desire — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.232", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, which is"}, {"st": "1.9.233", "t": "Empty,<br>or Signless,<br>or Aimless,"}, {"st": "1.9.234", "t": "and the dominant influence in which is"}, {"st": "1.9.235", "t": "Desire,<br>or Energy,<br>or a Thought,<br>or Investigation,"}, {"st": "1.9.236", "t": "then there is contact... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.237", "t": "[Here ends] Result in the First Path.<br>II. — IV. The Kemaining Three Paths. Cf. §§ 362-364a.<br>(553) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.238", "t": "When he cultivates the Jhana of the Higher Ideal (the rapt meditation), whereby there is a going forth and onward, making for the undoing of rebirth, and when, that he may attain to the Second Stage, he has diminished the strength of sensual passions and of malice..."}, {"st": "1.9.239", "t": "[or] when, that he may attain to the Third Stage, he has put away the entire residuum of sensual passions and of malice..."}, {"st": "1.9.240", "t": "[or] when, that he may attain to the Fourth Stage, he has put away absolutely and entirely all passion for Form, all passion for the Formless, all conceit, excitement, and ignorance — and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, then there is contact... the faculty of knowledge made perfect... balance."}, {"st": "1.9.241", "t": "Now these... are states that are good."}, {"st": "1.9.242", "t": "But when, as the result of just this good Jhana having been wrought, having been cultivated in pursuit of the Higher Ideal, he, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... progress whereto is difficult and intuition sluggish, and which is Empty, then there is contact... the faculty of one whose knowledge is made perfect... balance. And these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.243", "t": "(554)<i> Question and answer on ‘contact’ as above passim.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.244", "t": "(555) What on that occasion is the faculty of one whose knowledge is made perfect (annatavindriyam)?"}, {"st": "1.9.245", "t": "The perfected knowledge, the science, the understanding of the doctrines of those whose knowledge is made perfect, their searching, research, searching the Truth; discernment, discrimination, differentiation; erudition, proficiency, subtlety; criticism, reflection, analysis; breadth, sagacity, leading; insight, intelligence, incitement; wisdom as faculty and as power; wisdom as a sword, as a height, as light, as glory, as splendour, as a precious stone; the absence of dulness, searching the Truth, right views, that searching the truth which is a factor in the Great Awakening; a Path-component, contained in the Path — this is on that occasion the faculty of one whose knowledge is made perfect."}, {"st": "1.9.246", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.247", "t": "[Here ends] result in connexion with the Higher Ideal.<br>B. Bad Karma.<br>(a) The Five Modes of Sense-Cognition.<br>(556) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.248", "t": "When, as the result of bad karma having been wrought, having been stored up,<br>visual cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness and having as its object a sight...<br>auditory cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness and having as its object a sound...<br>olfactory cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness and having as its object a smell...<br>gustatory cognition has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness and having as its object a taste...<br>cognition of body has arisen, accompanied by distress and having as its object something tangible,<br>then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.249", "t": "contact, thinking,<br>feeling, thought,<br>perception, distress,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.250", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>distress,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.251", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.252", "t": "(557)<i> Question and answer on ‘contact’ as above passim.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.253", "t": "(558) What on that occasion is feeling?"}, {"st": "1.9.254", "t": "The bodily pain, the bodily distress which, on that occasion, is born of contact with the appropriate element of cognition of body; the painful, distressful sensation which is born of contact with the body; the painful, distressful feeling which is born of contact with the body — this is the feeling that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.255", "t": "(559) What on that occasion is distress?"}, {"st": "1.9.256", "t": "The bodily pain, the bodily distress which, on that occasion, is the painful, distressful sensation born of contact with the body; the painful, distressful feeling born of contact with the body — this is the distress that there then is."}, {"st": "1.9.257", "t": "(560) What on that occasion is the faculty of distress?"}, {"st": "1.9.258", "t": "<i>Answer as in § 559.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.259", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.260", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.261", "t": "Now at that time"}, {"st": "1.9.262", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are three,<br>contact,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.263", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 58.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.264", "t": "the faculty of ideation counts as a single factor,<br>the element of the cognition of body counts as a single factor,<br>the sphere of [mental] states counts as a single factor,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.9.265", "t": "<i>[Continue as in §§ 58-61.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.266", "t": "(561) What on that occasion is the skandha of syntheses?"}, {"st": "1.9.267", "t": "Contact, discursive thought,<br>thinking, self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.268", "t": "conception, the faculty of vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.269", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.9.270", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 63, etc.]<\/i><br>(b) The Element of Ideation.<br>(562) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.271", "t": "When, as the result of bad karma having been wrought, having been stored up, an element of ideation has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.272", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.273", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.274", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.275", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.276", "t": "<i>This including the description of the appropriate skandha of syntheses (563), is identical with the corresponding Summary, §§ 467a, 468.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.277", "t": "(c) The Element of Representative Intellection."}, {"st": "1.9.278", "t": "(564) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.9.279", "t": "When, as the result of bad karma having been wrought, having been stored up, an element of representative intellection has arisen, accompanied by disinterestedness, and having as its object a sight, etc. or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.9.280", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.9.281", "t": "the faculties of<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.9.282", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.9.283", "t": "<i>[Continue as in §§ 485-496.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.284", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.9.285", "t": "[564a, 565] <i>This including the description of the appropriate skandha of syntheses, is identical with §§ 496a, 497.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.9.286", "t": "[Here end] the Indeterminates which are the result of Bad [Karma]."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER II. ACTION-THOUGHTS"}, {"st": "1.10.1", "t": "A. In connexion with the Sensuous Universe.<br>(a) On occasion of Ideation (kamavacara-kiriya).<br>(566) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.2", "t": "When an element of ideation of the kind termed kiriya has arisen, which is neither good nor bad nor the result of karma, which is accompanied by disinterestedness, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.10.3", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.10.4", "t": "the faculties of:<br>ideation,<br>disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.10.5", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incor; -eal, causally in duced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.6", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.10.7", "t": "Now, at that time"}, {"st": "1.10.8", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are three,<br>contact,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.10.9", "t": "<i>(567) The skandha of syntheses (as well as the remainder of the foregoing summary) is identical with the corresponding passages in Chapter I. viz. §§ 467a, 468.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.10", "t": "(b) On occasion of Representative Intellection."}, {"st": "1.10.11", "t": "(568) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.12", "t": "When an element of representative intellection of the kind termed kiriya has arisen, which is neither good nor bad nor the result of karma, which is accompanied by happiness, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.10.13", "t": "contact, conception,<br>feeling, discursive thought,<br>perception, joy,<br>thinking, ease,<br>thought, self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.10.14", "t": "the faculties of<br>energy, ideation,<br>concentration, happiness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.10.15", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.16", "t": "(569) <i>Question and answer on ‘contact’ as above passim.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.17", "t": "(570) What on that occasion is self-collectedness?"}, {"st": "1.10.18", "t": "The stability, solidity, absorbed steadfastness of thought which on that occasion is the absence of distraction, balance, imperturbed mental procedure, quiet, the faculty and the power of concentration — this is the self-collectedness that there then is."}, {"st": "1.10.19", "t": "(571) What on that occasion is the faculty of energy... (572) of concentration?"}, {"st": "1.10.20", "t": "<i>Answers as in § 13 and § 570 respectively.<\/i><br>[Summary.]<br>Now at that time"}, {"st": "1.10.21", "t": "the skandhas are four,<br>the spheres are two,<br>the elements are two,<br>the nutriments are three,<br>the faculties are five,<br>contact,<br>etc."}, {"st": "1.10.22", "t": "<i>[Continue as in § 482a.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.23", "t": "(573) What on that occasion is the skandha of syn theses?"}, {"st": "1.10.24", "t": "Contact,<br>thinking,<br>conception,<br>discursive thought,<br>joy,<br>self-collectedness,"}, {"st": "1.10.25", "t": "the faculties of:<br>energy,<br>concentration,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.10.26", "t": "Or whatever other incorporeal, causally induced states there are on that occasion, exclusive of the skandhas of feeling, perception, and intellect — these are the skandha of syntheses."}, {"st": "1.10.27", "t": "(574) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.28", "t": "When an element of representative intellection of the kind termed kiriya has arisen, which is neither good, nor bad, nor the result of karma, which is accompanied by disinterestedness, and which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not, then there is"}, {"st": "1.10.29", "t": "contact, thought,<br>feeling, conception,<br>perception, discursive thought,<br>thinking, disinterestedness,<br>self-collectedness;"}, {"st": "1.10.30", "t": "the faculties of<br>energy, ideation,<br>concentration, disinterestedness,<br>vitality."}, {"st": "1.10.31", "t": "Now these, or whatever other incorporeal, causally in duced states there are on that occasion — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.32", "t": "[Summary.]"}, {"st": "1.10.33", "t": "<i>Identical with 572a<\/i>."}, {"st": "1.10.34", "t": "(575)<i> The skandha of syntheses is identical with that in § 573, hut ‘joy’ must be omitted.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.35", "t": "(576) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.36", "t": "When an element of representative intellection of the kind termed kiriya has arisen that is neither good, nor bad, nor the result of karma —"}, {"st": "1.10.37", "t": "i. which is accompanied by happiness and associated with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.10.38", "t": "ii. which is accompanied by happiness, associated with knowledge, and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.10.39", "t": "iii. which is accom panied by happiness and disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.10.40", "t": "iv. which is accompanied by happiness, disconnected with knowledge and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.10.41", "t": "v. which is accompanied by disinterestedness and associ ated with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.10.42", "t": "vi. which is accompanied by disinterestedness, associated with knowledge and prompted by a conscious motive..."}, {"st": "1.10.43", "t": "vii. which is accompanied by disinterestedness and ‘disconnected with knowledge..."}, {"st": "1.10.44", "t": "viii. which is accompanied by disinterestedness, discon nected with knowledge and prompted by a conscious motive"}, {"st": "1.10.45", "t": "— and, which has as its object a sight, a sound, a smell, a taste, something tangible, or what not — then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.46", "t": "That absence of lust which is the root of the indeterminate...<br>that absence of hate which is the root of the inde terminate...<br>that absence of dulness which is the root of the inde terminate..."}, {"st": "1.10.47", "t": "these are states that are indeterminate.<br>B. In connexion with the Universe of Form (rupavacara-kiriya).<br>(577) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.48", "t": "When he cultivates rapt meditation in connexion with the universe of Form, and of the kind termed kiriya which is neither good, nor bad, nor the result of karma, and which is concerned with easeful living under present conditions — and so, aloof from sensuous appetites, aloof from evil ideas, by earth-gazing, enters into and abides in the First Jhana... then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.49", "t": "(578) <i>Repeat in the case of each remaining Jhana on the Fourfold f and of those on the Fivefold System.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.50", "t": "C. In connexion with the Universe of the Formless (arupavacara-kiriya)."}, {"st": "1.10.51", "t": "(579) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "1.10.52", "t": "When he cultivates rapt meditation in connexion with the universe of the Formless, of the kind termed kiriya, which is neither good, nor bad, nor the result of karma, and is concerned with easeful living under present conditions — and when, by passing wholly beyond all consciousness of form, by the dying out of the consciousness of sensory reaction, by turning the attention from any consciousness of the manifold, he enters into and abides in that rapt meditation which is accompanied by the consciousness of a sphere of unbounded space — even the Fourth Jhana, to gain which all sense of ease must have been put away, etc.... then there is contact... balance. Now these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.53", "t": "[580-582]<i> Here follow, with the same opening formula as in the foregoing answer, the three remaining ‘Jhanas con nected with Formless Existence’. See §§ 266-268.<\/i>"}, {"st": "1.10.54", "t": "That absence of lust which is the root of the indeterminate...<br>that absence of hate which is the root of the indeterminate...<br>that absence of dulness which is the root of the indeterminate..."}, {"st": "1.10.55", "t": "these... are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "1.10.56", "t": "[Here ends] the Division on the Genesis of Thoughts."}, {"sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK II. FORM"}, {"st": "2.1", "t": "Introductory"}, {"st": "2.2", "t": "(583) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "2.3", "t": "The results of good and bad states taking effect in the universe of sense, in that of form, in that of the formless or in [the life] which is Unincluded, and as connected with the skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses, and intellect as well as those states known as kiriya which are neither good, nor bad, nor the result of karma; all form, moreover; and [finally] Uncompounded Element — these are states that are indeterminate."}, {"st": "2.4", "t": "(684) In this connexion what is ‘all form’ (sabbam rupam)?"}, {"st": "2.5", "t": "The four great phenomena and that form which is derived from the four great phenomena — this is what is called ‘all form’."}, {"st": "2.6", "t": "[584-594]<i> Here follows the Matika, or table of contents of the following analysis of Form, considered under quantitative categories — the usual Buddhist method. That is to say, Form is considered, first, under a number of single, uncorrelated qualities, then under dichotomized qualities, then under qualities which, taken singly, give inclusion, inclusion under the opposite, or exclusion from both; or which, taken in pairs, a ford three combinations. We then get pairs of qualities taken together, affording four combinations. After that comes consideration of Form under more inductive classifications, e.g. the four elements and, fifthly, their derivatives, and so on, as given below.<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER I. EXPOSITION OF FORM UNDER SINGLE CONCEPTS (595)"}, {"st": "2.1.1", "t": "All form is that which is"}, {"st": "2.1.2", "t": "not a cause,<br>not the concomitant of a cause,<br>disconnected with cause,<br>conditioned,<br>compound,<br>endowed with form,<br>mundane,<br>co-Intoxicant,"}, {"st": "2.1.3", "t": "favourable to:<br>the Fetters,<br>the Ties,<br>the Floods,<br>the Bonds,<br>the Hindrances;"}, {"st": "2.1.4", "t": "infected,<br>favourable to grasping,<br>belonging to corruption,<br>indeterminate,<br>void of idea,<br>neither feeling, nor perception, nor synthesis,<br>disconnected with thought,<br>neither moral result, nor productive of moral result,<br>uncorrupted yet belonging to corruption,<br>not that ‘where conception works and thought discursive’,<br>not that ‘wherein is no working of conception, but only of thought discursive’,<br>void of ‘the working of conception and of thought discursive’,<br>not ‘accompanied by joy’,<br>not ‘accompanied by ease’,<br>not ‘accompanied by disinterestedness’,<br>not something capable of being got rid of either by insight or by cultivation,<br>not that the cause of which may be got rid of either by insight or by cultivation,<br>neither tending to, nor away from, the accumulation involving re-birth,<br>belonging neither to studentship nor to that which is beyond studentship,<br>limited,<br>related to the universe of sense,<br>not related to the universe of form,<br>nor to that of the formless,<br>included,<br>not of the Unincluded,<br>not something entailing inevitable retribution,<br>unavailing for (ethical) guidance,<br>cognizable when apparent(GLNOTE::) by the six modes of cognition,<br>impermanent,<br>subject to decay."}, {"st": "2.1.5", "t": "Such is the category of Form considered by way of single attributes."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER II. THE CATEGORY OF FORM CONSIDERED BY WAY OF DUAL ATTRIBUTES"}, {"st": "2.2.1", "t": "(596) What is that form which is derived?"}, {"st": "2.2.2", "t": "The sphere of:<br>vision, smell,<br>hearing, taste,<br>bodysensibility;"}, {"st": "2.2.3", "t": "the sphere of:<br>sights, odours,<br>sounds, tastes;"}, {"st": "2.2.4", "t": "the faculties of:<br>femininity, masculinity,<br>vitality;"}, {"st": "2.2.5", "t": "intimation:<br>by act,<br>by speech;"}, {"st": "2.2.6", "t": "the element of space;"}, {"st": "2.2.7", "t": "buoyancy, &gt;<br>plasticity, &gt;<br>wieldiness, &gt;<br>integration, &gt; of form<br>maintenance, &gt;<br>decay, &gt;<br>impermanence, &gt;"}, {"st": "2.2.8", "t": "solid nutriment."}, {"st": "2.2.9", "t": "(597) What is that form which is the sphere of vision (cakkhayatanam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.10", "t": "The eye, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting"}, {"st": "2.2.11", "t": "— by which eye, invisible and reacting, one has seen, sees, will, or may see form that is visible and impingeing —"}, {"st": "2.2.12", "t": "this that is sight,<br>the sphere of sight,<br>the element of vision,<br>the faculty of vision,"}, {"st": "2.2.13", "t": "this that is:"}, {"st": "2.2.14", "t": "‘a world’,<br>‘a door’,<br>‘an ocean’,<br>‘lucent’,<br>‘a field’,<br>‘a basis’,<br>‘a guide’,<br>‘guidance’,<br>the ‘hither shore’,<br>an ‘empty village’"}, {"st": "2.2.15", "t": "— this is that form which constitutes the sphere of vision."}, {"st": "2.2.16", "t": "(598) What is that form which is the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.17", "t": "The eye, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting, and against which eye, invisible and reacting, form that is visible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge"}, {"st": "2.2.18", "t": "— this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.19", "t": "sight,<br>the sphere of sight,<br>the constituent element of sight,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.20", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 597]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.21", "t": "(599) What is that form which is the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.22", "t": "The eye, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting, which eye, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on form that is visible and impingeing"}, {"st": "2.2.23", "t": "— this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.24", "t": "sight,<br>the sphere of sight,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.25", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 597].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.26", "t": "(600) What is that form which is the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.27", "t": "The eye, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting, (i.) depending on which eye, in consequence of some visible form, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.28", "t": "visual contact;..."}, {"st": "2.2.29", "t": "(ii.) and depending on which eye, in consequence of some visible form, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise (born of that visual contact)"}, {"st": "2.2.30", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or iii.] a perception...<br>[or iv.] thinking...<br>[or v.] a visual cognition..."}, {"st": "2.2.31", "t": "[further, vi.] depending on which eye, and having a visible form as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.32", "t": "visual contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.33", "t": "(vii.) and depending on which eye, and having a visible form as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that visual contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.34", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or viii.] a perception...<br>[or ix.] thinking...<br>[or x.] visual cognition — this that is sight, the sphere of sight, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.35", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 597]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.36", "t": "[601-604] What is that form which is the sphere of hearing?"}, {"st": "2.2.37", "t": "The ear, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting, —"}, {"st": "2.2.38", "t": "a. by which ear, invisible and reacting, one has heard, hears, will, or may hear sound that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.39", "t": "b. against which ear, invisible and reacting, sound that is invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge; —"}, {"st": "2.2.40", "t": "c. which ear, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on sound that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.41", "t": "d. depending on which ear, in consequence of a sound, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise:"}, {"st": "2.2.42", "t": "auditory contact;..."}, {"st": "2.2.43", "t": "and, depending on which ear, in consequence of a sound, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.44", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] auditory cognition; —"}, {"st": "2.2.45", "t": "[further] depending on which ear, and having a sound as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.46", "t": "auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.47", "t": "and, depending on which ear, and having a sound as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.48", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] auditory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.49", "t": "— this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.50", "t": "hearing,<br>the sphere of hearing,<br>the constituent element of hearing,<br>the faculty of hearing,"}, {"st": "2.2.51", "t": "this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.52", "t": "‘a world’,<br>‘a door’,<br>‘an ocean’,<br>‘lucent’,<br>‘a field’,<br>‘a basis’,<br>‘the hither shore’,<br>‘an empty village’"}, {"st": "2.2.53", "t": "— this is that form which is the sphere of hearing."}, {"st": "2.2.54", "t": "[605-608] What is that form which is the sphere of smell?"}, {"st": "2.2.55", "t": "The nose, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting, —"}, {"st": "2.2.56", "t": "a. by which nose, invisible and reacting, one has smelt, smells, will, or may smell odour that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.57", "t": "b. against which nose, invisible and reacting, odour that is invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge; —"}, {"st": "2.2.58", "t": "c. which nose, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on odour that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.59", "t": "d. depending on which nose, in consequence of an odour... depending on which nose, and having an odour as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise:"}, {"st": "2.2.60", "t": "olfactory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.61", "t": "and, depending on which nose, in consequence of an odour... depending on which nose, and having an odour as its object, there has arisen, arises, will or may arise, born of that olfactory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.62", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] olfactory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.63", "t": "— this that is smell, the sphere, the constituent element, the faculty, of smell, this that is ‘a world’, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.64", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 604]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.65", "t": "[609-612] What is that form which is the sphere of taste?"}, {"st": "2.2.66", "t": "The tongue, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting; —"}, {"st": "2.2.67", "t": "by which tongue, invisible and reacting, one has tasted, tastes, will, or may taste sapids that are invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.68", "t": "against which tongue, invisible and reacting, sapids that are invisible and impingeing, have impinged, impinge, will, or may impinge; —"}, {"st": "2.2.69", "t": "which tongue, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on sapids that are invisibleand impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.70", "t": "depending on which tongue, in consequence of a sapid... depending on which tongue, and having a sapid as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise:"}, {"st": "2.2.71", "t": "gustatory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.72", "t": "and depending on which tongue, in consequence of a sapid... depending on which tongue, and having a sapid as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that gustatory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.73", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] gustatory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.74", "t": "— this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.75", "t": "taste,<br>the sphere,<br>the constituent element,<br>the faculty of taste,"}, {"st": "2.2.76", "t": "this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.77", "t": "‘a world’,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.78", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 604].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.79", "t": "[613-616] What is that form which is the sphere of body [-sensibility]?"}, {"st": "2.2.80", "t": "The body, that is to say the sentient organ, derived from the four Great Phenomena, forming part of the nature of the self, invisible and reacting; —"}, {"st": "2.2.81", "t": "a. by which body-sensibility, invisible and reacting, one has touched, touches, will, or may touch the tangible that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.82", "t": "b. against which body-sensibility, invisible and reacting, the tangible, which is invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge; —"}, {"st": "2.2.83", "t": "c. which bodysensibility, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on the tangible that is invisible and impingeing; —"}, {"st": "2.2.84", "t": "d. depending on which bodysensibility, in consequence of something tangible... depending on which bodysensibility, and having something tangible as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise:"}, {"st": "2.2.85", "t": "tactile contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.86", "t": "and depending on which bodysensibility, in consequence of something tangible... depending on which body-sensibility, and having something tangible as its object, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that tactile contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.87", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] tactile cognition; —"}, {"st": "2.2.88", "t": "this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.89", "t": "body sensibility,<br>the sphere,<br>constituent element,<br>faculty of bodysensibility,"}, {"st": "2.2.90", "t": "this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.91", "t": "‘a world’,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.92", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 604]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.93", "t": "(617) What is that form which is the sphere of [visible] form?"}, {"st": "2.2.94", "t": "The form which, derived from the great principles, is visible under the appearance of colour and produces impact —"}, {"st": "2.2.95", "t": "is blue,<br>yellow,<br>red,<br>white,<br>black,<br>crimson,<br>bronze,<br>green-coloured,<br>of the hue of the mangobud;"}, {"st": "2.2.96", "t": "is long,<br>short,<br>big,<br>little,<br>circular,<br>oval,<br>square,<br>hexagonal,<br>octagonal,<br>hekkaidecagonal;"}, {"st": "2.2.97", "t": "low,<br>high,<br>shady,<br>glowing,<br>light,<br>dim,<br>dull,<br>frosty,<br>smoky,<br>dusty;"}, {"st": "2.2.98", "t": "like in colour to the<br>disc of moon,<br>sun,<br>stars,"}, {"st": "2.2.99", "t": "a mirror,<br>a gem,<br>a shell,<br>a pearl,<br>a cat’s eye,"}, {"st": "2.2.100", "t": "gold or silver;"}, {"st": "2.2.101", "t": "or whatever other form there is which, derived from the four Great Phenomena, is visible and productive of impact — form which, visible and productive of impact, one has seen, sees, will, or may see with the eye that is invisible and reacting"}, {"st": "2.2.102", "t": "— this which is<br>visible form,<br>this which is"}, {"st": "2.2.103", "t": "the sphere of visible form,<br>the constituent element of visible form"}, {"st": "2.2.104", "t": "— this is that form which is the sphere of visible form."}, {"st": "2.2.105", "t": "(618) What is that form which is the sphere of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.2.106", "t": "That form which, derived from the Great Phenomena, is visible under the appearance of colour and produces impact... on which form, visible and productive of impact, the eye, invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge"}, {"st": "2.2.107", "t": "— this that is"}, {"st": "2.2.108", "t": "visible form,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.109", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 617]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.110", "t": "(619) What is that form which is the sphere of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.2.111", "t": "That form which, derived from the Great Phenomena, is visible under the appearance of colour and produces impact — which form, visible and producing impact, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on the eye that is invisible and reacting"}, {"st": "2.2.112", "t": "— this which is"}, {"st": "2.2.113", "t": "visible form,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.2.114", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 617]<\/i>."}, {"st": "2.2.115", "t": "(620) What is that form which is the sphere of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.2.116", "t": "That form which, derived from the four Great Phenomena, is visible and produces impact — in consequence of which form, and depending on the eye, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.117", "t": "visual contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.118", "t": "in consequence of which form and depending on the eye, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise, born of that visual contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.119", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] visual cognition..."}, {"st": "2.2.120", "t": "[further] having which visible form as its object, and depending on the eye there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.121", "t": "visual contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.122", "t": "and, having which visible form as its object, and depending on the eye, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.123", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] visual cognition..."}, {"st": "2.2.124", "t": "this which is"}, {"st": "2.2.125", "t": "visible form,"}, {"st": "2.2.126", "t": "the sphere,<br>the constituent element of visible form"}, {"st": "2.2.127", "t": "— this is that form which is the sphere of visible form."}, {"st": "2.2.128", "t": "(621) What is that form which is the sphere of sound?"}, {"st": "2.2.129", "t": "That sound which is derived from the four Great Phenomena, is invisible and produces impact, such as"}, {"st": "2.2.130", "t": "the sound<br>of drums,<br>of tabors,<br>of chank-shells,<br>of tom-toms,<br>of singing,<br>of music;"}, {"st": "2.2.131", "t": "clashing sounds,<br>manual sounds,"}, {"st": "2.2.132", "t": "the noise of people,"}, {"st": "2.2.133", "t": "the sound<br>of the concussion of substances,<br>of wind,<br>of water,"}, {"st": "2.2.134", "t": "sounds human and other than human,"}, {"st": "2.2.135", "t": "or whatever other sound there is, derived from the Great Phenomena, invisible and producing impact — such a sound, invisible and producing impact, as, by the ear, invisible and reacting, one has heard, hears, will, or may hear..."}, {"st": "2.2.136", "t": "(622)... and on which sound, invisible and producing impact, the ear, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge..."}, {"st": "2.2.137", "t": "(623)... which sound, invisible and producing impact, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on the ear that is invisible and reacting..."}, {"st": "2.2.138", "t": "(624)... in consequence of which sound and depending on the ear, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.139", "t": "auditory contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.140", "t": "... and... born of that auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.141", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] auditory cognition..."}, {"st": "2.2.142", "t": "... [further] having a sound as its object and depending on the ear, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise,"}, {"st": "2.2.143", "t": "auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.144", "t": "... and... born of that auditory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.145", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] auditory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.146", "t": "this that is sound, the sphere and constituent element of sound — this is that form which is the sphere of sound."}, {"st": "2.2.147", "t": "(625) What is that form which is the sphere of odour?"}, {"st": "2.2.148", "t": "That odour which is derived from the four Great Phenomena, is invisible and produces impact, such as"}, {"st": "2.2.149", "t": "the odour of roots,<br>sap,<br>bark,<br>leaves,<br>Flowers,<br>fruit;"}, {"st": "2.2.150", "t": "verminous odours,<br>putrid odours,<br>pleasant and unpleasant odours,"}, {"st": "2.2.151", "t": "or whatever other odour there is, derived from the four Great Phenomena, invisible and producing impact; such an odour, invisible and producing impact, as one has smelt, smells, will, or may smell with the nose, that is invisible and impingeing..."}, {"st": "2.2.152", "t": "(626)... on which odour, invisible and producing impact, the nose, invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge..."}, {"st": "2.2.153", "t": "(627)... such an odour, invisible and producing impact, as has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on the nose, invisible and reacting..."}, {"st": "2.2.154", "t": "(628)... in consequence of which odour and depending on the nose, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.155", "t": "olfactory contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.156", "t": "and... born of that olfactory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.157", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] olfactory cognition..."}, {"st": "2.2.158", "t": "... [further] having an odour as its object and depending on the nose, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.159", "t": "olfactory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.160", "t": "... and... born of that olfactory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.161", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] olfactory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.162", "t": "this that is odour, the sphere and constituent element of odour — this is that form which is the sphere of odours."}, {"st": "2.2.163", "t": "(629) What is that form which is the sphere of taste?"}, {"st": "2.2.164", "t": "That taste which is derived from the four Great Phenomena, is invisible and produces impact, such as"}, {"st": "2.2.165", "t": "the taste of roots,<br>stems,<br>bark,<br>leaves,<br>flowers,<br>fruits,"}, {"st": "2.2.166", "t": "of sour,<br>sweet,<br>bitter,<br>pungent,<br>saline,<br>alkaline,<br>acrid,<br>astringent,<br>nice and nauseous sapids,"}, {"st": "2.2.167", "t": "or whatever other taste there is, derived from the four Great Phenomena, invisible and producing impact — such tastes, invisible and producing impact, as with the tongue, invisible and reacting, one has tasted, tastes, will, or may taste..."}, {"st": "2.2.168", "t": "(630)... against which taste, invisible and producing impact, the tongue, invisible and impingeing, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge..."}, {"st": "2.2.169", "t": "(631)... a taste which, invisible and producing impact, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge on the tongue, invisible and reacting..."}, {"st": "2.2.170", "t": "(632)... in consequence of which taste and depending •on the tongue, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.171", "t": "gustatory contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.172", "t": "... and... born of that gustatory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.173", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] gustatory cognition,"}, {"st": "2.2.174", "t": "[further] having a taste as its object and depending on the tongue, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.175", "t": "gustatory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.176", "t": "... and... born of that gustatory contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.177", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] gustatory cognition;"}, {"st": "2.2.178", "t": "this that is taste, the sphere and constituent element of taste — this is that form which is the sphere of taste."}, {"st": "2.2.179", "t": "(633) What is that form which is femininity (itthindriyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.180", "t": "That which is of the female, feminine in appearance, feminine in characteristics, in occupation, in deportment, feminine in condition and being — this is that form which constitutes femininity."}, {"st": "2.2.181", "t": "(634) What is that form which is masculinity (purisindriyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.182", "t": "That which is of the male, masculine in appearance, masculine in characteristics, in occupation, in deportment, masculine in condition and being — this is that form which constitutes masculinity."}, {"st": "2.2.183", "t": "(635) What is that form which is vitality (jivitindriyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.184", "t": "The persistence of these corporeal states, their subsistence, their going on, their being kept going on, their progress, continuance, preservation, life, life as faculty — this is that form which is vitality."}, {"st": "2.2.185", "t": "(636) What is that form which is bodily intimation (kayavinnatti)?"}, {"st": "2.2.186", "t": "That tension, that intentness, that state of making the body tense, in response to a thought, whether good, bad, or indeterminate, on the part of one who advances, or recedes, or fixes the gaze, or glances around, or retracts an arm, or stretches it forth — the intimation, the making known, the state of having made known — this is that form which constitutes bodily intimation."}, {"st": "2.2.187", "t": "(637) What is that form which is intimation by language (vacivinnatti)?"}, {"st": "2.2.188", "t": "That speech, voice, enunciation, utterance, noise, making noises, language as articulate speech, which expresses a thought whether good, bad, or indeterminate — this is called language."}, {"st": "2.2.189", "t": "And that intimation, that making known, the state of having made known by language — this is that form which constitutes intimation by language."}, {"st": "2.2.190", "t": "(638) What is that form which is the element of space (akasa-dhatu)?"}, {"st": "2.2.191", "t": "That which is space and belongs to space, is sky and belongs to sky, is vacuum and belongs to vacuum, and is not in contact’ with the four Great Phenomena — this is that form which is the element of space."}, {"st": "2.2.192", "t": "(639) What is that form which is lightness of form (rupassa lahuta)?"}, {"st": "2.2.193", "t": "That lightness of form which is its capacity for changing easily, its freedom from sluggishness and inertia — this is that form which is lightness of form."}, {"st": "2.2.194", "t": "(640) What is that form which is plasticity of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.195", "t": "That plasticity of form which is its softness, smoothness, non-rigidity — this is that form which is plasticity of form."}, {"st": "2.2.196", "t": "(641) What is that form which is wieldiness of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.197", "t": "That wieldiness of form which is its serviceableness, its workable condition — this is that form which is wieldiness of form."}, {"st": "2.2.198", "t": "(642) What is that form which is the integration (upacayo) of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.199", "t": "That which is accumulation of form is the integration of form — this is that form which is the integration of form."}, {"st": "2.2.200", "t": "(643) What is that form which is the subsistence of form (rupassa santati)?"}, {"st": "2.2.201", "t": "That which is integration of form is the subsistence of form. — This is that form which is the subsistence of form."}, {"st": "2.2.202", "t": "(644) What is that form which is the decay of form (rupassa jarata)?"}, {"st": "2.2.203", "t": "That decay of form which is ageing, decrepitude, hoariness, wrinkles, the shrinkage in length of days, the hypermaturity of faculties — this is that form which is the decay of form."}, {"st": "2.2.204", "t": "(645) What is that form which is the impermanence of form (rupassa aniccata)?"}, {"st": "2.2.205", "t": "The destruction, disease, breaking-up, dissolution of form, the impermanence which is decline — this is that form which is the impermanence of form."}, {"st": "2.2.206", "t": "(646) What is that form which is bodily (solid) nutriment (kabalinkaro aharo)?"}, {"st": "2.2.207", "t": "Boiled rice, sour gruel, flour, fish, flesh, milk, curds, butter, cheese, tila-oil, cane-syrup, or whatever else there is in whatever region that by living beings may be eaten, chewed, swallowed, digested into the juice by which living beings are kept alive — this is that form which is bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.2.208", "t": "[All] this is form which is derived."}, {"st": "2.2.209", "t": "[End of] the Section on Derivatives. First Portion for Eecitation in the Division on Form."}, {"st": "2.2.210", "t": "<i>[‘There is form which is not derived’ (no upada)].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.211", "t": "(647) What is that form which is not derived?"}, {"st": "2.2.212", "t": "The sphere of the tangible, the fluid element — this is that form which is not derived."}, {"st": "2.2.213", "t": "(648) What is that form which is the sphere of the tangible (photthabbayatanam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.214", "t": "The earthy (solid) element,<br>the lambent (calorific) element,<br>the gaseous (aerial) element;"}, {"st": "2.2.215", "t": "the hard and the soft;<br>the smooth and the rough;"}, {"st": "2.2.216", "t": "pleasant (easeful) contact,<br>painful contact;"}, {"st": "2.2.217", "t": "the heavy and the light"}, {"st": "2.2.218", "t": "— such a tangible, invisible and producing impact, as, with the body-sensibility, invisible and reacting, one has touched, touches, will, or may touch..."}, {"st": "2.2.219", "t": "(649)... against which tangible, invisible, and producing impact, the bodysensibility, invisible and reacting, has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge..."}, {"st": "2.2.220", "t": "(650)... such a tangible, invisible and producing impact, as has impinged, impinges, will, or may impinge against the body-sensibility, invisible and reacting..."}, {"st": "2.2.221", "t": "(651)... in consequence of which tangible and depending on the body-sensibility, there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.222", "t": "bodily contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.223", "t": "and... born of that bodily contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.224", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] cognition of body..."}, {"st": "2.2.225", "t": "[further,] having a tangible as its object and depending on the body (-sensibility), there has arisen, arises, will, or may arise"}, {"st": "2.2.226", "t": "bodily contact..."}, {"st": "2.2.227", "t": "and... born of that bodily contact,"}, {"st": "2.2.228", "t": "a feeling...<br>[or] a perception...<br>[or] thinking...<br>[or] cognition of body;"}, {"st": "2.2.229", "t": "this that is the tangible, the sphere and element of the tangible — this is that form which is the sphere of the tangible."}, {"st": "2.2.230", "t": "(652) What is that form which is the fluid (aqueous) element (apodhatu)?"}, {"st": "2.2.231", "t": "That which is fluid and belongs to fluid, that which is viscid and belongs to viscous, the cohesiveness of form — this is that form which is the fluid element."}, {"st": "2.2.232", "t": "[All] this is that form which is not derived."}, {"st": "2.2.233", "t": "(653) What is that form which is the issue of grasping (upadinnam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.234", "t": "The spheres of:<br>sight,<br>hearing,<br>smell,<br>taste,<br>body-sensibility,<br>femininity,<br>masculinity,<br>vitality,"}, {"st": "2.2.235", "t": "or whatever form there exists through karma having been wrought, whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.2.236", "t": "the spheres of:<br>visible forms,<br>odours,<br>tastes,<br>or the tangible;"}, {"st": "2.2.237", "t": "the element of space,<br>the fluid element,<br>the integration or the subsistence of form,"}, {"st": "2.2.238", "t": "or bodily nutriment<br>— this is that form which is the issue of grasping.<br>(654) What is that form which is not the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.2.239", "t": "The sphere of sound, bodily and vocal intimation, lightness, plasticity and wieldiness of form, decay and impermanence of form, or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought, whether it be in the sphere of visible forms, smells, tastes, or the tangible; the element of space or that of fluidity; the integration or the subsistence of form, or bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the issue of grasping."}, {"st": "2.2.240", "t": "(655) What is that form which is both the issue of grasping and favourable to grasping (upadinn ‘upadaniyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.241", "t": "The spheres of:<br>the five senses,<br>femininity,<br>masculinity and vitality,"}, {"st": "2.2.242", "t": "or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought, whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.2.243", "t": "the spheres of:<br>visible forms,<br>odours,<br>tastes or the tangible,"}, {"st": "2.2.244", "t": "in the elements of space or fluidity,<br>in the integration,"}, {"st": "2.2.245", "t": "or the subsistence of form<br>or in bodily nutriment"}, {"st": "2.2.246", "t": "— this is that form which is both the issue of grasping and favourable to grasping."}, {"st": "2.2.247", "t": "(656) What is that form which is not the issue of grasping, but is favourable to grasping (anupadinn’ upadaniyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.248", "t": "The sphere of:<br>sounds,<br>bodily and vocal intimation,<br>the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,<br>decay and impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.2.249", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought, whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.2.250", "t": "the sphere of<br>visible forms,<br>smells,<br>tastes,<br>the tangible,"}, {"st": "2.2.251", "t": "in the element of space or of fluidity,<br>in the integration,"}, {"st": "2.2.252", "t": "or the subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment"}, {"st": "2.2.253", "t": "— this is that form which is not the issue of grasping but is favourable to grasping."}, {"st": "2.2.254", "t": "(657) What is that form which is visible?<br>The sphere of visible forms — this is that form which is visible.<br>(658) What is that form which is invisible?"}, {"st": "2.2.255", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is invisible."}, {"st": "2.2.256", "t": "(659) What is that form which reacts and impinges (sappatigham)?"}, {"st": "2.2.257", "t": "The spheres of:<br>vision,<br>hearing,<br>smell,<br>taste,<br>body-sensibility;"}, {"st": "2.2.258", "t": "the spheres of:<br>visible forms,<br>sounds,<br>smells,<br>tastes,<br>tangibles"}, {"st": "2.2.259", "t": "— this is that form which reacts and impinges.<br>(660) What is that form which does not react or impinge?<br>Femininity... and bodily nutriment<br>— this is that form which does not react or impinge."}, {"st": "2.2.260", "t": "(661) What is that form which is faculty (indriyam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.261", "t": "The faculties (or personal potentialities) of:<br>vision,<br>hearing,<br>smell,<br>taste,<br>body-sensibility,<br>femininity,<br>masculinity,<br>vitality"}, {"st": "2.2.262", "t": "— this is that form which is faculty."}, {"st": "2.2.263", "t": "(662) What is that form which is not faculty?"}, {"st": "2.2.264", "t": "The spheres of visible form... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not faculty."}, {"st": "2.2.265", "t": "(663) What is that form which is Great Phenomenon (mahabhutam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.266", "t": "The sphere of the tangible and the element of fluidity — this is that form which is Great Phenomenon."}, {"st": "2.2.267", "t": "(664) What is that form which is not Great Phenomenon?"}, {"st": "2.2.268", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not Great Phenomenon."}, {"st": "2.2.269", "t": "(665) What is that form which is intimation (vinnatti)?<br>Bodily and vocal intimation — this is that form which is intimation.<br>(666) What is that form which is not intimation?"}, {"st": "2.2.270", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not intimation."}, {"st": "2.2.271", "t": "(667) What is that form which is sprung from thought (citta-samutthanam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.272", "t": "Bodily and vocal intimation, or whatever other form exists that is born of thought, caused by thought, has its source in thought, whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.2.273", "t": "the sphere of:<br>visible forms,<br>sounds,<br>odours,<br>tastes or tangibles,"}, {"st": "2.2.274", "t": "in the spatial,<br>or the fluid element,"}, {"st": "2.2.275", "t": "in the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,<br>integration<br>or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment"}, {"st": "2.2.276", "t": "— this is that form which is sprung from thought."}, {"st": "2.2.277", "t": "(668) What is that form which is not sprung from thought?"}, {"st": "2.2.278", "t": "The sphere of:<br>the five senses,<br>femininity,<br>masculinity and vitality,"}, {"st": "2.2.279", "t": "the decay and the impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.2.280", "t": "or whatever other form exists that is not born of thought, not caused by thought, does not have its source in thought, whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.2.281", "t": "the sphere of<br>visible forms,<br>sounds,<br>odours,<br>tastes,<br>or tangibles,"}, {"st": "2.2.282", "t": "in the spatial or fluid element,"}, {"st": "2.2.283", "t": "in the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,"}, {"st": "2.2.284", "t": "integration or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment"}, {"st": "2.2.285", "t": "— this is that form which is not sprung from thought."}, {"st": "2.2.286", "t": "(669) What is that form which comes into being together with thought (citta-saha-bhu)?"}, {"st": "2.2.287", "t": "(670) What is that form which does not come into being; together with thought?<br>Answers as in the preceding pair of relatives.<br>(671) What is that form which is consecutive to thought (citt anuparivatti)?<br>(672) What is that form which is not consecutive to thought?"}, {"st": "2.2.288", "t": "Answers as in the preceding pair of relatives.<br>(673) What is that form which belongs to the self (ajjhattikam)?<br>The spheres of the five senses — this is that form which belongs to the self.<br>(674) What is that form which is external (to the self — bahiram)?"}, {"st": "2.2.289", "t": "The sphere of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is external (to the self)."}, {"st": "2.2.290", "t": "(675) Which is that form which is gross (olarikam)?"}, {"st": "2.2.291", "t": "The spheres of the five senses and of the five kinds of sense-objects — this is that form which is gross."}, {"st": "2.2.292", "t": "(676) Which is that form which is subtle (sukhumam)?<br>Femininity... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is subtle.<br>(677) What is that form which is remote (dure)?<br>Femininity... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is remote."}, {"st": "2.2.293", "t": "(678) What is that form which is near (santike)?"}, {"st": "2.2.294", "t": "The spheres of the five senses and of the five kinds of sense-objects — this is that form which is near."}, {"st": "2.2.295", "t": "<i>[Basis (vatthu).]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.296", "t": "(679) What is that form which is the basis of visual contact (cakkhusamphassassa vatthu)?"}, {"st": "2.2.297", "t": "The sphere of vision — this is that form which is the basis of visual contact."}, {"st": "2.2.298", "t": "(680) What is that form which is not the basis of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.299", "t": "The sphere of hearing... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.300", "t": "(681) What is that form which is the basis of"}, {"st": "2.2.301", "t": "the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the visual cognition"}, {"st": "2.2.302", "t": "which is born of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.303", "t": "The sphere of vision — this is that form which is the basis of the... visual cognition which is born of visual contact."}, {"st": "2.2.304", "t": "(682) What is that form which is not the basis of the... visual cognition born of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.305", "t": "The sphere of hearing... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the basis of the... visual cognition born of visual contact."}, {"st": "2.2.306", "t": "(683) What is that form which is the basis of"}, {"st": "2.2.307", "t": "auditory...<br>olfactory...<br>gustatory...<br>bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.308", "t": "The sphere of... bodysensibility — this is that form which is the basis of... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.309", "t": "(684) What is that form which is not the basis of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.310", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the basis of... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.311", "t": "(685) What is that form which is the basis of"}, {"st": "2.2.312", "t": "the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the... cognition of body"}, {"st": "2.2.313", "t": "that is born of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.314", "t": "The sphere... of bodysensibility — this is that form which is the basis of the... cognition of body that is born of... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.315", "t": "(686) What is that form which is not the basis of the... cognition of body born of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.316", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the basis of the... cognition of body born, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.317", "t": "<i>[Mental object or idea (arammanam).]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.318", "t": "(687) What is that form which is the object in visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.319", "t": "The sphere of visible forms — this is that form which is the object in visual contact."}, {"st": "2.2.320", "t": "(688) What is that form which is not the object in visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.321", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.322", "t": "(689) What is that form which is the object in"}, {"st": "2.2.323", "t": "the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the visual cognition"}, {"st": "2.2.324", "t": "that is born of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.325", "t": "The sphere of visible forms — this is that form which is the object in... the visual cognition that is born of visual contact."}, {"st": "2.2.326", "t": "(690) What is that form which is not the object in the... visual cognition born of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.327", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the object, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.328", "t": "(691) What is that form which is the object in"}, {"st": "2.2.329", "t": "auditory...<br>olfactory...<br>gustatory...<br>bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.330", "t": "The sphere of... the tangible — this is that form which is the object in... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.331", "t": "(692) What is that form which is not the object in... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.332", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the object in... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.333", "t": "(693) What is that form which is the object in"}, {"st": "2.2.334", "t": "the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the... cognition of body"}, {"st": "2.2.335", "t": "that is born of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.336", "t": "The sphere of the tangible — this is that form which is the object in the... cognition of body that is born of... bodily contact."}, {"st": "2.2.337", "t": "(694) What is that form which is not the object in the... cognition of body that is born of bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.2.338", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the object, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.339", "t": "<i>[Sphere of sense (ayatanam).]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.340", "t": "(695) What is that form which is the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.341", "t": "The eye, that is to say, the sentient organ which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this is that form which is the sphere of vision."}, {"st": "2.2.342", "t": "(696) What is that form which is not the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.343", "t": "The sphere of hearing... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the sphere of vision."}, {"st": "2.2.344", "t": "(697) What is that form which is the sphere of hearing... smell... taste... bodysensibility?"}, {"st": "2.2.345", "t": "The body, that is to say, the sentient organ which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this is that form which is the sphere of... body-sensibility."}, {"st": "2.2.346", "t": "(698) What is that form which is not the sphere of... body-sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.2.347", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.348", "t": "(699) What is that form which is the sphere of visible forms?"}, {"st": "2.2.349", "t": "That form which, derived from the four Great Phenomena, is visible under the appearance of colour... this... which is the constituent element of visible form — this is that form which is the sphere of visible forms."}, {"st": "2.2.350", "t": "(700) What is that form which is not the sphere of visible forms?"}, {"st": "2.2.351", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.352", "t": "(701) What is that form which is the sphere of"}, {"st": "2.2.353", "t": "sound,<br>odour,<br>taste,<br>the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.2.354", "t": "The earthy (solid) element... this that is the... element of the tangible — this is that form which is the sphere of the tangible."}, {"st": "2.2.355", "t": "(702) What is that form which is not the sphere of... the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.2.356", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.357", "t": "<i>[Element (dhatu).]<\/i><br>(703) What is that form which is the element of vision?<br>The sphere of vision — this is that form which is the element of vision.<br>(704) What is that form which is not the element of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.358", "t": "The sphere of hearing... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the element of vision."}, {"st": "2.2.359", "t": "(705) What is that form which is the element of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.2.360", "t": "The sphere of visible form — this is that form which is the element of visible form."}, {"st": "2.2.361", "t": "(706) What is that form which is not the element of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.2.362", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.363", "t": "(707) What is that form which is the element of sound... of odour... of taste... of the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.2.364", "t": "The sphere of... the tangible — this is that form which is the element of... the tangible."}, {"st": "2.2.365", "t": "(708) What is that form which is not the element of... the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.2.366", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not the element of... the tangible."}, {"st": "2.2.367", "t": "<i>[Faculty (indriyam).]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.368", "t": "(709) What is that form which is the faculty of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.369", "t": "The eye, that is to say, the sentient organ which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this is that form which is the faculty of vision."}, {"st": "2.2.370", "t": "(710) What is that form which is not the faculty of vision?"}, {"st": "2.2.371", "t": "The sphere of hearing... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.372", "t": "(711) What is that form which is the faculty of hearing... smell... taste... body-sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.2.373", "t": "The... body, that is to say, the sentient principle, which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this is that form which is the faculty of... body-sensibility."}, {"st": "2.2.374", "t": "(712) What is that form which is not the faculty of... body-sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.2.375", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.376", "t": "(713) What is that form which is femininity (lit. the female faculty or potentiality)?"}, {"st": "2.2.377", "t": "That which is of the female, feminine in appearance, characteristics, occupation, and deportment, feminine in condition and being — this is that form which is femininity."}, {"st": "2.2.378", "t": "What is that form which is not femininity?"}, {"st": "2.2.379", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.380", "t": "(714) What is that form which is masculinity?"}, {"st": "2.2.381", "t": "That which is of the male, masculine in appearance, characteristics, occupation, and deportment, masculine in condition and being — this is that form which is masculinity."}, {"st": "2.2.382", "t": "(715) What is that form which is not masculinity?<br><i>Answer as in § 713a.<\/i><br>(716) What is that form which is (the faculty of) vitality?"}, {"st": "2.2.383", "t": "The persistence of these corporeal states, their subsistence, their going on, their being kept going on, their progress, continuance, preservation, life, life as faculty — this is that form which is (the faculty of) vitality."}, {"st": "2.2.384", "t": "(717) What is that form which is not (the faculty of) vitality?<br><i>Answer as in § 713a.<\/i><br><i>[Intimation (vinnatti).]<\/i><br>(718) What is that form which is bodily intimation?"}, {"st": "2.2.385", "t": "<i>Answer as in § 636.<\/i><br>(719) What is that form which is not bodily intimation?"}, {"st": "2.2.386", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is bodily intimation."}, {"st": "2.2.387", "t": "(720) What is that form which is vocal intimation?<br><i>Answer as in § 637.<\/i><br>(721) What is that form which is not vocal intimation?"}, {"st": "2.2.388", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.389", "t": "<i>[Space and fluid.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.390", "t": "(722) What is that form which is the element of space?"}, {"st": "2.2.391", "t": "That which is space and belongs to space, is sky, belongs to sky, is vacuum, belongs to vacuum, and is not in contact with the four Great Phenomena — this is that form which is the element of space."}, {"st": "2.2.392", "t": "(723) What is that form which is not the element of space?<br><i>Answer as § 721.<\/i><br>(724) What is that form which is the element of fluidity?"}, {"st": "2.2.393", "t": "That which is fluid and belongs to fluid, that which is viscid and belongs to viscid; the cohesiveness of form — this is that form which is the element of fluidity."}, {"st": "2.2.394", "t": "(725) What is that form which is not the element of fluidity?<br><i>Answer as m § 721.<\/i><br><i>[Modes of form.]<\/i><br>(726) What is that form which is lightness of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.395", "t": "That lightness of form which is its capacity for changing easily, its freedom from sluggishness and inertia — this is that form which is lightness of form."}, {"st": "2.2.396", "t": "(727) What is that form which is not lightness of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.397", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment — this is that form which is not lightness of form."}, {"st": "2.2.398", "t": "[728-731]"}, {"st": "2.2.399", "t": "<i>Questions on the other two modes of form ‘plasticity’ and ‘wieldiness’ are answered by the descriptions given in §§ 640, 641. The corresponding contradictory terms are described in the same terms as in § 727, viz,: as in § 596, with the omissions and insertion as indicated on p, 216, n. 5.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.400", "t": "<i>[Evolution of form.]<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.401", "t": "(732) What is that form which is the integration of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.402", "t": "That which is accumulation of form is the integration of form — this is that form which is, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.403", "t": "(733) What is that form which is not the integration of form?"}, {"st": "2.2.404", "t": "The sphere of vision... and bodily nutriment<i> — <\/i>this is that form which is not, etc."}, {"st": "2.2.405", "t": "[734-737]"}, {"st": "2.2.406", "t": "<i>Questions on the ‘subsistence’, ‘decay’, and ‘impermanence’ of form and their contradictories are answered analogously icith those in the group on ‘Modes of form’, the three positives being described as in §§ 642-645.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.2.407", "t": "<i>[Nutrition.]<\/i><br>(740) What is that form which is bodily nutriment?<br>This is answered as in § 646.<br>(741) What is that form which is not bodily nutriment?"}, {"st": "2.2.408", "t": "The sphere of vision... and the impermanence of form — this is that form which is not bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.2.409", "t": "Such are the Categories of Form under Dual Aspects."}, {"st": "2.2.410", "t": "[End of] the Exposition of the Pairs."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER III. CATEGORIES OF FORM UNDER TRIPLE ASPECTS"}, {"st": "2.3.1", "t": "[742-744] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and derived?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and derived?"}, {"st": "2.3.2", "t": "The sphere of visible form... and bodily nutriment.<br>(iii.) external and not derived?<br>The sphere of the tangible and the fluid element.<br>[745-747] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.3.3", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.3.4", "t": "Femininity,<br>masculinity,<br>vitality,"}, {"st": "2.3.5", "t": "or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.3.6", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.3.7", "t": "in the spheres of visible form,<br>odour,<br>taste,<br>or the tangible,"}, {"st": "2.3.8", "t": "in the spatial or the fluid element,<br>in the integration or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.9", "t": "(iii.) external and not the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.3.10", "t": "The sphere of sound,<br>bodily and vocal intimation,<br>the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,<br>decay,<br>and impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.3.11", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.3.12", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.3.13", "t": "in the spheres of visible form,<br>odour,<br>taste,<br>or the tangible,"}, {"st": "2.3.14", "t": "in the spatial or the fluid element,<br>etc."}, {"st": "2.3.15", "t": "<i>[continue as in ii.].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.16", "t": "[748-750] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.17", "t": "(i.) personal and both the issue of grasping and favourable to grasping?<br>(ii.) external and both the issue of grasping and favourable to grasping?<br>(iii.) external and not the issue of grasping but favourable to grasping?"}, {"st": "2.3.18", "t": "<i>The answers are identical with those in the jweceding triplet, taken in order.<\/i><br>[751-753] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and invisible?<br>The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.19", "t": "(ii.) external and visible?<br>The sphere of visible form.<br>(iii.) external and invisible?<br>The sphere of sound... and bodily nutriment<br>[754-756] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and reacting (impingeing)?"}, {"st": "2.3.20", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and impingeing?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects.<br>(iii.) external and non-impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.3.21", "t": "Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>[757-759] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and a faculty?<br>The five faculties of sense."}, {"st": "2.3.22", "t": "(ii.) external and a faculty?<br>Sex and vitality.<br>(iii.) external and not a faculty?<br>The sphere of visible form... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.23", "t": "[760-762] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not one of the Great Phenomena?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and belonging to the Great Phenomena?"}, {"st": "2.3.24", "t": "The sphere of the tangible and the fluid element.<br>(iii.) external and not one of the Great Phenomena?"}, {"st": "2.3.25", "t": "The spheres of the [other four kinds of sense-objects]... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.26", "t": "[763-765] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not intimation?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and intimation?"}, {"st": "2.3.27", "t": "Bodily and vocal intimation.<br>(iii.) external and not intimation?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[766-768] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and sprung from thought?"}, {"st": "2.3.28", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and sprung from thought?<br>Bodily and vocal intimation,<br>or whatever other form exists which is"}, {"st": "2.3.29", "t": "born of thought,<br>caused by thought, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.30", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 667].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.31", "t": "(iii.) external and not sprung from thought?"}, {"st": "2.3.32", "t": "Sex and vitality,<br>the decay and impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.3.33", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is"}, {"st": "2.3.34", "t": "not born of thought,<br>caused by thought, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.35", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 668].<\/i><br>[769-771] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and does not come into being together with a thought?<br>The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.36", "t": "(ii.) external and comes into being together with a thought?<br>Bodily and vocal intimation.<br>(iii.) external and does not come into being, etc.?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.37", "t": "[772-774] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.38", "t": "(i.) personal and not consecutive to a thought?<br>(ii.) external and consecutive to a thought?<br>(iii.) external and not consecutive to a thought?"}, {"st": "2.3.39", "t": "<i>The answers are identical with those in the preceding triplet, taken in order.<\/i><br>[775-777] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and gross?<br>The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.40", "t": "(ii.) external and gross?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects.<br>(iii.) external and subtle?<br>Sex... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.41", "t": "[778-780] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and near?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and remote?"}, {"st": "2.3.42", "t": "Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>(iii.) external and near?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects.<br>[781-783] What is that form which is<br>(i.) external and not the basis of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.43", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>(ii.) personal and the basis of visual contact?<br>The sphere of vision.<br>(iii.) personal and not the basis of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.44", "t": "The sphere of the other four senses.<br>[784-786] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.45", "t": "(i.) external and not a basis of<br>the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the visual cognition<br>that is born of visual contact?<br>(ii.) personal and a basis of the feeling.. the visual cognition that is born of visual contact?<br>(iii.) personal and not a basis of the feeling... the visual cognition that is born of visual contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.46", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those in the preceding triplet.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.47", "t": "[787-789] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.48", "t": "(i.) external and not a basis of<br>auditory...<br>olfactory...<br>gustatory...<br>bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.49", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>(ii.) personal and a basis of... bodily contact?<br>The spheres of the other four senses respectively.<br>(iii.) personal and not a basis of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.50", "t": "The sphere of... vision, hearing, smell, taste.<br>[790-792] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.51", "t": "(i.) external and not a basis of<br>the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the... cognition of body<br>that is born of... bodily contact?<br>(ii.) personal and a basis of... the cognition of body that is born of bodily contact?<br>(iii.) personal and not a basis of... the cognition of body that is born of bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.52", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those in the preceding triplet taken in order.<\/i><br>[793-795] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the object apprehended on occasion of visual contact?<br>The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.53", "t": "(ii.) external and the object apprehended on occasion of visual contact?<br>The sphere of visible form.<br>(iii.) external and not the object apprehended on occasion of visual contact?<br>The spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.54", "t": "[796-798] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.55", "t": "(i.) personal and not the object of<br>the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the visual cognition<br>that is horn of visual contact?<br>(ii.) external and the object of... visual cognition?<br>(iii.) external and not the object of.... visual cognition?"}, {"st": "2.3.56", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those in the preceding triplet, taken in order.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.57", "t": "[799-801] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.58", "t": "(i.) personal and not the object apprehended on occasion of<br>auditory...<br>olfactory...<br>gustatory...<br>bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.59", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and the object apprehended on occasion of... bodily contact?<br>The sphere of the tangible."}, {"st": "2.3.60", "t": "(iii.) external and not the object apprehended on occasion of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.61", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[802-804] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the object of"}, {"st": "2.3.62", "t": "the feeling...<br>the perception...<br>the thinking...<br>the... cognition of body<br>that is born of... bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.63", "t": "The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.64", "t": "(ii.) external and the object of the... cognition of body that is born of bodily contact?"}, {"st": "2.3.65", "t": "The sphere of... the tangible."}, {"st": "2.3.66", "t": "(iii.) external and not the object of the... cognition of body that is born, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.67", "t": "The spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[805-807] What is that form which is<br>(i.) external and not the sphere of vision?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.68", "t": "(ii.) personal and the sphere of vision?"}, {"st": "2.3.69", "t": "The eye, that is to say the sentient organ which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.70", "t": "(iii.) personal and not the sphere of vision?<br>The spheres of the other four senses.<br>[808-810] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.71", "t": "(i.) external and not the sphere of hearing, smell, taste, or body -sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.3.72", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.73", "t": "(ii.) personal and the sphere of... body -sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.3.74", "t": "The body, that is to say the sentient organ, which is derived from the Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.75", "t": "(iii.) personal and not the sphere of... body-sensibility?<br>The spheres of the other four senses.<br>[811-813] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the sphere of visible fonn?"}, {"st": "2.3.76", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and the sphere of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.3.77", "t": "That form which, derived from the four Great Phenomena, is visible under the appearance of colour... this that is the element of visible form — this, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.78", "t": "(iii.) external and not the sphere of visible form?<br>The spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[814-816] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.79", "t": "(i.) personal and not the sphere of sound, or of odour, or of taste, or of the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.3.80", "t": "The spheres of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.3.81", "t": "(ii.) external and the sphere of... the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.3.82", "t": "The earthy (solid) element, etc.... this that is the constituent element of the tangible — this, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.83", "t": "(iii.) external and not the sphere of.. the tangible? The spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.84", "t": "[817-819] What is that form which is<br>(i.) external and not the element of vision?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>(ii.) personal and the element of vision?"}, {"st": "2.3.85", "t": "The sphere of vision.<br>(iii.) personal and not the element of vision?<br>The other four senses.<br>[820-822] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.86", "t": "(i.) external and not the element of hearing, smell, taste, or body -sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.3.87", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>(ii.) personal and the element of... body -sensibility?<br>The sphere of... body-sensibility."}, {"st": "2.3.88", "t": "(iii.) personal and not the element of.. hody-sensihility? The spheres of the other four senses."}, {"st": "2.3.89", "t": "[823-825] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the element of visible form?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and the element of visible form?"}, {"st": "2.3.90", "t": "The sphere of visible forms.<br>(iii.) external and not the element of visible form?<br>The spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[826-828] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the element of sounds odour, taste or the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.3.91", "t": "The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and the element of.. the tangible?<br>The sphere of the tangible.<br>(iii.) external and not the element of... the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.3.92", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[829-831] What is that form which is<br>(i.) external and not the facidty of vision?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.93", "t": "(ii.) personal and the facidty of vision?<br>The sphere of vision.<br>(iii.) personal and not the faculty of vision?<br>The spheres of the other four senses."}, {"st": "2.3.94", "t": "[832-834] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.3.95", "t": "(i.) external and not the faculty of hearing, smell, taste, or body -sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.3.96", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.97", "t": "(ii.) personal and the faculty of... body -sensibility?"}, {"st": "2.3.98", "t": "The body, that is to say the sentient organ which is derived from the four Great Phenomena... this that is ‘an empty village’ — this, etc."}, {"st": "2.3.99", "t": "(iii.) personal and not the faculty of... body-sensibility?<br>The spheres of the other four senses.<br>[835-837] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the potentiality of femininity?"}, {"st": "2.3.100", "t": "The spheres of the five senses<br>(ii.) external and femininity?<br>That which is of the female, female in appearance, etc.<br><i>[continue as in § 633].<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.101", "t": "(iii.) external and not femininity?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[838-840] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not the potentiality of mascidinity? etc."}, {"st": "2.3.102", "t": "<i>The questions and answers in this triplet are exactly analogous with those in the foregoing, § 634 constituting the answer to (ii.).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.103", "t": "[841-843]"}, {"st": "2.3.104", "t": "<i>This triplet is on ‘vitality’, and is also exactly analogous with that on ‘femininity’, § 635 being substituted in (ii.).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.105", "t": "[844-846] What is that form which is<br>(i.) personal and not bodily intimation?<br>The spheres of the five senses.<br>(ii.) external and bodily intimation?"}, {"st": "2.3.106", "t": "That tension, intension, tense state of the body, etc. [continue as in § 636].<br>(iii.) external and not bodily intimation?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment.<br>[847-849]"}, {"st": "2.3.107", "t": "<i>This triplet is on ‘vocal intimation’, and similar to the foregoing, § 637 being quoted as the answer to the second question.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.108", "t": "[850-876]"}, {"st": "2.3.109", "t": "<i>Here follow ten triplets on the presence or absence, inform that is personal or external, of the ten following attributes — identical with those ending the categories according to Pairs (§§ 722-741).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.110", "t": "the element of space, the integration of form,<br>the element of fluidity, the subsistence of form,<br>the lightness of form, the decay of form,<br>the plasticity of form, the impermanence of form,<br>the wieldiness of form, bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.3.111", "t": "<i>Questions and answers in each of these triplets are identical with those in the triplet last set out on ‘bodily intimation’ (§§ 844-846), the only varying elements being the specific kind of form inquired into and its definition in the second answer of each triplet.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.112", "t": "<i>Thus the schema of the questions is<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.113", "t": "<i>(i.) personal and not...<br>(ii.) external and...<br>(iii.) external and not...<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.3.114", "t": "Such are the Categories of Form under Threefold Aspects."}, {"st": "2.3.115", "t": "[End of] the Exposition of Triplets."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER IV. CATEGORIES OF FORM UNDER FOURFOLD ASPECTS [Derivation — Work of Karma.]"}, {"st": "2.4.1", "t": "[877-880] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.4.2", "t": "(i.) derived and the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.4.3", "t": "The spheres of the five senses; the potentialities of sex and vitality, or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought, whether it be in the sphere of visible forms, odours, or tastes, the element of space, the integration or subsistence of form, or bodily nutriment,"}, {"st": "2.4.4", "t": "(ii.) derived and not the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.4.5", "t": "The sphere of sounds, bodily and vocal intimation, the lightness, plasticity, and wieldiness of form, or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought, whether it be in the spheres of visible forms, odours, or tastes, the element of space, the integration or subsistence of form, or bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.6", "t": "(iii.) underived and the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.4.7", "t": "[Form] due to karma having been wrought, which is in the sphere of the tangible and the fluid element."}, {"st": "2.4.8", "t": "(iv.) underived and not the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "2.4.9", "t": "[Form] not due to karma having been wrought, which is in the sphere of the tangible and the fluid element."}, {"st": "2.4.10", "t": "[Derivation — Production of Karma.]"}, {"st": "2.4.11", "t": "[881-884] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.4.12", "t": "(i.) derived and both the issue of grasping and favourable to it?<br>(ii.) derived and not the issue of grasping but favourable to it?<br>(iii.) underived and both the issue of grasping and favourable to it?<br>(iv.) underived and not the issue of grasping but favourable to it?"}, {"st": "2.4.13", "t": "<i>The four answers are respectively identical with those in the preceding group.<\/i><br>[Derivation — Impact.]<br>[885-888] What is that form which is<br>(i.) derived and impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.4.14", "t": "The spheres of the five senses, and of visible form, sound, odour and taste.<br>(ii.) derived and non-impingeing?<br>Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>(iii.) underived and impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.4.15", "t": "The sphere of the tangible.<br>(iv.) underived and non -impingeing?<br>The fluid element.<br>[Derivation — Bulk.]<br>[889-892] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.4.16", "t": "(i.) derived and gross?<br>(ii.) derived and subtle?<br>(iii.) underived and gross?<br>(iv.) derived and subtle?"}, {"st": "2.4.17", "t": "<i>Answers respectively identical icith those in the preceding group.<\/i><br>[Derivation — Proximity.]<br>[893-896] What is that form which is<br>(i.) derived and remote?"}, {"st": "2.4.18", "t": "Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>(ii.) derived and near?"}, {"st": "2.4.19", "t": "The spheres of the five senses, and those of visible form, sound, odour and taste."}, {"st": "2.4.20", "t": "(iii.) underived and remote?<br>The fluid element.<br>(iv.) underived and near?<br>The sphere of the tangible."}, {"st": "2.4.21", "t": "[Work of Karma — Visibility.]<br>[897-900] What is that form which is<br>(i.) the issue of grasping and visible?<br>The sphere of visible form which is due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "2.4.22", "t": "(ii.) the issue of grasping and invisible?"}, {"st": "2.4.23", "t": "The spheres of the five senses;<br>sex and vitality,"}, {"st": "2.4.24", "t": "or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.4.25", "t": "whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.4.26", "t": "the spheres of odour, taste, or the tangible,<br>the elements of space or fluidity,<br>the integration or subsistence of form, or bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.27", "t": "(iii.) not the issue of grasping and visible?<br>The sphere of visible form which is not due to karma having been wrought.<br>(iv.) not the issue of grasping and invisible?"}, {"st": "2.4.28", "t": "The sphere of sound,<br>bodily and vocal intimation,<br>the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness of form,<br>the decay,<br>or the impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.4.29", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.4.30", "t": "whether it be in"}, {"st": "2.4.31", "t": "the spheres of odour, or of taste, or of the tangible,<br>the elements of space, or of fluidity,<br>the integration or subsistence of form,<br>or bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.32", "t": "[Work of Karma — Impact.]<br>[901-904] What is that form which is<br>(i.) the issue of grasping and impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.4.33", "t": "The spheres of the five senses, or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought in"}, {"st": "2.4.34", "t": "the spheres of visible form, odour, taste or the tangible."}, {"st": "2.4.35", "t": "(ii.) the issue of grasping and non-imping eing?"}, {"st": "2.4.36", "t": "Sex or vitality, or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought in"}, {"st": "2.4.37", "t": "the elements of space or fluidity,<br>in the integration, or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.38", "t": "(iii.) not the issue of grasping and impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.4.39", "t": "The sphere of sound, or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought in the spheres of the other four kinds of sense-objects."}, {"st": "2.4.40", "t": "(iv.) not the issue of grasping and non-impingeing?"}, {"st": "2.4.41", "t": "Bodily and vocal intimation,<br>the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,<br>decay,<br>or impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.4.42", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought in"}, {"st": "2.4.43", "t": "the elements of space or fluidity,<br>in the integration or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.44", "t": "[Work of Karma — Great Phenomena.]<br>[905-908] What is that form which is<br>(i.) the issue of grasping and great phenomenon?"}, {"st": "2.4.45", "t": "The sphere of the tangible and the fluid element which are due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "2.4.46", "t": "(ii.) the issue of grasping and not great phenomenon?"}, {"st": "2.4.47", "t": "The spheres of the five senses,<br>sex,<br>vitality,"}, {"st": "2.4.48", "t": "or whatever other form exists through karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.4.49", "t": "in the element of space,<br>in the integration or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.50", "t": "(iii.) not the issue of grasping but great phenomenon?"}, {"st": "2.4.51", "t": "The sphere of the tangible and the fluid element which are not due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "2.4.52", "t": "(iv.) not the issue of grasping nor great phenomenon?"}, {"st": "2.4.53", "t": "The sphere of sound,<br>bodily and vocal intimation,<br>the lightness,<br>plasticity,<br>wieldiness,<br>decay<br>and impermanence of form,"}, {"st": "2.4.54", "t": "or whatever other form exists which is not due to karma having been wrought,"}, {"st": "2.4.55", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.4.56", "t": "in the spheres of visible form, odour, or taste,<br>in the element of space,<br>in the integration or subsistence of form,<br>or in bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.4.57", "t": "[Work of Karma — Bulk.]"}, {"st": "2.4.58", "t": "[909-912] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.4.59", "t": "(i.) the issue of grasping and gross?<br>(ii.) the issue of grasping and subtle?<br>(iii.) not the issue of grasping and gross?<br>(iv.) not the issue of grasping and subtle?"}, {"st": "2.4.60", "t": "<i>Answers respectively identical with the four in the next preceding group but one (§§ 901-904).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.4.61", "t": "[Work of Karma — Proximity.]"}, {"st": "2.4.62", "t": "[913-916] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.4.63", "t": "(i.) the issue of grasping and remote?<br>(ii.) the issue of grasping and near?<br>(iii.) not the issue of grasping and remote?<br>(iv.) not the issue of grasping and near?"}, {"st": "2.4.64", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those in the preceding group (i.e. with those in §§ 901-904), but having the order of the first and second answers inverted, as ivell as that of the third and fourth.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.4.65", "t": "[917-936]"}, {"st": "2.4.66", "t": "<i>In the next five groups of four, form is considered as a combination of<br>(a) ‘the issue of grasping and favourable to grasping’, and either<br>‘visible’,<br>‘impingeing’,<br>‘a great phenomenon’,<br>‘gross’ or<br>‘remote’, or the opposites of these five taken successively;<br>(b) ‘not the issue of grasping, but favourable to grasping’,<br>and the five above-named attributes and their opposites taken successively.<br>Thus the questions are analogous to, and the answers identical with, those in the preceding five groups (§§ 897-916).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.4.67", "t": "[Impact — Faculty.]<br>[937-940] What is that form which is<br>(i.) impingeing and faculty?<br>The faculties of the five senses."}, {"st": "2.4.68", "t": "(ii.) impingeing and not faculty?<br>The five kinds of sense-objects.<br>(iii.) non-impingeingandfacidty?<br>Sex and vitality."}, {"st": "2.4.69", "t": "(iv.) non-impingeing and not faculty?<br>Bodily and vocal intimation... and bodily nutriment.<br>[Impact — Great Phenomenon.]<br>[941-944] What is that form which is<br>(i.) impingeing and a great phenomenon? The sphere of the tangible."}, {"st": "2.4.70", "t": "(ii.) impingeing and not a great phenomenon ‘? The spheres of visible form, sound, odour and taste."}, {"st": "2.4.71", "t": "(iii.) non-impingeing and a great phenomenon? The fluid element.<br>(iv.) non-impingeing and not a great phenomenon? Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>[Faculty (Potentiality) — Bulk.]<br>[945-948] What is that form which is<br>(i.) faculty and gross?"}, {"st": "2.4.72", "t": "The faculties of the five senses.<br>(ii.) faculty and subtle?<br>Sex and vitality.<br>(iii.) non faculty and gross?"}, {"st": "2.4.73", "t": "The spheres of the five kinds, of sense-objects.<br>(iv.) nonfacidty and subtle?<br>Intimation... and bodily nutriment.<br>[Faculty (Potentiality) — Proximity.]<br>[949-952] What is that form which is<br>(i.) facidty and remote?"}, {"st": "2.4.74", "t": "Sex and vitality.<br>(ii.) faculty and near?<br>The faculties of the five senses.<br>(iii.) nonfacidty and remote?"}, {"st": "2.4.75", "t": "Intimation... and bodily nutriment.<br>(iv.) non faculty and near?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects.<br>[Great Phenomenon — Bulk.]<br>[953-956] What is that form which is<br>(i.) a great phenomenon and gross?"}, {"st": "2.4.76", "t": "The sphere of the tangible.<br>(ii.) a great phenomenon and subtle?<br>The fluid element.<br>(iii.) not a great phenomenon and gross?"}, {"st": "2.4.77", "t": "The spheres of the five senses and of visible form, sounds odour and taste.<br>(iv.) not a great phenomenon and subtle?<br>Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>[Great Phenomenon — Proximity.]<br>[957-960] What is that form which is<br>(i.) a great phenomenon and remote?"}, {"st": "2.4.78", "t": "The fluid element.<br>(ii.) a great phenomenon and near?<br>The sphere of the tangible.<br>(iii.) not a great phenomenon and remote?"}, {"st": "2.4.79", "t": "Sex and vitality.<br>(iv.) not a great phenomenon and near?<br>The spheres of the five senses and of visible form, sound, odour and taste.<br>[Form as Seen, Heard, Imagined, Understood.]<br>(961)"}, {"st": "2.4.80", "t": "(i.) The sphere of visible form is Form Seen,<br>(ii.) The sphere of sound is Form Heard,<br>(iii.) The sphere of odour, taste and the tangible is Form Imagined,<br>(iv.) All form is Form Cognized by the mind."}, {"st": "2.4.81", "t": "Such are the Categories of Form under Fourfold Aspects."}, {"st": "2.4.82", "t": "[End of] the Groups of Four."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER V. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER A FIVEFOLD ASPECT"}, {"st": "2.5.1", "t": "[962-966] What is that form which is<br>(i.) earth-element (pathavi-dhatu)?<br>That which is"}, {"st": "2.5.2", "t": "hard,<br>rough,<br>hardness,<br>rigidity,"}, {"st": "2.5.3", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.5.4", "t": "of the self,<br>or external,<br>or the issue of grasping,<br>or not the issue of grasping."}, {"st": "2.5.5", "t": "(ii.) fluid-element (apodhatu)?"}, {"st": "2.5.6", "t": "That which is"}, {"st": "2.5.7", "t": "fluid, belonging to fluid,<br>viscid, belonging to what is viscid,<br>the cohesiveness of form,"}, {"st": "2.5.8", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.5.9", "t": "of the self,<br>or external,<br>or the issue of grasping<br>or not the issue of grasping."}, {"st": "2.5.10", "t": "(iii.) flame-element (tejodhatu)?"}, {"st": "2.5.11", "t": "That which is"}, {"st": "2.5.12", "t": "flame, belonging to flame,<br>heat, belonging to heat,<br>hot, belonging to what is hot,"}, {"st": "2.5.13", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.5.14", "t": "of the self,<br>or, etc,"}, {"st": "2.5.15", "t": "<i>[continue as in preceding].<\/i><br>(iv.) air-element (vayodhatu)?<br>That which is"}, {"st": "2.5.16", "t": "air, belongs to air [the fluctuation],<br>the inflation,<br>of form,"}, {"st": "2.5.17", "t": "whether it be"}, {"st": "2.5.18", "t": "of the self,<br>or, etc."}, {"st": "2.5.19", "t": "(v.) derived?<br>The spheres of the five senses... and solid nutriment.<br>Such is the Category of Form under a Fivefold Aspect.<br>[End of] the Group of Five."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VI. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER A SIXFOLD ASPECT (967)"}, {"st": "2.6.1", "t": "(i.) The sphere of visible forms is form cognizable by sight,<br>(ii.) The sphere of sounds is form cognizable by hearing,<br>(iii.) The sphere of odours is form cognizable by smell,<br>(iv.) The sphere of tastes is form cognizable by taste,<br>(v.) The sphere of the tangible is form cognizable by body-sensibility.<br>(vi.) All form is form cognizable by the mind."}, {"st": "2.6.2", "t": "Such is the Category of Form under a Sixfold Aspect."}, {"st": "2.6.3", "t": "[End of] the Group of Six."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VII. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER A SEVENFOLD ASPECT (968)"}, {"st": "2.7.1", "t": "(i.) The sphere of visible form is form cognizable by sight,<br>(ii.) The sphere of sound is form cognizable by hearing,<br>(iii.) The sphere of odour is form cognizable by smell.<br>(iv.) The sphere of taste is form cognizable by taste,<br>(v.) The sphere of the tangible is form cognizable by bodysensibility."}, {"st": "2.7.2", "t": "(969)"}, {"st": "2.7.3", "t": "(vi.) The spheres of visible form, sound, odour, taste, and the tangible are form cognizable by the element of ideation.<br>(vii.) All form is form comprehensible by the element of representative intellection."}, {"st": "2.7.4", "t": "Such is the Category of Form under a Sevenfold Aspect."}, {"st": "2.7.5", "t": "[End of] the Group of Seven."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VIII. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER AN EIGHTFOLD ASPECT (970)"}, {"st": "2.8.1", "t": "(i.) The sphere of visible form is form cognizable by the eye.<br>(ii.) The sphere of sound is form cognizable by the ear.<br>(iii.) The sphere of odour is form cognizable by the nose.<br>(iv.) The sphere of taste is form cognizable by the tongue,<br>(v.) Pleasurable agreeable contact obtainable by touch is form cognizable by the body,<br>(vi.) Unpleasant disagreeable contact obtainable by touch is form cognizable by the body,<br>(vii.) The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects are form cognizable by ideation,<br>(viii.) All form is form comprehensible by representative intellection."}, {"st": "2.8.2", "t": "Such is the Category of Form under an Eightfold Aspect."}, {"st": "2.8.3", "t": "[End of] the Group of Eight."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER IX. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER A NINEFOLD ASPECT"}, {"st": "2.9.1", "t": "[971-973] What is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.9.2", "t": "(i.) the faculty of vision?<br>(ii.) the faculty of hearing?<br>(iii.) the faculty of smell?<br>(iv.) the faculty of taste?<br>(v.) the faculty of bodysensibility?<br>(vi.) the potentiality of femininity?<br>(vii.) the potentiality of masculinity?<br>(viii.) the potentiality of vitality?"}, {"st": "2.9.3", "t": "<i>The eight answers are those given in the original descriptions of the eight faculties or potentialities enumerated (§§ 597, 601, 605, 609, 613, 633-535).<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.9.4", "t": "(ix.) What is that form which is not faculty? The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects... and bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "2.9.5", "t": "Such is the Category of Form under a Ninefold Aspect."}, {"st": "2.9.6", "t": "[End of] the Group of Nine."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER X. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER A TENFOLD ASPECT [974, 975]"}, {"st": "2.10.1", "t": "<i>The first eight questions and ansivers are identical with the first eight in the preceding group.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.10.2", "t": "[976, 977] What is that form which is<br>(ix.) not faculty hut imping eing?<br>The spheres of the five kinds of sense-objects.<br>(x.) notfacidty and non-imping eing?"}, {"st": "2.10.3", "t": "Intimation... and bodily nutriment.<br>Such is the Category of Form under a Tenfold Aspect.<br>[End of] the Group of Ten."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER XI. THE CATEGORY OF FORM UNDER AN ELEVENFOLD ASPECT"}, {"st": "2.11.1", "t": "[978, 979] WHAT is that form which is"}, {"st": "2.11.2", "t": "(i.) the sphere of vision?<br>(ii.) the sphere of hearing?<br>(iii.) the sphere of smell?<br>(iv.) the sphere of taste?<br>(v.) the sphere of bodysensibility?<br>(vi.) the sphere of visible form?<br>(vii.) the sphere of sound?<br>(viii.) the sphere of odour?<br>(ix.) the sphere of sapids?<br>(x.) the sphere of the tangible?"}, {"st": "2.11.3", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 597, 601, 605, 609, 613, 617, 621, 625, 629, 649 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "2.11.4", "t": "(980)"}, {"st": "2.11.5", "t": "(xi.) What is that form which is invisible, nonimpingeing, and included in the sphere of [mental] states?"}, {"st": "2.11.6", "t": "Sex... and bodily nutriment.<br>Such is the Category of Form under an Elevenfold Aspect.<br>[End of] the Group of Eleven.<br>[End of] THE Divisions of Form."}, {"st": "2.11.7", "t": "[End of] the Eighth Portion for Eecitation."}, {"sp": "HDR", "t": "BOOK III. THE DIVISION ENTITLED ‘ELIMINATION’"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "PART I"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER I. THE GROUP OF TRIPLETS"}, {"st": "3.2.1", "t": "(981) Which are the states that are good?"}, {"st": "3.2.2", "t": "The three roots of good (karma), to wit, absence of lust, absence of hate, absence of dulness; the skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses and intellect when they are associated with those three roots; whatever action, bodily, vocal and mental, springs from those three roots."}, {"st": "3.2.3", "t": "(982) Which are the states that are bad?"}, {"st": "3.2.4", "t": "The three roots of bad (karma), to wit, lust, hate, dulness; the Corruptions that are united with them; the skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses and intellect when these are associated with them; whatever action, bodily, vocal and mental, springs from them."}, {"st": "3.2.5", "t": "(983) Which are the states that are indeterminate?"}, {"st": "3.2.6", "t": "The results of good and bad states taking effect in the worlds of sense, form, or the formless, or in the [life that is] Unincluded; the skandhas of feeling, perception, syntheses and intellect, those states, moreover, known as kiriya-thoughts, which are neither good, nor bad, nor the results of karma; lastly, all form and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.7", "t": "(984) Which are the states that are associated with a feeling of ease?"}, {"st": "3.2.8", "t": "The skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect (the feeling itself being excepted) are the states associated [with the consciousness arising] in an ease-yielding soil, whether it belong to the worlds of sense or of form, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.9", "t": "(985) Which are the states that are associated with distressful feeling?"}, {"st": "3.2.10", "t": "The skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect (the feeling itself being excepted) are the states associated [with the consciousness arising] in a distressful soil belonging to the sensuous universe."}, {"st": "3.2.11", "t": "(986) Which are the states that are associated with feeling that is neither painful nor pleasant?"}, {"st": "3.2.12", "t": "The skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect (the feeling itself being excepted) are the states associated [with the consciousness arising] in a neutral soil, whether it belong to the worlds of sense, form, or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.13", "t": "(987) Which are the states that are results?"}, {"st": "3.2.14", "t": "The results of good and bad states which take effect in the worlds of sense, form and the formless, and in the life that is Unincluded; [in other words] the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.15", "t": "(988) Which are the states that involve resultant states?"}, {"st": "3.2.16", "t": "Good and bad states belonging to the worlds of sense, form and the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; [in other words] the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.17", "t": "(989) Which are the states that neither are results, nor have the quality of involving resultant states?"}, {"st": "3.2.18", "t": "Those states concerning action which are neither good, nor bad, nor the results of karma; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.19", "t": "(990) Which are the states that are both the issue of grasping and favourable to it?"}, {"st": "3.2.20", "t": "The co-Intoxicant results of good and bad states taking effect in the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the four skandhas; such form, moreover, as is due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "3.2.21", "t": "(991) Which are the states that are not the issue of grasping but are favourable to grasping?"}, {"st": "3.2.22", "t": "Good and bad co-Intoxicant states taking effect in the worlds of sense, form, or the formless; in other words, the four skandhas; those states, moreover, known as kiriyathoughts, which are neither good, nor bad, nor the results of karma; as well as such form as is not due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "3.2.23", "t": "(992) Which are the states that are neither the issue of grasping nor favourable to it?"}, {"st": "3.2.24", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.25", "t": "(993) Which are the states that are corrupt and baneful?"}, {"st": "3.2.26", "t": "The three roots of bad (karma), to wit, lust, hate, dulness; the Corruptions that are united with them; the four skandhas when these are associated with them; whatever action, bodily, vocal and mental, springs from them."}, {"st": "3.2.27", "t": "(994) Which are the states that are not corrupt but baneful?"}, {"st": "3.2.28", "t": "Good and indeterminate co-Intoxicant states taking effect in the worlds of sense, form and the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.29", "t": "(995) Which are the states that are neither corrupt nor baneful?"}, {"st": "3.2.30", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.31", "t": "(996) Which are the states ‘wherein conception works and thought discursive’?"}, {"st": "3.2.32", "t": "The four skandhas (conception and discursive thought excluded) which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in a soil favourable to the working of conception and of discursive thought, whether it belong to the world of sense or of form, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.33", "t": "(997) Which are the states ‘wherein is no working of conception but only of thought discursive’?"}, {"st": "3.2.34", "t": "The four skandhas (discursive thought excluded) which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in a soil favourable to the working, not of conception, but only of discursive thought, whether it belong to the world of form, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.35", "t": "(998) Which are the states that are ‘void of the working of conception and of thought discursive’?"}, {"st": "3.2.36", "t": "The four skandhas which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in a soil void of conception and discursive thought, whether it belong to the world of sense, form, or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.37", "t": "(999) Which are the states that are accompanied by joy?"}, {"st": "3.2.38", "t": "The four skandhas (joy being excluded) which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in a soil yielding joy, whether it belong to the worlds of sense or form, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.39", "t": "(1000) Which are the states that are accompanied by ease?"}, {"st": "3.2.40", "t": "The skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect (ease being excluded) which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in an ease-yielding soil, whether it belong to the worlds of sense or form, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.41", "t": "(1001) Which are the states that are accompanied by disinterestedness?"}, {"st": "3.2.42", "t": "The skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect (disinterestedness being excluded) which are associated [with the consciousness arising] in a soil favourable to disinterestedness, whether it belong to the worlds of form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.43", "t": "(1002) Which are the states that are to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.2.44", "t": "The three Fetters, to wit, the theory of individuality, perplexity, and the contagion of mere rule and ritual."}, {"st": "3.2.45", "t": "In this connexion"}, {"st": "3.2.46", "t": "(1003) What is the ‘theory of individuality’?"}, {"st": "3.2.47", "t": "When in this world the ignorant, average man who perceives not the Noble Ones,<br>who comprehends not, nor is trained according to the doctrine of the Noble Ones,<br>who perceives not good men,<br>who comprehends not, nor is trained according to, the doctrine of good men, regards"}, {"st": "3.2.48", "t": "(1) the self as bodily form, or<br>(2) as having bodily form, or regards<br>(3) bodily form as being in the self, or<br>(4) the self as being in bodily form or regards<br>(5) the self as feeling, or<br>(6) as having feeling, or regards<br>(7) feeling as being in the self, or<br>(8) the self as being in feeling; or regards<br>(9) the self as perception, or<br>(10) as having perception, or regards<br>(11) perception as being in the self, or<br>(12) the self as being in perception; or regards<br>(13) the self as syntheses, or<br>(14) as having syntheses, or regards<br>(15) syntheses as being in the self, or<br>(16) the self as being in syntheses; or regards<br>(17) the self as intellect, or<br>(18) as having intellect, or regards<br>(19) intellect as being in the self, or<br>(20) the self as being in intellect"}, {"st": "3.2.49", "t": "— then this kind of opinion,<br>this walking in opinion,<br>this jungle of opinion, wilderness of opinion, puppet-show of opinion, scuffling of opinion,<br>this Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path, wrong road, wrongness,<br>this ‘fording-place’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.2.50", "t": "— this is called the theory of individuality.<br>(1004) What is ‘perplexity’?<br>To doubt, to be perplexed about,"}, {"st": "3.2.51", "t": "(1) the Master, to doubt, to be perplexed about,<br>(2) the Doctrine, to doubt, to be perplexed about,<br>(3) the Order, about<br>(4) the Discipline, about<br>(5) the past, the future, about both the past and the future,<br>(6) as to whether there be an assignable cause of states causally determined"}, {"st": "3.2.52", "t": "— it is this kind of doubt,<br>this working of doubt,<br>this dubiety, puzzlement, perplexity, distraction, standing at cross-roads;<br>collapse, uncertainty of grasp;<br>evasion, hesitation,<br>incapacity of grasping thoroughly,<br>stiffness of mind, mental scarifying,"}, {"st": "3.2.53", "t": "that is called perplexity."}, {"st": "3.2.54", "t": "(1005) What is the contagion of mere rule and ritual?"}, {"st": "3.2.55", "t": "The theory, held by recluses and Brahmins outside our doctrine,<br>that purification is got by rules of moral conduct,<br>that purification is got by rites,<br>that purification is got by rules of moral conduct and by rites"}, {"st": "3.2.56", "t": "— this kind of opinion,<br>this walking in mere opinion,<br>this jungle of opinion,<br>this wilderness of opinion,<br>this puppet-show of opinion,<br>scuffling of opinion,<br>Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path, wrong road, wrongness,<br>this ‘fording-place’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.2.57", "t": "— this is called the contagion of mere rule and ritual."}, {"st": "3.2.58", "t": "(1006)"}, {"st": "3.2.59", "t": "These three Fetters, and the Corruptions united with them, and the four skandhas associated with them, as well as the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them — these are the states which are to be put away by insight."}, {"st": "3.2.60", "t": "(1007) Which are the states that are to be put away by culture?"}, {"st": "3.2.61", "t": "Whatever lust, hate and dulness still remain, and any corruptions united with them; the four skandhas that are associated with them; whatever action, bodily, vocal or mental, springs from them."}, {"st": "3.2.62", "t": "(1008) Which are the states that are to be put away neither by insight nor by culture?"}, {"st": "3.2.63", "t": "Good and indeterminate states relating to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; the four skandhas; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.64", "t": "(1009) Which are the states the causes of which are to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.2.65", "t": "The three Fetters, to wit, theory of individuality, perplexity, contagion of mere rule and ritual."}, {"st": "3.2.66", "t": "In this connexion<br>(1010) What is ‘theory of individuality’?...<br><i>[continue as in §§ 1003-1005].<\/i><br>[1010a]"}, {"st": "3.2.67", "t": "These three Fetters, and the Corruptions united with them, and the four skandhas associated with them, as well as the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them — these are the states the causes of which are to be put away by insight."}, {"st": "3.2.68", "t": "[1010b]<br>The three Fetters: —"}, {"st": "3.2.69", "t": "theory of individuality,"}, {"st": "3.2.70", "t": "perplexity,"}, {"st": "3.2.71", "t": "contagion of mere rule and ritual<br>— are the states that are to be put away by insight."}, {"st": "3.2.72", "t": "The lust, hate and dulness united with them are the causes that are to be put away by insight. And the Corruptions united with them, the four skandhas associated with them, and the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them, are the states the causes oftvhich are to be put away by insight."}, {"st": "3.2.73", "t": "(1011) Which are the states the causes of which are to be put away by culture?"}, {"st": "3.2.74", "t": "Whatever lust, hate and dulness still remain, these are causes that are to be put away by culture. And the Corruptions united with them, the four skandhas associated with them, and the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them — these are states the causes of which are to be put away by culture."}, {"st": "3.2.75", "t": "(1012) Which are the states the causes of which are to be put away neither by insight, nor by cultivation?"}, {"st": "3.2.76", "t": "The afore-mentioned states excepted, all other states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, form and the formless, and to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.77", "t": "(1013) Which are the states that make for the piling up [of rebirth]?"}, {"st": "3.2.78", "t": "Good and bad co-Intoxicant states relating to the worlds of sense, form and the formless; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.79", "t": "(1014) Which are the states that make for the undoing of rebirth?"}, {"st": "3.2.80", "t": "The four Paths that are the Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.81", "t": "(1015) Which are the states that make neither for the piling up, nor for the undoing of rebirth?"}, {"st": "3.2.82", "t": "The results of good and bad states taking effect in the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or in the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; those states, moreover, known as kiriya-thoughts, which are neither good nor bad, nor the result of karma; all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.83", "t": "(1015) Which are the states that appertain to studentship?"}, {"st": "3.2.84", "t": "The four Paths that are the Unincluded and the three lowest Fruits of the life of the recluse."}, {"st": "3.2.85", "t": "(1016) Which are the states not appertaining to studentship?"}, {"st": "3.2.86", "t": "The topmost fruit — the fruit that is Arahatship."}, {"st": "3.2.87", "t": "(1017) Which are the states neither appertaining, nor not appertaining to studentship?"}, {"st": "3.2.88", "t": "The afore-mentioned states excepted, all other states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, form and the formless; all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.89", "t": "(1018) Which are the states that are limited?"}, {"st": "3.2.90", "t": "All states, good, bad and indeterminate, which relate to the universe of sense; in other words, the Jive skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.91", "t": "(1019) Which are the states that have a wider scope?"}, {"st": "3.2.92", "t": "States, good, bad and indeterminate, which relate to the worlds of form and the formless; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.93", "t": "(1021) Which are the states that are infinite?"}, {"st": "3.2.94", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.95", "t": "(1022) Which are the states that have limited objects of thought?"}, {"st": "3.2.96", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with limited matters."}, {"st": "3.2.97", "t": "(1023) Which are the states that have enlarged objects of thought?"}, {"st": "3.2.98", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with matters of wider scope."}, {"st": "3.2.99", "t": "(1024) Which are the states that have infinite objects of thought?"}, {"st": "3.2.100", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with matters of infinite importance."}, {"st": "3.2.101", "t": "(1025) Which are the states that are base?"}, {"st": "3.2.102", "t": "The three roots of bad (karma) — lust, hate, dulness — the Corruptions united with them; the four skandhas associated with them; the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them."}, {"st": "3.2.103", "t": "(1026) Which are the states that are of medium worth?"}, {"st": "3.2.104", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, form and the formless; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.105", "t": "(1027) Which are the states that are perfected?"}, {"st": "3.2.106", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.107", "t": "(1028) Which are the states the wrongfulness of which is fixed as to its consequences?"}, {"st": "3.2.108", "t": "The five acts that have immediate results, and those wrong views that are fixed in their consequences."}, {"st": "3.2.109", "t": "(1029) Which are the states the righteousness of which is fixed as to its consequences?"}, {"st": "3.2.110", "t": "The four Paths that are the Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.2.111", "t": "(1030) Which are the states that do not entail fixed consequences?"}, {"st": "3.2.112", "t": "The afore-mentioned states excepted, all other states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, form and the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.113", "t": "(1031) Which are the states that have the Path as their object of thought?"}, {"st": "3.2.114", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with the Noble Path."}, {"st": "3.2.115", "t": "(1032) Which are the states that are causally dependent upon the Path?"}, {"st": "3.2.116", "t": "[Firstly] the four skandhas when associated with the stages of the Path as experienced by one who is conversant with the Path (the stages being excepted)."}, {"st": "3.2.117", "t": "[Secondly (1033)] the four skandhas when associated with the right views — these being both Path and Cause — of one who is conversant with the Path (the right views being excepted)."}, {"st": "3.2.118", "t": "[Thirdly] the four skandhas when associated with the states of freedom from lust, hate and dulness peculiar to one who is conversant with the Path."}, {"st": "3.2.119", "t": "Now, these [last named] states are the ‘Path-causes’; the former (the skandhas) are those states which are causally dependent upon the Path."}, {"st": "3.2.120", "t": "(1034) Which are the causes that are Path-governed?"}, {"st": "3.2.121", "t": "[Firstly] those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to senseimpressions, which in arising make the Noble Path their governor."}, {"st": "3.2.122", "t": "[Secondly] the four skandhas when associated with the investigation carried on by one who is conversant with the Path, and who is cultivating a way wherein investigation is the dominant factor."}, {"st": "3.2.123", "t": "(1035) Which are the states that ‘have arisen’?"}, {"st": "3.2.124", "t": "Those states that have been born,<br>have become,<br>have been gotten,<br>created, re-created,<br>made manifest,"}, {"st": "3.2.125", "t": "— that have arisen,<br>have come to pass,<br>have happened,<br>have supervened,<br>have been caused to arise,<br>are classed together among the things that have arisen, to wit, form, feeling, perception, syntheses, intellect."}, {"st": "3.2.126", "t": "(1036) Which are the states that have ‘not arisen’?"}, {"st": "3.2.127", "t": "Those states that are unborn,<br>have not become,<br>have not been gotten,<br>nor created,<br>nor re-created,<br>nor made manifest;"}, {"st": "3.2.128", "t": "that have not arisen nor come to pass;<br>nor happened,<br>nor supervened;"}, {"st": "3.2.129", "t": "that have not been caused to arise,<br>that are classed together among the things that have not arisen, to wit, forms, feelings, perceptions, syntheses, intellect."}, {"st": "3.2.130", "t": "(1037) Which are the states that are bound to arise?"}, {"st": "3.2.131", "t": "The results of those good and bad states related to the worlds of sense, form and the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded, the consequences of which are not yet matured, to wit, the four skandhas and that form due to karma having been wrought which will arise."}, {"st": "3.2.132", "t": "(1038) Which are the states that are past?"}, {"st": "3.2.133", "t": "Those states that are past are extinct, dissolved, changed, terminated, exterminated; are past and classed among the things that are past; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.134", "t": "(1039) Which are the states that are future?"}, {"st": "3.2.135", "t": "The states that are unborn,<br>that have not become,<br>not been gotten,<br>nor created,<br>nor re-created,<br>nor made manifest;"}, {"st": "3.2.136", "t": "that have not arisen,<br>nor come to pass,<br>nor happened,<br>nor supervened;"}, {"st": "3.2.137", "t": "that have not arrived,<br>and are classed among the things that have not arrived."}, {"st": "3.2.138", "t": "(1040) Which are the things that are present?"}, {"st": "3.2.139", "t": "Those states that have been born,<br>have become,<br>have been gotten,<br>created, re-created,<br>made manifest;"}, {"st": "3.2.140", "t": "that have arisen,<br>have come to pass,<br>have supervened,<br>have been caused to arise;"}, {"st": "3.2.141", "t": "that have arisen over against and are classed among the things that have so arisen."}, {"st": "3.2.142", "t": "[1041-1048] Which are the states that have the past... future... present as their object of thought?"}, {"st": "3.2.143", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual and synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with states that are past... future... present."}, {"st": "3.2.144", "t": "(1044) Which are the states that are personal?"}, {"st": "3.2.145", "t": "Those states which, for this or that being, are of the self, self-referable, one’s own, individual, the issue of grasping; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.146", "t": "(1045) Which are the states that are external?"}, {"st": "3.2.147", "t": "Those states which, for this or that other being, for other individuals, are of the self, self-referable, their own, individual, the issue of grasping in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.2.148", "t": "(1046) Which are the states that are personal-external?"}, {"st": "3.2.149", "t": "States which are both [personal and external]."}, {"st": "3.2.150", "t": "[1047-1049] Which are the states that have an object of thought concerning the self... concerning that which is external [to the self]... concerning that which is ‘personal-external’?"}, {"st": "3.2.151", "t": "Those emotional, perceptual, synthetic states, as well as those of intellect applied to sense-impressions, which arise in connexion with states of the self... states that are external... states that are personal-external."}, {"st": "3.2.152", "t": "(1050) Which are the states that are both visible and impingeing?<br>The sphere of visible form.<br>(1051) Which are the states that are invisible, but impingeing?"}, {"st": "3.2.153", "t": "The spheres of the &amp;yq senses and the spheres of sound, odour, taste and the tangible."}, {"st": "3.2.154", "t": "(1052) Which are the states that are both invisible and non-impingeing?"}, {"st": "3.2.155", "t": "The four skandhas; that form, moreover, which, being invisible and non-impingeing, is yet included in the sphere of [mental] states; also uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.2.156", "t": "[End of] the Triplets."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER II. THE GROUP ON CAUSE"}, {"st": "3.3.1", "t": "I.<br>(1053) Which are the states that are causes?<br>A. The three causes of good (karma).<br>B. The three causes of bad (karma)."}, {"st": "3.3.2", "t": "C. The three causes of the indeterminate.<br>The nine causes operative in the sensuous universe.<br>The six causes operative in the universe of form.<br>The six causes operative in the universe of the formless."}, {"st": "3.3.3", "t": "D. The six causes operative in the life that is the Unincluded.<br>(A) In this connexion,<br>(1054) Which are the three causes of good karma?<br>The absence of lust, hate and dulness."}, {"st": "3.3.4", "t": "In this connexion,<br>(1055) What is the absence of lust?"}, {"st": "3.3.5", "t": "The absence of lust, lusting, lustfulness is the absence of infatuation, of raving, of passionateness;<br>the absence of covetousness, that absence of lust which is the root of good (karma)."}, {"st": "3.3.6", "t": "(1056) What is the absence of hate?"}, {"st": "3.3.7", "t": "The absence of hate, hating, hatred;<br>love, loving, loving disposition;<br>tender care, forbearance, considerateness;<br>seeking the general good, compassion;<br>the absence of malice, of malignity;<br>that absence of hate which is the root of good (karma)."}, {"st": "3.3.8", "t": "(1057) What is the absence of dulness?"}, {"st": "3.3.9", "t": "Knowledge about ill, about the uprising of ill, about the cessation of ill, and about the way leading to the cessation of ill;<br>knowledge about the former things, about the latter things, about both taken together;<br>knowledge about the assignable causation of causally determined states"}, {"st": "3.3.10", "t": "— even that kind of wisdom which is understanding, search, research, searching the Truth, etc."}, {"st": "3.3.11", "t": "<i>[continue as in § 34].<\/i><br>These are the three causes of good (karma).<br>(B) In this connexion,<br>(1058) Which are the three causes of bad (karma)?"}, {"st": "3.3.12", "t": "Lust, hate, dulness.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1059) What is lust?<br>That which is"}, {"st": "3.3.13", "t": "passion (rago),<br>infatuation (sarago),<br>fawning (anunayo),<br>compliance (anurodho),<br>delighting in (nandi),<br>taking passionate delight in (nandi-rago),<br>infatuation of mind (cittassa sarago),<br>longing (iccha),<br>languishing (muccha),<br>devouring (ajjhosanam),<br>greed (gedho),<br>omnivorous greed (paligedho),<br>cleaving to (sango),<br>a slough (panko),<br>seduction (eja),<br>trickery (maya),<br>genitrix (janika),<br>progenitrix (sanjananl),<br>seamstress (sibbani),<br>she who ensnares (jalini),<br>the river (sarita),<br>she who is poisonous (visattika),<br>the thread (suttam),<br>diffusion (visata),<br>she who toils (ayuhani),<br>the consort (dutiya),<br>hankering (panidhi),<br>she who leads to renewed existence (bhavanetti),<br>the jungle (vanam),<br>the undergrowth (vanatho),<br>intimacy (santhavo),<br>unctuous affection (sineho),<br>affection (apekkha),<br>connexion (patibandhu),<br>craving (asa),<br>wanting (asimsana),<br>cupidity (asimsitattam),<br>craving for visual forms (rupasa, etc.),<br>craving for sounds,<br>craving for odours,<br>craving for tastes,<br>craving for the tangible,<br>craving for getting,<br>craving for wealth,<br>craving for children,<br>craving for life,<br>mumbling (jappa),<br>mumbling on,<br>mumbling over,<br>muttering,<br>murmuring,<br>self-indulgence (loluppam),<br>self-indulging,<br>intemperateness,<br>agitation (puncikata),<br>longing for the agreeable (sadhu kamyata),<br>incestuous passion (adhammarago),<br>lawless lust (visamalobho),<br>appetite (nikanti),<br>hungering for (nikamana),<br>entreating (patthana),<br>envying (pihana),<br>imploring (sampatthana),<br>thirst for sensual indulgence (kamatanha),<br>thirst for existence (bhavatanha),<br>thirst for non-existence (vibhavatanha),<br>thirst for form,<br>thirst for formlessness,<br>thirst for annihilation,<br>thirst for visible forms,<br>thirst for sounds,<br>thirst for smells,<br>thirst for tastes,<br>thirst for the tangible,<br>thirst for mental states (dhammatanha),<br>a flood (ogho),<br>a yoke (yogo),<br>trammels (gantho),<br>attachment (upadanam),<br>obstruction (avaranam),<br>hindrance (nivaranam),<br>counterfeiting (chadanam),<br>bondage (bandhanam),<br>depravity (upakkileso),<br>faltering (anusayo),<br>pervading (pariyutthanam),<br>a creeper (lata),<br>avarice (veviccham),<br>root of pain,<br>source of pain (dukkhanidanam),<br>production of pain (dukkhappabhavo),<br>Mara’s trap (marapaso),<br>Mara’s fish-hook (marabalisam),<br>Mara’s domain (maravisayo),<br>thirst,<br>thirst for delight (nanditanha),<br>the fishing-net of thirst (jalamtanha),<br>the leash of thirst (gaddulatanh a),<br>the ocean (samuddo),<br>covetousness (abhijjha),<br>the lust that is the root of evil"}, {"st": "3.3.14", "t": "— this is what is called lust.<br>(1060) What is hate?<br>When annoyance springs up at the thought:"}, {"st": "3.3.15", "t": "he has done me harm, is doing, will do me harm;<br>he has done harm, is doing harm, will do harm to someone dear and precious to me;<br>he has conferred a benefit, is conferring, will confer a benefit on someone I dislike and object to;<br>or when annoyance springs up groundlessly:"}, {"st": "3.3.16", "t": "— all such vexation of"}, {"st": "3.3.17", "t": "spirit, resentment, repugnance, hostility;<br>ill-temper, irritation, indignation;<br>hate, antipathy, abhorrence;<br>mental disorder, detestation;<br>anger, fuming, irascibility;<br>hate, hating, hatred;<br>disorder, getting upset, derangement;<br>opposition, hostility;<br>churlishness, abruptness, disgust of heart"}, {"st": "3.3.18", "t": "— this is what is called hate."}, {"st": "3.3.19", "t": "(1061) What is dulness?"}, {"st": "3.3.20", "t": "Lack of knowledge about Ill,<br>lack of knowledge about the uprising of Ill,<br>lack of knowledge about the cessation of Ill,<br>lack of knowledge about the way leading to the cessation of Ill;<br>lack of knowledge about the former things, about the latter things and about both taken together;<br>lack of knowledge about the assignable causation of causally determined states"}, {"st": "3.3.21", "t": "— even all that kind of lack of knowledge which is"}, {"st": "3.3.22", "t": "lack of insight,<br>of understanding,<br>of wakefulness,<br>of enlightenment,<br>of penetration,<br>of comprehension,<br>of sounding,<br>of comparing,<br>of contemplation,<br>of perspicacity;"}, {"st": "3.3.23", "t": "impurity,<br>childishness,<br>unintelligence,<br>the dulness that is stupidity,<br>obtuseness, ignorance,<br>a flood of ignorance,<br>the yoke of ignorance,<br>the dependence of ignorance,<br>the being possessed by ignorance,<br>the barrier of ignorance,<br>the dulness that is the root of evil"}, {"st": "3.3.24", "t": "— this is called dulness.<br>These are the three causes of bad [karma].<br>(C) In this connexion,<br>(1062) Which are the three causes of the indeterminate?"}, {"st": "3.3.25", "t": "The absence of lust, hate and dulness coming to pass as the result of good states, or as the indeterminate states known as kiriya-thoughts."}, {"st": "3.3.26", "t": "(1063) Which are the nine causes operative in the sensuous universe (kamavacarahetu)?"}, {"st": "3.3.27", "t": "The three causes of good [karma], the three causes of bad [karma], the three causes of indeterminate [states] — these are the nine."}, {"st": "3.3.28", "t": "(1064) Which are the six causes operative in the universe of form?"}, {"st": "3.3.29", "t": "The three causes of good [karma], the three causes of indeterminate [states] — these are the six."}, {"st": "3.3.30", "t": "(1065) Which are the six causes operative in the universe of the formless?"}, {"st": "3.3.31", "t": "The three causes of good [karma], the three causes of indeterminate [states] — these are the six."}, {"st": "3.3.32", "t": "(1066) Which are the six causes operative in the Unincluded?"}, {"st": "3.3.33", "t": "The three causes of good [karma], the three causes of indeterminate [states] — these are the six."}, {"st": "3.3.34", "t": "In this connexion,<br>(1067) Which are the three causes of good [karma]?<br>The absence of lust, hate and dulness.<br>In this connexion,<br>[1068-1070] What is the absence of lust... of hate... of dulness?"}, {"st": "3.3.35", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 1055-1057, but omitting in § 1056, from ‘hatred’ to ‘the absence of malice’, exclusively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.3.36", "t": "These are the three causes of good [karma].<br>(D) In this connexion,<br>(1071) Which are the three causes of indeterminate [states]?"}, {"st": "3.3.37", "t": "The absence of lust, hate and dulness coming to pass as the effect of good states — these are the three."}, {"st": "3.3.38", "t": "These are the six causes operative in the Unincluded. These are the states which are causes."}, {"st": "3.3.39", "t": "(1072) Which are the states that are not causes?"}, {"st": "3.3.40", "t": "Every state, good, bad and indeterminate, whether related to the worlds of sense, of form, of the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded, except the states enumerated above; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.3.41", "t": "(1073) Which are the states that have causes as concomitants?"}, {"st": "3.3.42", "t": "Those states, to wit, the four skandhas, which have as concomitant causes the states enumerated above."}, {"st": "3.3.43", "t": "[1074J Which are the states that have not concomitant causes?"}, {"st": "3.3.44", "t": "Those states, to wit, the four skandhas, all form also, and uncompounded element, which have not as concomitant causes the states enumerated above."}, {"st": "3.3.45", "t": "(1075) Which are the states that are associated with a cause?"}, {"st": "3.3.46", "t": "The states, to wit, the four skandhas, which are associated with those states enumerated above."}, {"st": "3.3.47", "t": "(1076) Which are the states that are not associated with a cause?"}, {"st": "3.3.48", "t": "The states, to wit, the four skandhas, all form also, and uncompounded element, which are not associated with the states enumerated above."}, {"st": "3.3.49", "t": "(1077) Which are the states that both are causes and have causes as their concomitants?"}, {"st": "3.3.50", "t": "Lust with dulness is both. Dulness with lust is both. Hate with dulness is both. Dulness with hate is both."}, {"st": "3.3.51", "t": "The absence of lust, the absence of hate, the absence of dulness — these also, taken one with the other, both are causes and have causes as their concomitants."}, {"st": "3.3.52", "t": "(1078) Which are the states that have causes as their concomitants, but are not causes?"}, {"st": "3.3.53", "t": "The states, to wit, the four skandhas, which have as their concomitant causes those states [enumerated above as causes], the latter states themselves excepted."}, {"st": "3.3.54", "t": "(1079) Which are the states that are both causes and associated with a cause?"}, {"st": "3.3.55", "t": "(1080) Which are the states that are associated with a cause, but are not causes?"}, {"st": "3.3.56", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those in the foregoing pair.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.3.57", "t": "(1081) Which are the states that are not causes, but have a cause as their concomitant?"}, {"st": "3.3.58", "t": "The states, to wit, the four skandhas, which are not the causes of those states enumerated above, but which have any of them as their concomitants."}, {"st": "3.3.59", "t": "(1082) Which are the states that are not causes and have not causes as their concomitants?"}, {"st": "3.3.60", "t": "The states, to wit, the four skandhas, all form also, and uncompounded element, which neither are the causes of those states enumerated above, nor have any of them as their concomitants."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER III. THE SHORT INTERMEDIATE SET OF PAIRS"}, {"st": "3.4.1", "t": "(1083) Which are the states that are conditioned?"}, {"st": "3.4.2", "t": "The five skandhas, to wit, the skandhas of form, feeling, perception, syntheses and intellect."}, {"st": "3.4.3", "t": "(1084) Which are the states that are unconditioned?<br>‘And uncompounded element’.<br>(1085) Which are the states that are compound?<br>Those states which are conditioned."}, {"st": "3.4.4", "t": "(1086) Which are the states that are uncompounded?<br>That state which is unconditioned.<br>(1087) Which are the states that have visibility?<br>The sphere of [visible] forms."}, {"st": "3.4.5", "t": "(1088) Which are the states that have no visibility?"}, {"st": "3.4.6", "t": "The spheres of the senses and sense-objects; the four skandhas; that form also which, being neither visible nor impingeing, is included under [mental] states; and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.4.7", "t": "(1089) Which are the states that impinge?<br>The spheres of the senses and sense-objects.<br>(1090) Which are the states that are non-impingeing?"}, {"st": "3.4.8", "t": "The four skandhas; that form also which, being neither visible nor impingeing, is included under [mental] states; also uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.4.9", "t": "(1091) Which are the states that have [material] form?"}, {"st": "3.4.10", "t": "The four great principles as well as the form that is derived from the four great phenomena."}, {"st": "3.4.11", "t": "(1092) Which are the states that have no material form?<br>The four skandhas, and uncompounded element.<br>(1093) Which are the states that are mundane?"}, {"st": "3.4.12", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless, to wit, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.4.13", "t": "(1094) Which are the states that are supra-mundane?"}, {"st": "3.4.14", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.4.15", "t": "(1095) Which are the states that are cognizable in one way, and not cognizable in another way?"}, {"st": "3.4.16", "t": "States that are cognizable by sight are not cognizable by hearing; conversely, states that are cognizable by hearing are not cognizable by sight."}, {"st": "3.4.17", "t": "States that are cognizable by sight are not cognizable by smell... by taste... by body-sensibility, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.4.18", "t": "States that are cognizable by hearing are not cognizable by smell... by taste... by bodysensibility... by sight, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.4.19", "t": "So for states that are cognizable by smell, by taste, and by bodysen sibility."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER IV. THE INTOXICANT GROUP"}, {"st": "3.5.1", "t": "(1096) Which are the states that are Intoxicants?"}, {"st": "3.5.2", "t": "The four Intoxicants,<br>to wit,<br>the Intoxicant of sensuality,<br>the Intoxicant of renewed existence,<br>the Intoxicant of speculative opinion,<br>the Intoxicant of ignorance."}, {"st": "3.5.3", "t": "In this connexion"}, {"st": "3.5.4", "t": "(1097) What is the Intoxicant of sensuality?"}, {"st": "3.5.5", "t": "That sensual desire,<br>sensual passion,<br>sensual delight,<br>sensual craving,<br>sensual fondness,<br>sensual thirst,<br>sensual fever,<br>sensual languishing,<br>sensual rapacity,<br>which is excited by the pleasures of the senses"}, {"st": "3.5.6", "t": "— this is called the Intoxicant of sensuality."}, {"st": "3.5.7", "t": "(1098) What is the Intoxicant of renewed existence?"}, {"st": "3.5.8", "t": "The desire,<br>the passion for coming into being,<br>delight in coming into being, craving,<br>fondness for coming into being,<br>the fever,<br>the yearning,<br>the hungering to come into being,<br>which is felt concerning rebirths"}, {"st": "3.5.9", "t": "— this is called the Intoxicant of renewed existence.<br>(1099) What is the Intoxicant of speculative opinion?<br>To hold"}, {"st": "3.5.10", "t": "that the world is eternal,<br>or that it is not eternal, infinite or finite;"}, {"st": "3.5.11", "t": "that the living soul is the body,<br>or that the living soul is a different thing from the body;"}, {"st": "3.5.12", "t": "or that he who has won truth exists after death,<br>or does not exist after death,<br>or both exists and does not exist after death,<br>or neither exists nor does not exist after death"}, {"st": "3.5.13", "t": "— this kind of opinion,<br>this walking in opinion,<br>this jungle of opinion,<br>wilderness of opinion,<br>puppet-show of opinion,<br>scuffling of opinion,<br>the fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path, wrong road, wrongness,<br>this ‘fordingplace’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.5.14", "t": "— this is called the Intoxicant of speculative opinion. Moreover, the Intoxicant of speculation includes all false theories."}, {"st": "3.5.15", "t": "(1100) What is the Intoxicant of ignorance?<br>Answer as in § 1061 for ‘dulness’.<br>These are the states that are Intoxicants.<br>(1102) Which are the states that are not Intoxicants?"}, {"st": "3.5.16", "t": "Every state, good, bad and indeterminate, which is not included in the foregoing (Intoxicants), whether relating to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded, to wit, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.5.17", "t": "(1103) Which are the states that are co-Intoxicant?"}, {"st": "3.5.18", "t": "Good, bad and indeterminate states, whether relating to the worlds of sense, form, or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.5.19", "t": "(1104) Which are the states that are not co-Intoxicant?"}, {"st": "3.5.20", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.5.21", "t": "(1105) Which are the states that are ‘associated with Intoxicants’?"}, {"st": "3.5.22", "t": "The states which are associated with those [Intoxicant] states, to wit, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.5.23", "t": "(1106) Which are the states that are ‘disconnected with Intoxicants’?"}, {"st": "3.5.24", "t": "The states which are disconnected with those [Intoxicant] states, to wit, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.5.25", "t": "(1107) Which are the states that are both Intoxicants and co-Intoxicant?<br>The Intoxicants themselves.<br>(1108) Which are the states that are co-Intoxicant, but not Intoxicants?"}, {"st": "3.5.26", "t": "The states which have the foregoing states (§ 1096) as their concomitants; that is to say, with the exception of the Intoxicants, all states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate, which are co-Intoxicant, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.5.27", "t": "(1109) Which are the states that are both Intoxicants and associated with Intoxicants?"}, {"st": "3.5.28", "t": "The Intoxicant of sensuality together with that of ignorance, and conversely. The Intoxicant of renewed existence together with that of ignorance, and conversely. The Intoxicant of speculative opinion together with that of ignorance, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.5.29", "t": "(1110) Which are the states that are associated with Intoxicants but are not Intoxicants?"}, {"st": "3.5.30", "t": "The states which are associated with the foregoing states (§ 1096) — the latter themselves excepted — to wit, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.5.31", "t": "(1111) Which are the states that are disconnected with Intoxicants but co-Intoxicant?"}, {"st": "3.5.32", "t": "The states which are disconnected with those abovenamed states (§ 1096), but which, good, bad, or indeterminate, have them as concomitants, whether they belong to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless, to wit, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.5.33", "t": "(1112) Which are the states that are disconnected with Intoxicants and are not co -Intoxicant?"}, {"st": "3.5.34", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths and uncompounded element."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER V. THE GROUP OF THE FETTERS"}, {"st": "3.6.1", "t": "(1113) Which are the states that are Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.2", "t": "The ten Fetters, to wit, the Fetter of"}, {"st": "3.6.3", "t": "sensuality,<br>repulsion,<br>conceit,<br>speculative opinion,<br>perplexity,<br>the contagion of mere rule and ritual,<br>the passion for renewed existence,<br>envy,<br>meanness,<br>ignorance."}, {"st": "3.6.4", "t": "In this connexion,"}, {"st": "3.6.5", "t": "(1114) What is the Fetter of sensual passion?"}, {"st": "3.6.6", "t": "That sensual desire,<br>sensual passion,<br>sensual delight,<br>sensual craving,<br>sensual fondness,<br>sensual fever,<br>sensual languor,<br>sensual rapacity,"}, {"st": "3.6.7", "t": "which is excited by the pleasures of the senses<br>— this is called the Fetter of sensuality.<br>(1115) What is the Fetter of repulsion?<br><i>Answer as for ‘hate’, § 1060.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.6.8", "t": "(1116) What is the Fetter of conceit?"}, {"st": "3.6.9", "t": "Conceit at the thought ‘I am the better man’;<br>conceit at the thought ‘I am as good [as they]’;<br>conceit at the thought ‘I am lowly’"}, {"st": "3.6.10", "t": "— all such sort of"}, {"st": "3.6.11", "t": "conceit,<br>overweening,<br>conceitedness,<br>loftiness,<br>haughtiness,<br>flaunting a flag,<br>assumption,<br>desire of the heart for self-advertisement"}, {"st": "3.6.12", "t": "— this is called conceit."}, {"st": "3.6.13", "t": "(1117) What is the Fetter of speculative opinion?"}, {"st": "3.6.14", "t": "<i>Answer as for the ‘Intoxicant of speculative opinion’, § 1099, with this supplement:<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.6.15", "t": "And, with the exception of the ‘Fetter of the contagion of mere rule and ritual’, all wrong views are included in the Fetter of speculative opinion."}, {"st": "3.6.16", "t": "(1118) What is the Fetter of perplexity?<br><i>Answer as for ‘perplexity’, § 1004.<\/i><br>(1119) What is the Fetter of the contagion of mere rule and ritual?<br><i>Ansiver as for the ‘contagion of’, etc. § 1005.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.6.17", "t": "(1120) What is the Fetter of the passion for renewed existence?<br><i>Answer as for the ‘Intoxicant of renewed existence’, § 1098.<\/i><br>(1121) What is the Fetter of envy (issasannojanam)?"}, {"st": "3.6.18", "t": "Envy,<br>envying,<br>enviousness — jealousy,<br>the expression and mood of jealousy at the gifts,<br>the hospitality,<br>the respect,<br>affection,<br>reverence and worship accruing to others"}, {"st": "3.6.19", "t": "— this is called the Fetter of envy."}, {"st": "3.6.20", "t": "(1122) What is the Fetter of meanness (maccharisannojanam)?"}, {"st": "3.6.21", "t": "The Five Meannesses,<br>to wit,<br>meanness as regards dwelling,<br>families,<br>gifts,<br>reputation,<br>doctrine"}, {"st": "3.6.22", "t": "— all this sort of"}, {"st": "3.6.23", "t": "meanness,<br>grudging,<br>mean spirit,<br>avarice and ignobleness,<br>niggardliness and want of generosity of heart"}, {"st": "3.6.24", "t": "— this is called the Fetter of meanness.<br>(1123) What is the Fetter of ignorance?<br><i>Answer as for the Intoxicant of ignorance, § 1100.<\/i><br>These are the states that are Fetters."}, {"st": "3.6.25", "t": "(1124) Which are the states that are not Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.26", "t": "Every state, good, bad and indeterminate, which is not included in the foregoing [ten] states, whether it relates to the worlds of sense, or of form, or of the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.6.27", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.6.28", "t": "(1125) (a) favourable to the Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.29", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, relating to the worlds of sense, form, or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.6.30", "t": "(1126) (b) unfavourable to the Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.31", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.6.32", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.6.33", "t": "(1127) (a) associated with the Fetters? (1128) (b) disconnected with the Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.34", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those given to corresponding questions respecting the ‘Intoxicants’, §§ 1105, 1106.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.6.35", "t": "Which are the states that are<br>(1129) (a) both Fetters and favourable to the Fetters?<br>The Fetters themselves are both<br>(1130) (b) favourable to the Fetters but not themselves Fetters."}, {"st": "3.6.36", "t": "The states which are favourable to those [ten] states afore-named; that is to say, with the exception of the Fetters themselves, all co-Intoxicant states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate, whether relating to the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.6.37", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.6.38", "t": "(1131) (a) both Fetters and associated with Fetters?"}, {"st": "3.6.39", "t": "The Fetter of sensuality in conjunction with the Fetter of ignorance, and conversely, is both. So is any one of the remaining eight Fetters when in conjunction with the Fetter of ignorance, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.6.40", "t": "(1132) (b) associated with the Fetters but not a Fetter?"}, {"st": "3.6.41", "t": "The states which are associated with those ten states afore-named, with the exception of the Fetters themselves; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.6.42", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.6.43", "t": "(1133) (a) disconnected with the Fetters yet favourable to them?"}, {"st": "3.6.44", "t": "The states which are disconnected with those aforementioned [ten] states, that is to say, good, bad and indeterminate states which are co-Intoxicant, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, or of form, or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.6.45", "t": "(1134) (b) disconnected with the Fetters and not favourable to them?"}, {"st": "3.6.46", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.6.47", "t": "(arahatta) path,<br>the Fetters of speculative opinion, perplexity, and the contagion of mere rule and ritual, in the first (sotapatti) path,<br>the Fetter of the passion for renewed existence in the fourth path,<br>the Fetters of envy and meanness in the first path,<br>the Fetter of ignorance in the fourth path."}, {"st": "3.6.48", "t": "Hence the second path seems, according to Buddhaghosa, to constitute an interim in the breaking of Fetters."}, {"st": "3.6.49", "t": "The following tables show how far the Dh. S. and its Cy. agree with the authorities quoted in Childers, s.v. samyojanam (cf. D. i. 156):"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VI. THE GROUP OF THE TIES"}, {"st": "3.7.1", "t": "(1135) Which are the states that are Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.2", "t": "The four Ties,<br>to wit,<br>the bodily Tie of covetousness,<br>the bodily Tie of ill-will,<br>the bodily Tie of the contagion of mere rule and ritual,<br>the bodily Tie of the inclination to dogmatize."}, {"st": "3.7.3", "t": "In this connexion,<br>(1136) what is the bodily Tie of covetousness (abijjha kayagantho)?<br><i>Answer as for ‘lust’, § 1059.<\/i><br>(1137) what is the bodily Tie of ill-will (vyapado kayagantho)?"}, {"st": "3.7.4", "t": "<i>Answer as for ‘hate’, § 1060.<\/i><br>(1138) what is the bodily Tie of the contagion of mere rule and ritual?<br><i>Answer as in § 1005, and, § 1119, for the Fetter so-called.<\/i><br>(1139) what is the bodily Tie of the inclination to dogmatize?"}, {"st": "3.7.5", "t": "‘“The world is eternal” — this is true, all else is false!<br>“The world is not eternal” — this is true, all else is false!<br>“The world is finite... is infinite” — this is true, all else is false!<br>“The living soul is the body... is a different thing from the body” — this is true, all else is false!<br>“He who has won truth exists after death... does not exist after death... both exists and does not exist after death... neither exists nor does not exist after death” — this is true, all else is false!’"}, {"st": "3.7.6", "t": "— this kind of opinion,<br>this walking in opinion,<br>this jungle of opinion,<br>wilderness of opinion,<br>puppet-show of opinion,<br>scuffling of opinion,<br>this Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path,<br>wrong road,<br>wrongness,<br>this ‘fording-place’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.7.7", "t": "— this is called the bodily Tie of the inclination to dogmatize."}, {"st": "3.7.8", "t": "And, excepting only the bodily Tie of the contagion of mere rule and ritual, all wrong views are included under the bodily Tie of the inclination to dogmatize."}, {"st": "3.7.9", "t": "These are the states which are Ties."}, {"st": "3.7.10", "t": "(1140) Which are the states that are not Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.11", "t": "Every state, good, bad and indeterminate, which is not included in the foregoing [four] states, whether it relates to the worlds of sense or of form, or of the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.7.12", "t": "Which are the states that"}, {"st": "3.7.13", "t": "(1141) (a) tend to become tied?"}, {"st": "3.7.14", "t": "Good, bad and indeterminate states, relating to the worlds of sense, of form or of the formless, which are co-Intoxicant; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.7.15", "t": "(1142) (b) do not tend to become tied?"}, {"st": "3.7.16", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.7.17", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.7.18", "t": "(1143) (a) associated with the Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.19", "t": "The states connected with those four afore-named states; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.7.20", "t": "(1144) (b) disconnected with the Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.21", "t": "The states which are disconnected with those [four aforenamed] states; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.7.22", "t": "Which are the states that<br>(1145) (a) are themselves Ties and tend to become tied?<br>The Ties themselves are both.<br>(1146) (b) tend to become tied, but are not Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.23", "t": "The states which tend to become tied by those [four afore-named] states, that is, every state, good, bad and indeterminate, which is not included in the latter, whether it relates to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.7.24", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.7.25", "t": "(1147) (a) Ties themselves, and associated with the Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.26", "t": "The bodily Tie of the contagion of mere rule and ritual in conjunction with the bodily Tie of covetousness, and conversely, is both. The bodily Tie of the inclination to dogmatize in conjunction with the bodily Tie of covetousness, and conversely, is both."}, {"st": "3.7.27", "t": "(1148) (b) associated with the Ties but not Ties?"}, {"st": "3.7.28", "t": "The states which are associated with the four states aforenamed (the Ties), the latter themselves excepted; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.7.29", "t": "Which are the states that"}, {"st": "3.7.30", "t": "(1149) (a) are disconnected with the Ties, hut tend to become tied?"}, {"st": "3.7.31", "t": "The states which are disconnected with the afore-named states, that is, good, bad and indeterminate states relating to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless, which are co-Intoxicant; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.7.32", "t": "(1150) (c) are disconnected with the Ties and do not tend to become tied?"}, {"st": "3.7.33", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VII. THE GROUP OF THE FLOODS"}, {"st": "3.8.1", "t": "(1151) Which are the states that are Floods?"}, {"st": "3.8.2", "t": "<i>... [continue as in the Group of Fetters.]<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER VIII. THE GROUP OF THE BONDS"}, {"st": "3.9.1", "t": "[1151a] Which are the states that are Bonds?"}, {"st": "3.9.2", "t": "<i>... [continue as in the Group of Fetters].<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER IX. THE GROUP OF THE HINDRANCES"}, {"st": "3.10.1", "t": "(1152) Which are the states that are Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.2", "t": "The six Hindrances,<br>to wit,<br>the Hindrance of sensual desire,<br>the Hindrance of ill will,<br>the Hindrance of stolidity and torpor,<br>the Hindrance of excitement and worry,<br>the Hindrance of perplexity,<br>the Hindrance of ignorance."}, {"st": "3.10.3", "t": "In this connexion<br>(1153) What is the Hmclrance of sensual desire?<br><i>Answer as for the ‘Intoxicant of sensuality’, § 1097.<\/i><br>(1154) What is the Hindrance of ill will?"}, {"st": "3.10.4", "t": "<i>Answer as for the ‘Tie of ill will’, § 1137.<\/i><br>(1155) What is the Hindrance of stolidity and torpor?<br>First distinguish between stolidity and torpor.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1156) What is stolidity?"}, {"st": "3.10.5", "t": "That which is indisposition, unwieldiness of intellect;<br>adhering and cohering;<br>clinging, cleaving to, stickiness;<br>stolidity, that is, a stiffening, a rigidity of the intellect"}, {"st": "3.10.6", "t": "— this is called stolidity."}, {"st": "3.10.7", "t": "(1157) What is torpor?"}, {"st": "3.10.8", "t": "That which is indisposition and unwieldiness of sense, a shrouding, enveloping, barricading within;<br>torpor that is sleep, drowsiness;<br>sleep, slumbering, somnolence"}, {"st": "3.10.9", "t": "— this is called torpor."}, {"st": "3.10.10", "t": "Now this is the stolidity and this is the torpor which are called ‘the Hindrance of stolidity and torpor’."}, {"st": "3.10.11", "t": "(1159) What is the Hindrance of excitement and worry?<br>First distinguish between ‘excitement’ and ‘worry’.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1160) What is excitement?"}, {"st": "3.10.12", "t": "That excitement of mind which is disquietude, agitation of heart, turmoil of mind"}, {"st": "3.10.13", "t": "— this is called excitement."}, {"st": "3.10.14", "t": "(1161) What is worry?"}, {"st": "3.10.15", "t": "Consciousness of what is lawful in something that is unlawful;<br>consciousness of what is unlawful in something that is lawful;<br>consciousness of what is immoral in something that is moral;<br>consciousness of what is moral in something that is immoral"}, {"st": "3.10.16", "t": "— all this sort of worry,<br>fidgeting,<br>overscrupulousness,<br>remorse of conscience,<br>mental scarifying"}, {"st": "3.10.17", "t": "— this is what is called Worry."}, {"st": "3.10.18", "t": "Now this is the excitement and this is the worry which are what is called ‘the Hindrance of excitement and worry’."}, {"st": "3.10.19", "t": "(1162) What is the Hindrance of ignorance?<br><i>Answer as for ‘dulness’, § 1061.<\/i><br>(1163) Which are the states that are not Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.20", "t": "Every state, good, bad and indeterminate, which is not included in the foregoing [six] states, whether it relates to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.10.21", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.10.22", "t": "(1164) (a) favourable to the Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.23", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, whether relating to the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.10.24", "t": "(1165) (b) unfavourable to the Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.25", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.10.26", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.10.27", "t": "(1166) (a) associated with the Hindrances? (1167) (b) disconnected with the Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.28", "t": "<i>Answers identical with those given to corresponding questions respecting the Intoxicants, §§ 1105, 1106.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.10.29", "t": "Which are the states that are<br>(1168) (a) Hindrances themselves and favourable to the Hindrances?<br>The Hindrances themselves are both.<br>(1169) (b) favourable to the Hindrances, but not themselves Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.30", "t": "The states which are favourable to the Hindrances afore named; that is to say, with the exception of the Hindrances, all co-Intoxicant states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.10.31", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.10.32", "t": "(1170) (a) both themselves Hhidrances and associated with Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.33", "t": "The following pairs are both themselves Hindrances and associated with Hindrances:"}, {"st": "3.10.34", "t": "Sensual desire in conjunction with ignorance, and conversely.<br>Ill-will in conjunction with ignorance, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.10.35", "t": "(1171) (b) associated with Hindrances, hut not themselves Hindrances?"}, {"st": "3.10.36", "t": "The states which are associated with the [six aforementioned] states, the latter themselves being excepted; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.10.37", "t": "Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.10.38", "t": "(1172) (a) disconnected with the Hindrances, hut favourable to them?"}, {"st": "3.10.39", "t": "The states which are disconnected with those [six] states afore-named, that is to say, co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.10.40", "t": "(1173) (b) disconnected with the Hindrances and unfavourable to them?"}, {"st": "3.10.41", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER X. THE GROUP ON CONTAGION"}, {"st": "3.11.1", "t": "(1174) Which are the states that are contagious?<br>The Contagion of speculative opinion.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1175) What is the ‘Contagion of speculative opinion’?"}, {"st": "3.11.2", "t": "<i>Answer as for the ‘Intoxicant of speculative opinion,’<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.11.3", "t": "viz.: ‘To hold that the world is eternal, or that it is not eternal’, etc. (§ 1099)."}, {"st": "3.11.4", "t": "(1176) Which are the states that are not a Contagion?<br><i>Answer as in the case of the ‘states that are not Hindrances’ (§ 1163).<\/i><br>[1177, 1178] Which are the states that are<br>(a) infected? (b) uninfected?"}, {"st": "3.11.5", "t": "<i>Answers as in the corresponding answers relating to the Hindrances (§§ 1164, 1165).<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.11.6", "t": "[1179, 1180] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.11.7", "t": "(a) associated with the Contagion? (b) disconnected with the Contagion?"}, {"st": "3.11.8", "t": "<i>Answers as in the corresponding ansivers relating to the Hindrances (§§ 1166, 1167).<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.11.9", "t": "[1181, 1182] Which are the states that are<br>(a) themselves Contagious and infected?<br>The Contagion itself is both.<br>(b) infected but not Contagious?"}, {"st": "3.11.10", "t": "The states which are infected by the states afore-named; that is to say, with the exception of the latter, all co-Intoxicant states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form, or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.11.11", "t": "[1183, 1184] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.11.12", "t": "(a) disconnected with the Contagion, yet infected? (b) disconnected with the Contagion and uninfected?"}, {"st": "3.11.13", "t": "<i>Answers as in the corresponding sections on the Hindrances (§§ 1172, 1173).<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER XI. THE GREAT INTERMEDIATE SET OF PAIRS"}, {"st": "3.12.1", "t": "[1185, 1186] Which are the states that have<br>(a) a concomitant object of thought?<br>The four skandhas.<br>(b) no concomitant object of thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.2", "t": "All form, and uncompounded element.<br>[1187, 1188] Which are the states that are<br>(a) of the intellect?"}, {"st": "3.12.3", "t": "Cognition applied to sense-impressions; the element of ideation and the element of ideational cognition."}, {"st": "3.12.4", "t": "(b) not of the intellect?"}, {"st": "3.12.5", "t": "The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses; all form, moreover, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.12.6", "t": "[1189, 1190] Which are the states that are<br>(a) involved in the life of sense?<br>The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses.<br>(b) not involved in the life of sense?"}, {"st": "3.12.7", "t": "Intellect and all form and uncompounded element.<br>[1191, 1192] Which are the states that are<br>(a) associated tvith thought?<br>The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses."}, {"st": "3.12.8", "t": "(b) disconnected with thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.9", "t": "All form and uncompounded element. (Thought must not be described as associated or disconnected with itself.)"}, {"st": "3.12.10", "t": "[1193, 1194] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.12.11", "t": "(a) conjoined with thought?<br>(b) detached from thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.12", "t": "<i>Answers as in §§ 1191, 1192 respectively, (Thought must not be described as conjoined with, or detached from itself.)<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.12.13", "t": "[1195, 1196] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.12.14", "t": "(a) sprung from thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.15", "t": "The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses; bodily and vocal intimation; or whatever other form there be which is born of thought, caused by thought, sprung from thought, whether it be in the spheres of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, or the tangible, the elements of space or fluidity, the lightness, plasticity or wieldiness of form, the integration or subsistence of form, or bodily nutriment."}, {"st": "3.12.16", "t": "(b) not sprung from thought?<br>Thought; also every other kind of form, and uncompounded element.<br>[1197, 1198] Which are the states that<br>(a) come into being together with thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.17", "t": "The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses; bodily and vocal intimation."}, {"st": "3.12.18", "t": "(b) do not come into being together with thought?<br>Thought; also all other kinds of form, and uncompounded element.<br>[1199, 1200] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.12.19", "t": "(a) consecutive to thought?<br>(b) not consecutive to thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.20", "t": "<i>Answers as in the two foregoing answers respectively,<\/i><br>[1201, 1202] Which are the states that are<br>(a) conjoined with aiid sprung from thought?<br>The skandhas of feeling, perception and syntheses."}, {"st": "3.12.21", "t": "(b) not conjoined with and sprung from thought?<br>Thought itself; all form also, and uncompounded element.<br>[1203, 1204] Which are the states that"}, {"st": "3.12.22", "t": "(a) are conjoined with and sprung from, and that come into being together loith, thought? (b) are not such as are conjoined with and sprung from and as come into being together with thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.23", "t": "<i>Answers as in the two foregoing answers respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.12.24", "t": "[1205, 1206] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.12.25", "t": "(a) conjoined with and sprung from and consecutive to thought? (b) not conjoined with, sprung from and consecutive to thought?"}, {"st": "3.12.26", "t": "<i>Answers as in the two foregoing ansivers.<\/i><br>[1207, 1208] Which are the states that are<br>(a) of the self?<br>The spheres of the five senses and of ideation."}, {"st": "3.12.27", "t": "(b) external?<br>The spheres of the five objects of sense and of ideas.<br>[1209, 1210] Which are the states that are<br>(a) derived?"}, {"st": "3.12.28", "t": "The spheres of the five senses... and bodily nutriment.<br>(b) not derived?<br>The four skandhas, the four great phenomena and uncompounded element.<br>[1211-1212] Which are the states that are<br>(a) the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "3.12.29", "t": "Co-Intoxicant good and bad states, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless; in other words, the four skandhas, and such form as is due to karma having been wrought."}, {"st": "3.12.30", "t": "(b) not the issue of grasping?"}, {"st": "3.12.31", "t": "Co-Intoxicant good and bad states, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless; in other words, the four skandhas; also such kiriya-thoughts as are neither good, nor bad, nor the effects of karma; the Paths, moreover, that are the Unincluded and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER XII. THE GROUP ON GRASPING"}, {"st": "3.13.1", "t": "(1213) Which are the states that have the attribute of Grasping?<br>The four Graspings: —"}, {"st": "3.13.2", "t": "the Grasping after sense,"}, {"st": "3.13.3", "t": "the Grasping after speculative opinion,"}, {"st": "3.13.4", "t": "the Grasping after mere rule and ritual,"}, {"st": "3.13.5", "t": "the Grasping after a theory of soul.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1214) What is the Grasping after sense?"}, {"st": "3.13.6", "t": "That sensual desire,<br>sensual passion,<br>sensual delight,<br>sensual craving,<br>sensual cleaving,<br>sensual fever,<br>sensual languishing,<br>sensual rapacity,"}, {"st": "3.13.7", "t": "which is excited by the pleasures of the senses."}, {"st": "3.13.8", "t": "(1215) What is the Grasping after speculative opinion?"}, {"st": "3.13.9", "t": "‘There is no such thing as alms, or sacrifice, or offering;<br>there is neither fruit, nor result of good, or of evil deeds;<br>there is no such thing as this world, or the next;<br>there is no such thing as mother or father, or beings springing into birth without them;<br>there are in the world no recluses or brahmins who have reached the highest point, who have attained the height, who, having understood and realized by themselves alone both this world and the next, make known the same’"}, {"st": "3.13.10", "t": "— all this sort of speculation,<br>this walking in opinion,<br>wilderness of opinion,<br>puppet-show of opinion,<br>scuffling of opinion,<br>this Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path,<br>wrong road,<br>wrongness,<br>this ‘fording-place’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.13.11", "t": "— this is what is called the Grasping after speculative opinion."}, {"st": "3.13.12", "t": "And with the exception of the Graspings after mere rule and ritual and after soul-theory, all wrong views are included in the Grasping after speculative opinion."}, {"st": "3.13.13", "t": "(1216) What is the Grasping after mere rule and ritual?<br><i>Answer as for the ‘Contagion of mere rule and ritual’, § 1005.<\/i><br>(1217) What is the Grasping after soultheory?<br><i>Ansiver as for the ‘Theory of individuality’, § 1003.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.13.14", "t": "(1218) Which are the states that have not the attribute of Grasping?"}, {"st": "3.13.15", "t": "All other states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate (except the foregoing), whether they relate to the worlds of sense, or of form, or of the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.13.16", "t": "(1219) Which are the states that are favourable Grasping?"}, {"st": "3.13.17", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.13.18", "t": "(1220) Which are the states that are not favourable to Grasping?"}, {"st": "3.13.19", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths; and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.13.20", "t": "[1221-1224] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.13.21", "t": "(a) associated with Grasping?<br>(b) disconnected with Grasping?<br>(c) Grasping and also favourable to Grasping?<br>(d) favourable to Grasping but not Grasping?"}, {"st": "3.13.22", "t": "<i>Ansivers exactly analogous to those given to corresponding questions in other Groups, e.g. §§ 1125, 1141, 1164.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.13.23", "t": "[1225-1228] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.13.24", "t": "(a) both Grasping and associated with Grasping?"}, {"st": "3.13.25", "t": "The Grasping after speculation in conjunction with that after sense is both, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.13.26", "t": "So is each of the other tivo Grasping s in conjunction with that after sense, and conversely."}, {"st": "3.13.27", "t": "(b) associated with Grasping but not Grasping?<br>(c) disconnected with Grasping yet favourable to it?<br>(d) disconnected with Grasping and not favourable to it?"}, {"st": "3.13.28", "t": "<i>Ansivers as in the Groups specified above, §§ 1125, 1141, 1164 et seq.<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER XIII. THE GROUP ON THE CORRUPTIONS"}, {"st": "3.14.1", "t": "(1229) Which are the states that have the attribute of corruption?"}, {"st": "3.14.2", "t": "The ten bases of corruption, to wit:"}, {"st": "3.14.3", "t": "lust, perplexity,<br>hate, stolidity,<br>dulness, excitement,<br>conceit, unconscientiousness,<br>speculative opinion, disregard of blame."}, {"st": "3.14.4", "t": "[1230-1237] In this connexion,"}, {"st": "3.14.5", "t": "What is lust?<br>... hate?<br>... dulness?<br>... conceit?<br>... speculative opinion?<br>... perplexity?<br>... stolidity?<br>... excitement?"}, {"st": "3.14.6", "t": "<i>Answers as m §§ 1059-1061, 1116-1118, 1156, 1159.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.14.7", "t": "(1238) What is unconscientiousness?"}, {"st": "3.14.8", "t": "The absence of any feeling of conscientious scruple when scruples ought to be felt; the absence of conscientious scruple at attaining to bad and evil states."}, {"st": "3.14.9", "t": "(1239) What is disregard of blame?"}, {"st": "3.14.10", "t": "The absence of any sense of guilt where a sense of guilt ought to be felt, the absence of a sense of guilt at attaining to bad and evil states."}, {"st": "3.14.11", "t": "These are the states which have the attribute of corruption."}, {"st": "3.14.12", "t": "(1240) Which are the states that have not the attribute of corruption?"}, {"st": "3.14.13", "t": "All other states whatever (i.e. all except the afore-named ten), good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas, all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.14.14", "t": "[1241, 1242] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.14.15", "t": "(a) baneful?"}, {"st": "3.14.16", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.14.17", "t": "(b) harmless?"}, {"st": "3.14.18", "t": "The Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.14.19", "t": "[1243, 1243a] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.14.20", "t": "(a) corrupt?"}, {"st": "3.14.21", "t": "The three roots of bad (karma), to wit, lust, hate, dulness, as well as the Corruptions united with them, the four skandhas associated with them, and the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them."}, {"st": "3.14.22", "t": "(b) not corrupt?"}, {"st": "3.14.23", "t": "Good and indeterminate states, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form, or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.14.24", "t": "[1244-1247] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.14.25", "t": "(a) associated with the Corruptions?<br>(b) disconnected with the Corruptions?"}, {"st": "3.14.26", "t": "(c) both Corruptions and baneful?<br>(d) banefid but not themselves Corruptions?"}, {"st": "3.14.27", "t": "<i>Answers as in the corresponding pairs in the ‘Graspings’, §§ 1221-1224.<\/i><br>[1248, 1249] Which are the states that are<br>(a) both Corruptions and corrupt?<br>The Corruptions themselves."}, {"st": "3.14.28", "t": "(b) corrupt but not themselves Corruptions?"}, {"st": "3.14.29", "t": "The states which by those ten states are made corrupt, the ten themselves excepted; in other words, the four skandhas."}, {"st": "3.14.30", "t": "[1250-1253] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.14.31", "t": "(a) both Corruptions and associated with Corruptions?"}, {"st": "3.14.32", "t": "Lust,<br>hate,<br>conceit,<br>speculative<br>opinion,<br>perplexity,<br>stolidity,<br>excitement,<br>unconscientiousness<br>and disregard of blame"}, {"st": "3.14.33", "t": "taken severally in conjunction with dulness, and dulness in conjunction with each of them."}, {"st": "3.14.34", "t": "Lust, also, in conjunction with excitement, and conversely, and so for the remaining eight bases.<br>Lust, also, in conjunction with unconscientiousness, and conversely, and so for the remaining eight.<br>Lust, also, in conjunction with disregard of blame, and conversely, and so for the remaining eight."}, {"st": "3.14.35", "t": "(b) associated with Corruptions but not themselves Corruptions?<br>(c) disconnected with Ills but baneful?<br>(d) disconnected with Ills and harmless?"}, {"st": "3.14.36", "t": "<i>Answers to these three questions as in former groups. See §§ 1226-1228, 1171-1173, etc.<\/i>"}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "CHAPTER XIV. THE SUPPLEMENTARY SET OF PAIRS"}, {"st": "3.15.1", "t": "(1254) Which are the states that are to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.15.2", "t": "The Three Fetters, to wit: theory of individuahty, perplexity and the contagion of mere rule and ritual."}, {"st": "3.15.3", "t": "[1255-1257]<br><i>Identical with §§ 1003-1006.<\/i><br>(1258) Which are the states that are not to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.15.4", "t": "All states whatever, good, bad and indeterminate, except the three afore-mentioned, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.15.5", "t": "[1259, 1260] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.15.6", "t": "(a) to he put aivay by cultivation?"}, {"st": "3.15.7", "t": "All the remaining lust, hate and dulness as well as the Corruptions united with them, the four skandhas associated with them, and the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them."}, {"st": "3.15.8", "t": "(b) not to he put away by cultivation?<br>Answer as for those states which are not to he put away by insight [§ 1258].<br>(1261) Which are the states the causes of which are to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.15.9", "t": "The Three Fetters, to wit: theory of individuality, perplexity and the contagion of mere rule and ritual."}, {"st": "3.15.10", "t": "[1262-1264]"}, {"st": "3.15.11", "t": "<i>Identical with §§ 1003-1006.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.15.12", "t": "(1265) Which are the states the causes of which are not to be put away by insight?"}, {"st": "3.15.13", "t": "<i>Answer as for those ‘states which are not to be put away by insight’, § 1258.<\/i><br>[1266, 1267] Which are the states the causes of which are<br>(a) to he put away by cultivation?"}, {"st": "3.15.14", "t": "All the remaining lust, hate and dulness: these are the causes that are to be put away by cultivation. And the Corruptions united with them, the four skandhas associated with them, and the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them, are the states the causes of which are to be put away by cultivation."}, {"st": "3.15.15", "t": "(b) not to he put away by cultivation?"}, {"st": "3.15.16", "t": "<i>Ansiver as for the ‘ states which are not to be put away by cultivation’, § 1260.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.15.17", "t": "[1268-1271] Which are the states"}, {"st": "3.15.18", "t": "(a) ‘wherein conception works’?"}, {"st": "3.15.19", "t": "The four skandhas when associated with conception (the latter not being included), [which springs up] in a soil wherein conception works, either in the worlds of sense or form, or in the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.15.20", "t": "(b) ‘void of the working of conception’?"}, {"st": "3.15.21", "t": "The four skandhas when springing up in a soil void of conception, either in the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or in the life that is Unincluded; conception itself also, and all form and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.15.22", "t": "(c) ‘wherein works thought discursive’?<br>(d) ‘void of the working of thought discursive’?"}, {"st": "3.15.23", "t": "<i>Answers (substituting ‘discursive thought’ for ‘conception’) as in §§ 1268, 1269 respectively.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.15.24", "t": "[1272-1277] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.15.25", "t": "(a) joyous?<br>(b) not joyous?"}, {"st": "3.15.26", "t": "(c) accompanied by joy?<br>(d) unaccompanied by joy?"}, {"st": "3.15.27", "t": "(e) accompanied by ease?<br>(f) unaccompanied by ease?"}, {"st": "3.15.28", "t": "<i>Answers to each pair of questions analogous to those in §§ 1268, 1269, ‘joy’ or ‘ease’ being substituted in due order for ‘conception’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.15.29", "t": "[1278, 1279] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.15.30", "t": "(a) accompanied by disinterestedness?"}, {"st": "3.15.31", "t": "The three skandhas of perception, syntheses and intellect, when associated with disinterestedness (the latter not being included), [which springs up] in a soil congenial to it, either in the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or in the life that is Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.15.32", "t": "(b) unaccompanied by disinterestedness?"}, {"st": "3.15.33", "t": "The four skandhas [when springing up] in a soil uncongenial to disinterestedness, either in the worlds of sense or form, or in the life that is Unincluded; disinterestedness itself also, and all form and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.15.34", "t": "[1281-1287] Which are the states that"}, {"st": "3.15.35", "t": "(a) relate (belong) to the universe of sense?"}, {"st": "3.15.36", "t": "Take from the waveless deep of woe beneath up to the heaven above of the Parinimittavasavatti gods inclusive — then whatever has there its range, and is therein included, whether it be skandha, element or sphere — form, feeling, perception, syntheses, intellect: these are states that relate (belong) to the universe of sense."}, {"st": "3.15.37", "t": "(b) do not relate (belong) to the universe of sense?<br>The universe of form, that of the formless and the life that is Unincluded.<br>(c) relate (belong) to the universe of form?"}, {"st": "3.15.38", "t": "Take from the Brahma-world below up to the heaven above of the Akanittha gods inclusive — then whatever states, both of sense and intellect, have therein their range and are therein included, whether they are states of one who has attained [Jhana having potential good], or of one in whom [resultant Jhana] has arisen, or of one living happily under present conditions."}, {"st": "3.15.39", "t": "(d) do not relate (belong) to the universe of form?<br>The universe of sense, that of the formless and the life that is Unincluded.<br>(e) relate (belong) to the universe of the formless?"}, {"st": "3.15.40", "t": "Take from the entrance among the gods of ‘the sphere of infinite space’ as the lower limit, and up to the entrance above among the gods of ‘the sphere where there is neither perception nor non-perception’ — then whatever states, both of sense and intellect, have therein their range and are therein included, whether they are states of one who has attained [Jhana having potential good], or of one in whom [resultant Jhana] has arisen, or of one living happily under present conditions: these are states that relate (belong) to the universe of the formless."}, {"st": "3.15.41", "t": "(f) do not relate (belong) to the universe of the formless?<br>The universe of sense, that of form, and the life that is Unincluded.<br>(g) belong to the Included?"}, {"st": "3.15.42", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.15.43", "t": "(h) belong to the Unincluded?<br>The Paths, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element.<br>[1288, 1289] Which are the states by which<br>(a) there is a going away?"}, {"st": "3.15.44", "t": "The four Paths that are the Unincluded.<br>(b) there is no going away?"}, {"st": "3.15.45", "t": "All states, good, bad and indeterminate, except those four, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the Hfe that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.15.46", "t": "[1290, 1291] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.15.47", "t": "(a) fired in their consequences?"}, {"st": "3.15.48", "t": "The five acts that have immediate results, and those wrong views that are fixed in their consequences; the four Paths also that are the Unincluded."}, {"st": "3.15.49", "t": "(b) not fixed in their consequences?<br>Answer as in § 1289.<br>[1292, 1293] Which are the states that have<br>(a) something beyond?"}, {"st": "3.15.50", "t": "Co-Intoxicant states, good, bad and indeterminate, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, of form, or of the formless; in other words, the five skandhas."}, {"st": "3.15.51", "t": "(b) no ‘beyond’?"}, {"st": "3.15.52", "t": "The four Paths that are the Unincluded, and the Fruits of the Paths, and uncompounded element."}, {"st": "3.15.53", "t": "[1294, 1295] Which are the states that are"}, {"st": "3.15.54", "t": "(a) concomitant with war?"}, {"st": "3.15.55", "t": "The three roots of bad (karma): lust, hate and dulness — and the Corruptions united with them; the four skandhas associated with them; the action, bodily, vocal and mental, springing from them."}, {"st": "3.15.56", "t": "(b) not concomitant with war?"}, {"st": "3.15.57", "t": "Good, bad and indeterminate states, whether they relate to the worlds of sense, form or the formless, or to the life that is Unincluded; in other words, the four skandhas; all form also, and uncompounded element."}, {"sp": "SEC", "t": "PART II. THE SUTTANTA PAIRS OF TERMS"}, {"st": "3.16.1", "t": "[1296, 1297] Which are the states that<br>(a) partake of wisdom?<br>States which are the associates of wisdom.<br>(b) partake of ignorance?"}, {"st": "3.16.2", "t": "States which are the associates of ignorance.<br>[1298, 1299] Which are the states that have<br>(a) the likeness of lightning?<br>The science of the three lowest of the Noble Paths."}, {"st": "3.16.3", "t": "(b) the likeness of the thunderbolt?<br>The science of the topmost Path, the Path of Arahatship.<br>[1300, 1301] Which are the states that are<br>(a) foolish?"}, {"st": "3.16.4", "t": "Unconscientiousness and disregard of blame. Besides, all bad states are foolish."}, {"st": "3.16.5", "t": "(b) discreet?<br>Conscientiousness and fear of blame. Besides, all good states are discreet.<br>[1302, 1303] Which are the states that are<br>(a) dark?"}, {"st": "3.16.6", "t": "Unconscientiousness and disregard of blame. Besides, all bad states are dark.<br>(b) bright?<br>Conscientiousness and fear of blame. Besides, all good states are bright.<br>[1304, 1305] Which are the states that<br>(a) conduce to remorse?"}, {"st": "3.16.7", "t": "Misconduct in act, word and thought. Besides, all bad states conduce to remorse."}, {"st": "3.16.8", "t": "(b) do not conduce to remorse?"}, {"st": "3.16.9", "t": "Good conduct in act, word and thought. Besides, no good states conduce to remorse."}, {"st": "3.16.10", "t": "(1306) Which are the states that are synonymous?"}, {"st": "3.16.11", "t": "That which is an enumeration, that which is a designation, an expression, a current term, a name, a denomination, the assigning of a name, an interpretation, a distinctive mark of discourse on this or that state."}, {"st": "3.16.12", "t": "[1306a] All states are processes of synonymous nomenclature.<br>(1307) Which are the states that are interpretative?<br><i>Answer as in § 1306.<\/i><br>[1307a] All states are processes of interpretation."}, {"st": "3.16.13", "t": "(1308) Which are the states that are expressions?<br><i>Answer as in § 1306.<\/i><br>[1308a] All states are processes of expression.<br>In this connexion,<br>(1309) What is name?"}, {"st": "3.16.14", "t": "The four skandhas and uncompounded element.<br>(1310) What is form?<br>The four great phenomena and the form which is derived from them.<br>(1311) What is ignorance?"}, {"st": "3.16.15", "t": "<i>Ansiver as for ‘dulness’, § 390 (omitting ‘on that occasion’).<\/i><br>(1312) What is the craving for renewed existence?<br><i>Answer as for the ‘Fetter of the passion for renewed existence’, § 1120.<\/i><br>(1313) What is speculative opinion about renewed existence?"}, {"st": "3.16.16", "t": "Theories that both soul and world will be re-born, etc.<br>(1314) What is speculative opinion about existence not being renewed?<br>Theories that both soul and world will not be re-born, etc.<br>(1315) What is the sort of speculation known as Eternalism?"}, {"st": "3.16.17", "t": "That both soul and world are eternal, etc.<br>(1316) What is the sort of speculation known as Annihilation?<br>That both soul and world will be dissolved, etc."}, {"st": "3.16.18", "t": "(1317) What is the sort of speculation known as the Finite Theory... (1318) the Infinite Theory?"}, {"st": "3.16.19", "t": "That both soul and world are finite... infinite, etc."}, {"st": "3.16.20", "t": "(1319) What is the sort of speculation known as the Theory of Origins... (1320) the Theory of the Hereafter?"}, {"st": "3.16.21", "t": "Theories concerning the ultimate past... concerning futurity."}, {"st": "3.16.22", "t": "All this sort of opinion,<br>walking in opinion,<br>jungle of opinion,<br>wilderness of opinion,<br>puppet-show of opinion,<br>scuffling of opinion,<br>the Fetter of opinion,<br>the grip and tenacity of it,<br>the inclination towards it,<br>the being infected by it,<br>this by-path,<br>wrong road,<br>wrongness,<br>this ‘fordingplace’,<br>this shiftiness of grasp"}, {"st": "3.16.23", "t": "— this is what is called speculative opinion about renewed existence, and the rest."}, {"st": "3.16.24", "t": "(1321) What is unconscientiousness?... (1322) disregard of blame?<br><i>Answers as for the ninth and tenth ‘bases of corruption’, §§ 1238, 1239.<\/i><br>(1323) What is conscientiousness?"}, {"st": "3.16.25", "t": "The feeling of conscientious scruple when scruples ought to be felt, conscientious scruple at attaining to bad and evil states."}, {"st": "3.16.26", "t": "(1324) What is the fear of blame?"}, {"st": "3.16.27", "t": "The sense of guilt where a sense of guilt ought to be felt, a sense of guilt at attaining to bad and evil states."}, {"st": "3.16.28", "t": "(1825) What is contumacy?"}, {"st": "3.16.29", "t": "The being surly, refractious, contumacious when that which is in accordance with the Law has been said, contrariness, captiousness, want of regard, of consideration, of reverence, of deference."}, {"st": "3.16.30", "t": "(1326) What is friendship with evil?"}, {"st": "3.16.31", "t": "To follow after, to frequent the company of, and associate with, such persons as are unbelievers, immoral, uneducated, meanspirited and witless to resort to and consort with them, to be devoted to them, enthusiastic about them, and entangled with them."}, {"st": "3.16.32", "t": "(1327) What is suavity?"}, {"st": "3.16.33", "t": "The being gentle, tractable, amenable when that which is in accordance with the Law has been said, the refraining from contradiction and from captiousness; the showing regard and consideration, reverence and deference."}, {"st": "3.16.34", "t": "(1328) What is friendship with good?"}, {"st": "3.16.35", "t": "To follow after, frequent the company of, and associate with, such persons as are believers, virtuous, well educated, generous and intellectual; to resort to and consort with them, to be devoted to them, enthusiastic about them, mixed up with them."}, {"st": "3.16.36", "t": "[1329-1332] What is skill in"}, {"st": "3.16.37", "t": "(a) the Offences?<br>(b) restoration from the Offences?"}, {"st": "3.16.38", "t": "(c) the Attainments?<br>(d) recovery from the Attainments?"}, {"st": "3.16.39", "t": "That skill which is science, understanding, search, research, etc. when applied to"}, {"st": "3.16.40", "t": "(a) the Offences termed the Five Groups of Offences<br>and the Seven Groups of Offences;<br>(b) restoration from [the effects of] those Offences;"}, {"st": "3.16.41", "t": "(c) a case of Attainment where ‘conception works and thought discursive’,<br>a case of Attainment ‘wherein is no working of conception, but only of thought discursive’,<br>a case of Attainment ‘void of the working of conception and of thought discursive’;<br>(d) recovery from those Attainments."}, {"st": "3.16.42", "t": "(1333) What is proficiency in the Elements?"}, {"st": "3.16.43", "t": "That proficiency which is"}, {"st": "3.16.44", "t": "science,<br>understanding,<br>search,<br>research,<br>etc."}, {"st": "3.16.45", "t": "when applied to the eighteen elements, viz.:"}, {"st": "3.16.46", "t": "sight, visual object and visual cognition,<br>hearing, sound and auditory cognition,<br>smell, odour and olfactory cognition,<br>taste, sapid object and gustatory cognition,<br>bodysensibility, the tangible and tactile cognition,<br>mind, mental object and representative cognition."}, {"st": "3.16.47", "t": "(1334) What is proficiency in attention?"}, {"st": "3.16.48", "t": "That proficiency in attention which is science, understanding, etc. when applied to those elements."}, {"st": "3.16.49", "t": "(1335) What is skill in the spheres?"}, {"st": "3.16.50", "t": "That skill which is science, understanding, etc. when applied to the twelve spheres, namely, sight and visual form, hearing and sound, smell and odorous object, taste and sapid object, bodysensibility and the tangible, mind and mental object."}, {"st": "3.16.51", "t": "(1336) What is skill in the ‘Conditioned Geneses’?"}, {"st": "3.16.52", "t": "Science, understanding, etc. when applied to the formula:"}, {"st": "3.16.53", "t": "‘The syntheses come to pass because of ignorance;<br>cognition comes to pass because of syntheses;<br>name and form come to pass because of cognition;<br>the sixfold sphere comes to pass because of name and form;"}, {"st": "3.16.54", "t": "contact comes to pass because of the sixfold sphere,<br>feeling because of contact,<br>craving because of feeling,<br>grasping because of craving,<br>renewed existence because of grasping,<br>birth because of renewed existence,<br>old age and death, grief, lamentation, distress, melancholy and despair come to pass because of birth."}, {"st": "3.16.55", "t": "Such is the uprising of this whole mass of Ill’."}, {"st": "3.16.56", "t": "[1337, 1338] What is skill in affirming... in negating [causal conjuncture]?"}, {"st": "3.16.57", "t": "Science, understanding, etc. when applied to discerning that, in a given conjuncture, certain states are... are not, the cause and conditions of certain other states."}, {"st": "3.16.58", "t": "(1339) What is upright?<br>Uprightness, without deflexion, twist, or crookedness.<br>(1340) What is soft?<br>That which is plasticity, gentleness, smoothness, pliancy, lowliness of heart."}, {"st": "3.16.59", "t": "(1341) What is patience?"}, {"st": "3.16.60", "t": "That patience which is long-suffering, compliance, absence of rudeness and abruptness, complacency of heart."}, {"st": "3.16.61", "t": "(1342) What is temperance?"}, {"st": "3.16.62", "t": "That which is the absence of excess in deed, in word, and in deed and word together."}, {"st": "3.16.63", "t": "Besides, all moral self-restraint is temperance.<br>(1343) What is amity?<br>When all such speech as is"}, {"st": "3.16.64", "t": "insolent,<br>disagreeable,<br>grating,<br>harsh to others,<br>vituperative to others,<br>bordering upon anger,<br>not conducive to concentration,<br>is put away,"}, {"st": "3.16.65", "t": "and when all such speech as is"}, {"st": "3.16.66", "t": "innocuous,<br>pleasant to the ear,<br>affectionate,<br>such as goes to the heart,<br>is urbane,<br>sweet and acceptable to people generally"}, {"st": "3.16.67", "t": "— when speech of this sort is spoken — polished, friendly and gentle language — this is what is called amity."}, {"st": "3.16.68", "t": "(1344) What is courtesy?"}, {"st": "3.16.69", "t": "The two forms of courtesy: hospitality towards bodily needs and considerateness in matters of doctrine. When anyone shows courtesy it is in one or other of these two forms."}, {"st": "3.16.70", "t": "(1345) What is it to have the door of the faculties unguarded?"}, {"st": "3.16.71", "t": "When a certain individual sees an object with the eye he is entranced with the general appearance, or in the details of it. He does not set himself to restrain that which might give occasion for wicked states, covetous, dejected, to flow in over him, were he to dwell unrestrained as to the sense of sight. He keeps no watch over his faculty of sight, nor does he attain to mastery over it."}, {"st": "3.16.72", "t": "And so in like manner when he hears a sound with the ear... smells an odour with the nose... tastes a sapid with the tongue... feels a tangible with the body... recognises a mental object with the mind, he is entranced with the general appearance and in the details of it."}, {"st": "3.16.73", "t": "He does not set himself to restrain that which might give occasion for wicked states, covetous, dejected, to flow in over him, were he to dwell unrestrained as to the mental faculty."}, {"st": "3.16.74", "t": "He keeps no watch over the mental faculty, nor does he attain to mastery over it."}, {"st": "3.16.75", "t": "That these six faculties should be thus unguarded, untended, unwatched over, unrestrained, is what is called having the door of the faculties unguarded."}, {"st": "3.16.76", "t": "(1346) What is immoderation in diet?"}, {"st": "3.16.77", "t": "When anyone, through carelessness and without judgment, takes food for purposes of sport, sensual excess, personal charm and adornment, his insatiableness, immoderation, and want of judgment are what is called immoderation in diet."}, {"st": "3.16.78", "t": "(1347) What is it to have the doors of the faculties guarded?"}, {"st": "3.16.79", "t": "When a certain individual sees an object with the eye he is not entranced with the general appearance or the details of it."}, {"st": "3.16.80", "t": "He sets himself to restrain that which might give occasion for wicked states, covetous, dejected, to flow in over him, were he to dwell unrestrained as to the sense of sight."}, {"st": "3.16.81", "t": "He keeps watch over this faculty of sight, and attains to mastery over it. And so in like manner, when he hears a sound with the ear... smells an odour with the nose... tastes a sapid with the tongue... feels a tangible with the body... recognises an idea with the mind, he is not entranced with the general appearance and the details of it."}, {"st": "3.16.82", "t": "He sets himself to restrain that which might give occasion for wicked states, covetous, dejected, to flow in over him, were he to dwell unrestrained as to the mental faculty."}, {"st": "3.16.83", "t": "He keeps watch over the mental faculty, and attains to mastery over it."}, {"st": "3.16.84", "t": "That these six faculties should be thus guarded, tended, watched over, restrained, is what is called having the doors of the faculties guarded."}, {"st": "3.16.85", "t": "(1348) What is moderation in diet?"}, {"st": "3.16.86", "t": "When anyone takes food with reflection and judgment, not for purposes of sport, excess, personal charm and attractions, but so as to suffice for the sustenance and preservation of the body, for allaying the pangs [of hunger] and for aiding the practice of the higher life, and thinking the while,"}, {"st": "3.16.87", "t": "‘I shall subdue that which I have been feeling and shall cause no new feeling to arise, and maintenance shall be mine, blamelessness also and comfort’"}, {"st": "3.16.88", "t": "— this content, moderation, judgment in diet is what is called moderation in diet."}, {"st": "3.16.89", "t": "(1349) What is forgetfulness?"}, {"st": "3.16.90", "t": "Unmindfulness,<br>lapse of memory,<br>non-recollection,<br>nonremembrance,<br>not bearing in mind,<br>superficiality,<br>oblivion."}, {"st": "3.16.91", "t": "(1350) What is lack of intelligence?<br><i>Answer as for ‘ignorance’ or ‘dulness’, § 1311, etc.<\/i><br>(1351) What is mindfulness?<br><i>Answer as in § 14, omitting ‘on that occasion’.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.16.92", "t": "(1352) What is intelligence?<br><i>Answer as for ‘wisdom’ or ‘science’, § 16. And see § 53.<\/i><br>(1353) What is the power of computation?<br><i>Answer as for ‘wisdom’, § 16.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.16.93", "t": "(1354) What is the power of cultivation?<br>That which is the pursuing, the cultivating, the multiplying of good states."}, {"st": "3.16.94", "t": "Moreover, the seven factors in the Great Awakening are the power of cultivation."}, {"st": "3.16.95", "t": "(1355) What is composure?<br><i>Answer as for ‘quiet’, §§ 11, 54.<\/i><br>(1356) What is insight?<br><i>Answer as for ‘insight’ and ‘wisdom’, §§ 55, 16.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.16.96", "t": "(1357) What is ‘the mark of composure’?<br><i>Answer as for ‘quiet’, § 1357.<\/i><br>(1358) What is ‘the mark of grasp’?<br><i>Answer as for ‘grasp’ and ‘energy’, §§ 56, 13.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.16.97", "t": "(1359) What is grasp?<br><i>Answer as for ‘the mark of grasp’, § 1358.<\/i><br>(1360) What is balance?<br><i>Answer as for ‘balance’, § 57.<\/i>"}, {"st": "3.16.98", "t": "(1361) What is moral failure?"}, {"st": "3.16.99", "t": "Excess in deed, excess in word, excess in both together. Moreover, all immorality is moral failure."}, {"st": "3.16.100", "t": "(1362) What is theoretic fallacy?"}, {"st": "3.16.101", "t": "‘There is no such thing as alms, or sacrifice, or offering; there is neither fruit, nor result of good or evil deeds;<br>there is no such thing as this world or the next;<br>there is no such thing as mother, or father, or beings springing into birth without them;<br>there are in the world no recluses or brahmins who have reached the highest point, who have attained the height, who, having understood and realized by themselves alone both this world and the next, make known the same’"}, {"st": "3.16.102", "t": "— all this sort of speculation... this is what is called theoretic fallacy. Moreover, all wrong views are theoretic fallacies."}, {"st": "3.16.103", "t": "(1363) What is moral progress?<br>Absence of excess in deed, in word, and in deed and word together’.<br>(1364) What is progress in theory?"}, {"st": "3.16.104", "t": "‘There is such a thing as alms, sacrifice, and offering;<br>... fruit, and the result of good and evil deeds;<br>... this world and the next;<br>mother, father and beings springing into birth without them;<br>... recluses and brahmins who have reached the highest, who have attained the height, who having understood and realized by themselves alone both this world and the other world, make known the same’"}, {"st": "3.16.105", "t": "— all this sort of"}, {"st": "3.16.106", "t": "science,<br>understanding,<br>etc...."}, {"st": "3.16.107", "t": "this is what is called progress in theory. Moreover, all right views are progress in theory."}, {"st": "3.16.108", "t": "(1365) What is purity in morals?<br>Absence of excess in deed, in word, and in deed and word together.<br>(1366) What is purity in theory?"}, {"st": "3.16.109", "t": "Knowledge of the specific nature of Karma; knowledge of the Truths in their due order; the knowledge of him who holds the Path; the knowledge of him who holds the Fruit of the Path."}, {"st": "3.16.110", "t": "(i.) The phrase ‘Now purity of theory’ is equivalent to that science, understanding... right theory (views) [described above, § 16]."}, {"st": "3.16.111", "t": "(ii) In the phrase ‘And as the struggle of him who holds certain views’, ‘struggle’ means that inception of energy etc. [described above, § 13]."}, {"st": "3.16.112", "t": "(iii.) The phrase ‘agitation’ implies dread of birth, dread of old age, dread of sickness, dread of death."}, {"st": "3.16.113", "t": "(iv.) The phrase ‘occasion of agitation’ means birth, old age, sickness, death."}, {"st": "3.16.114", "t": "(v.) The phrase ‘And the earnest struggle of him who is agitated’ refers to [the four Eight Struggles]:"}, {"st": "3.16.115", "t": "— When a bhikkhu brings forth the desire"}, {"st": "3.16.116", "t": "(a) that bad and wicked states which have not arisen should not arise,<br>(b) that bad and wicked states which have arisen should be put away,<br>(c) that good states which have not arisen should arise,<br>(d) that good states which have arisen should stand firm, should not get confused, should be frequently practised, made to abound, cultivated, and perfected"}, {"st": "3.16.117", "t": "— then he uses endeavour, sets energy a-going, reaches forward in thought and struggles."}, {"st": "3.16.118", "t": "(vi.) The phrase ‘And discontent in good states’ means the longing for higher achievement in one who is dissatisfied over the cultivation of good states."}, {"st": "3.16.119", "t": "(vii.) The phrase ‘And the not shrinking back in the struggle’ means the thorough and persevering and unresting performance, the absence of stagnation, the unfaltering volition, the unflinching endurance, the assiduous pursuit, exercise and repetition which attend the cultivation of good states."}, {"st": "3.16.120", "t": "(viii.) The phrase ‘Wisdom’ means the threefold wisdom, namely"}, {"st": "3.16.121", "t": "(a) reminiscent knowledge of one’s former births,<br>(b) knowledge of the relapse and renascence of beings,<br>(c) the knowledge that makes an end of the Intoxicants."}, {"st": "3.16.122", "t": "(ix.) The phrase ‘Emancipation’ means the twofold emancipation, namely,"}, {"st": "3.16.123", "t": "(a) detachment of thought, and<br>(b) Nirvana."}, {"st": "3.16.124", "t": "(x.) The phrase ‘knowledge in making an end’ means the knowledge he has who holds the Path."}, {"st": "3.16.125", "t": "(xi.) The phrase ‘knowledge in origins’ means the knowledge he has who holds the Fruit of the Path."}, {"st": "3.16.126", "t": "End of the Division entitled ‘Elimination’."}],[])})();
